{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"cat .claude/skills/prompt-audit/SKILL.md; sed -n '86,201p' docs/prd/10-filing-phase.md; tail -3 docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/log.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"cat .claude/skills/prompt-audit/SKILL.md; sed -n '86,201p' docs/prd/10-filing-phase.md; tail -3 docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/log.md\"","aggregated_output":"---\nname: prompt-audit\ndescription: After every measurement of an extraction configuration, and before any prompt change, map each failure class in the stored draws to the sentence of the request that produced it, check that sentence against the frozen standard and the prompt principles, and write the audit table. Alex, 2026-11-02 - \"你应该看着prompt，然后对比失败的trace，然后仔细的根据尺子做对比，并且我们prompt的principle也要带入，是尝试教他general hedge fund analyst skill，不是要hardcode或是benchmark farming；你把这个写成一个skill以后固定跑\".\n---\n\n# Prompt audit: failures → the request's sentences → the standard → the principle\n\nRun this after every measured configuration (three draws scored on the frozen ruler) and before any edit to a prompt file, a schema\ndescription or a takes line. A measurement without this audit is a score without a diagnosis (AGENTS §7.2, §6). The audit is read,\nnot scripted: the scripts below only produce the lists to read.\n\n## 0. Inputs\n\n- The arm's run folders (`docs/research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/arms/<arm>/d1..3`) and the baseline's.\n- The frozen ruler (AGENTS §7.1): the extraction standard `docs/prd/05-extraction-standard.html` (version 3, D243, with D250's\n  boundaries), golden-v5, the questions standard, the judgement gold. Never edited; every disagreement is a gap in the system.\n- The prompt principles (AGENTS §6 and §8): the prompt carries the standard and no new rule; it teaches a general hedge-fund\n  analyst, so every sentence and example reads the same for a bank, a drug maker, a retailer or a chip maker, names no covered\n  company, product or number, and takes no example from a test article; a classification is a closed set with examples; never grow a\n  prompt to fix quality without cutting as much elsewhere; a field's definition sits in the schema beside the field; code owns\n  identity, never importance.\n\n## 1. Dump the request as the model reads it\n\nOne stored request of the configuration (any article), printed whole: the system text (the role), every user block before the item\n(category block, subjects, research reference, open questions with their takes lines, research groups), the closing sentence, the\nend-of-request check, and every schema field description (evidence and candidates). Read it top to bottom once. List every\ninstruction that decides keep, reject, link, open, or a field value, with its location (role paragraph, check row, schema field,\ntakes line, category block). This list is the thing the failures are mapped onto.\n\n```sh\npython3 - <<'PY'\nimport sqlite3, json\na = sqlite3.connect('file:<run folder>/archive.sqlite?mode=ro', uri=True)\nreq = json.loads(a.execute(\"select request_json from model_calls where purpose='extract' and request_json like '%<title fragment>%' order by call_id desc limit 1\").fetchone()[0])\nprint(req['messages'][0]['content']); print(req['messages'][1]['content'].split('[Item]')[0]); print(req['messages'][1]['content'].rsplit('\\n\\n', 2)[-2:])\nprint(json.dumps({k: v.get('description') for k, v in req['response_format']['json_schema']['schema']['properties']['evidence']['items']['properties'].items() if v.get('description')}, ensure_ascii=False, indent=1))\nPY\n```\n\n## 2. Produce the failure lists (all at no model cost, on the stored draws)\n\n| List | Script | What it gives |\n|---|---|---|\n| Matter states across draws | `harness/matter_states.py arm=d1,d2,d3 base=d1,d2,d3` | every gold core/supporting matter: extracted or reaching in 3/2/1/0 draws; the systematic misses (0 of 3) and the coin flips (1 of 3) |\n| Written noise by the standard's row | the `rows_all` count in `design-v72.md` §10 (the gold's `not_kept[].row`) | which row of the standard the written noise falls under, with examples |\n| No-home drops | `harness/dropped_census.py d1 d2 d3` | gold matters written but dropped, with the questions open at the time |\n| Where links go | `harness/links_by_question.py d1 d2 d3 --sample 20` | the pile per question, the revenue-sink share, statements under several questions, noise under revenue |\n| Quote refusals | the `dropped` reasons of the handovers (`quote not in item`), each diffed against its closest source window | paraphrase against a word or two off |\n| Question recall | `harness/question_recall.py d1 d2 d3` | must_open / may_open covered by seeds and by article-opened questions |\n| Fields | 15 random stored statements with speaker, original_who, original_day, tier | read against the quote and the item's day and publisher |\n\nFor every list, read the items, not the counts: the raw answer beside the article for every systematic miss (a miss the scorer reports\nmay be the matter written in other words: check before counting it), a sample of twenty for every pile.\n\n## 3. Map each failure class to the request\n\nFor each class with more than a handful of cases, fill one row:\n\n| Failure class (count, share) | The sentence(s) of the request that produced it, quoted | The standard's line on the same case (section, decision) | Verdict | The model's own account (when the sentence is not obvious) |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n\nVerdicts, one of: **contradiction** (two sentences of the request pull opposite ways, or a request sentence removes what the standard\nkeeps, D240), **missing** (no sentence decides the case; the model guesses, a coin flip per send), **abstract** (a clause without an\nexample, read differently each send), **misread** (the sentence is right, the model does not apply it; needs the model's own account\nbefore any change), **not the prompt** (code, scorer or sampler).\n\nThe model's own account: continue the stored conversation with one question about one case (`harness/probe_v72_link.py`,\n`probe_v72_missing.py`), a cent a case, for every \"misread\" and for any class whose sentence is not obvious from reading.\n\n## 4. Check every proposed fix against the principles before writing it\n\n- It is written from the standard's line, not from the failing article: the example comes from another domain, names no covered\n  company, product or number, and reads the same for every industry. A fix that only recovers the bench's misses is benchmark\n  farming and is not written.\n- It removes a contradiction, supplies a missing definition at its owner (schema field, takes line, check row, category block), or\n  replaces an abstract clause with a closed set and examples. It never adds a rule the standard does not hold.\n- It cuts at least as much as it adds, or the growth has a written reason; report characters before and after.\n- One class per change; the change is proved on the stored requests of that class, three sends as stored against three changed,\n  with the noise guards of the same articles; the whole bench accepts it (three draws) only when Alex asks for a run.\n\n## 5. Output\n\nWrite the table and the verdicts into the arm's design document (a numbered section), one row per class, in the order of the loss;\nthe report to Alex states each class in a reader's words (what the system does wrong, which sentence makes it do that, what the\nstandard says), never in the loop's own words (AGENTS, \"Authority and scope\": explain as to a sixteen-year-old).\n  of the previous filing topic).\n- **Prompt principles** (AGENTS §6): carry only the standard and add no new rule; a general hedge-fund analyst: every rule and example reads the same\n  for a bank, a drug maker, a retailer or a chip company, names neither NVIDIA nor its products or figures, and is not taken from a test article; a\n  classification is written as a closed set with two or three examples per class, replacing the abstract wording; quality is not fixed by lengthening\n  the prompt: remove as much as is added, or write down the reason.\n- **Code decides only \"is it the same thing\", never \"does it matter\"** (AGENTS §2).\n\n## 5. What to do\n\nStatus: the links-rule version that carried items 1–3 (v74l) failed its one test (§8 step 5); D361 keeps the fallback sentence, so item 3 is not carried out. The plan below is kept as written.\n\n1. **Replace the abstract sentence \"makes the answer more or less likely through demand, supply, competition or pricing\" with the few classes in which an analyst recognises an indirect relation, written as a closed set, each class with two or three examples from other industries** (AGENTS §6: one\n   abstract sentence is a coin flip on every send; examples replace it). A first draft of the classes (checked against the standard line by line at\n   design time; it holds only meanings the standard already has):\n   - The question's own figure: this question's figures, guidance, estimates and revisions (estimates for another period or another population follow\n     the existing rule).\n   - The other side of the same money: a seller's revenue from the buyers the question counts; those buyers' orders and backlog with these sellers.\n   - Price and volume: a product's price or shipments, for the question on that product's revenue.\n   - Variants and parts: a product's variants, alternatives, key parts and schedule changes, for the question on that product's launch.\n   - Demand, supply and competition moves with a number: customers' orders and capital-spending plans, suppliers' capacity and prices, competitors'\n     sales and share, for the question they affect.\n2. **State how to file a fact when two specific questions both bear on it** (the problem the `definition_no_fallback` arm exposed: Morgan Stanley's rack\n   price was filed under the buyers' capital-spending question, and the seller's Rubin revenue question lost it). One home per fact (D207): file it under\n   the question its figure measures most directly (for example, a hotel's room rate goes under its room-revenue question, not under the\n   travellers'-spending question; a parts maker's revenue from carmakers goes under the carmakers' parts-spending question, not under a rival parts\n   maker's share question); when one sentence gives a separate figure for each of two questions, write two items, each filed under its own question\n   (code stores two items that quote the same sentence as one record linked to both questions); never also file it under a broader question about the\n   same company (the failure of D355's test 2: the model filed it under an NVIDIA revenue question as well). The original draft's \"when the other\n   party's question is open … file it there as well\" conflicted with D207 and was deleted on 2026-11-04 with Alex's agreement (D360).\n3. **Delete the fallback sentence** and return to the standard's §6: when no question takes a fact, it is not filed; code drops it as \"no home\" and\n   records it (D209), and evaluation reads each one.\n4. Change only the one place that holds the filing rule; report its length in characters before and after, and write down the reason for any growth\n   (this time's reason: examples replace one abstract sentence).\n\n## 6. How to measure (the ruler does not move; the method does not change again)\n\n- **Requests**: rebuild each stored request byte for byte with the production code, changing only the one place that holds the filing rule; the\n  rebuilt request's archive id must equal the original's, or it is not sent (`resend_filing.py`, AGENTS §5). Never re-send the archive's copy whose\n  keys were sorted alphabetically.\n- **Articles**: development set = the 54 articles of `selection.json` (already read many times); held-out set = articles the extraction gold read and\n  the judgement gold uses that are not among those 54, drawn once before the test, with the list frozen and never changed afterwards. Both sets are\n  measured and reported apart.\n- **Control**: the unchanged requests re-sent 3 times the same day. No comparison with the archive (model drift).\n- **Each answer**: goes through the production code checks and filing first (including \"keep only the narrowest\" and \"no home means dropped\"), and is\n  counted after.\n- **What is counted** (`measure_filing.py`, with its matching rule fixed: items are matched on the distinct numbers and the proper nouns other than\n  NVIDIA in the source text; a source passage the system quotes that overlaps the gold's passage also counts as the same matter; \"optional\" counts as\n  optional; the citations in the body of the gold's answers are read):\n  - whether each fact that matters (one the gold uses to judge any question) reached the question that uses it, counted per fact, not per \"fact ×\n    question\";\n  - per question: the facts that matter that reached it and the new noise that appeared, counted by the decision rule below;\n  - the parking lot: how many facts the total-revenue question (v1, together with v22) takes in, and what they are;\n  - noise: how many of the sentences the standard does not keep are kept; per question, how noise is filed (links on facts the standard does not keep\n    or no question uses);\n  - how many core and supporting facts appear; the facts that matter that are dropped; how many questions each fact is filed under on average.\n  - Matching still has a small error (spot check: the new rule mismatches a few and misses a few); say so when reporting the numbers, and read the\n    ones that matter one by one.\n- **Decision rule** (rewritten on 2026-11-04 with Alex's agreement, D360. The original draft required \"every draw above the highest of the control's\n  three\" and the like, which the control itself fails when measured again, so it cannot tell good from bad. Script `decide.py`; written down and frozen\n  before each test; each draw's numbers and the totals are all reported):\n  - **Totals, with luck ruled out first**: each article's 6 answers from the new and the old arm are randomly reassigned between the two arms 4,000\n    times (with a fixed random seed); the new rule's difference counts as a real change only when fewer than 5% of the reassignments reach it.\n    - Development set: facts that matter reaching the question that uses them must rise; the count under the total-revenue questions (v1, v22) must\n      fall; sentences the standard does not keep must not rise; core facts must not fall.\n    - Held-out set: facts that matter reaching the question that uses them must not fall; sentences the standard does not keep must not rise; core\n      facts must not fall.\n  - **Per question, counted by code against the gold** (development and held-out sets together):\n    - A fact that matters which reaches the question at least two fewer times under the new rule than under the control counts as \"one fewer\" for that\n      question; in under the control all three times and under the new rule none of the three, it counts as \"lost outright\". A move to another question\n      that the gold also uses it for and that is no broader than the original (one home per fact, D207) does not count as fewer; a move to a broader\n      question counts. \"One more\" in the opposite direction offsets it.\n    - A noise sentence (not kept by the standard, or kept but used by no question) that the new rule files under the question at least twice while the\n      control never does counts as \"new noise\"; filed all three times, it counts as \"outright new noise\". The opposite direction offsets it.\n    - Any question with a net 3 fewer, a net 2 lost outright, a net 5 more new noise, or a net 3 more outright new noise fails. These thresholds are the\n      smallest values that keep \"misjudged when new and old are exactly the same\" within about one in ten; at the test, first compute the\n      misjudgement rate from the reassignments; if it is above one in ten, raise the threshold most prone to misjudgement by one step, then compare with\n      what was measured.\n  - **Smaller changes** (a question 2 fewer, two or three more noise) cannot be told from luck in three measurements: all are listed, given to a reader\n    who did not write the rule to read against the raw answers, and written into the report; they do not decide pass or fail.\n  Only when everything passes does it become a candidate; a candidate runs end to end three times on the 14-day window (extraction re-sent, matching and\n  judging run as usual, scored on the frozen judgement ruler), and that step needs Alex's word.\n\n## 7. Process (no drift)\n\n- **One recommendation and one test at a time** (AGENTS §7.3, Alex: \"就测试一种，一次\"): write the design first and put it through rounds of review (own\n  checks, Codex read-only, a reader who did not write it reading the raw records) until a round finds nothing serious; write down and freeze the\n  predictions and stop conditions; test once; if it does not match, stop and take it to Alex (D355: \"如果有什么不符合hypothesis 的地方你需要终止然后和我说我们重新评估\").\n- **A review's report is a lead**: each finding counts only once it is checked against the records (AGENTS §1).\n- **A prompt audit before any prompt change** (`.claude/skills/prompt-audit/SKILL.md`): check the whole request against the standard sentence by\n  sentence, in both directions.\n- **Every step goes into `log.md`**: what was done, what came of it, the files; mistakes and overturned results stay in it too.\n- **Do not repeat what was ruled out** (§3).\n\n## 8. Steps and where we are\n\n| # | Step | Output | Status |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Design: the classes of indirect relation (a closed set + examples from other industries), how to file when a fact measures both sides, deleting the fallback; checked against the standard sentence by sentence; length before and after | `docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/design-links.md` | Done (draft 3, v74l / poc-v115) |\n| 2 | Checks at no cost: a sample answer written by the new rule goes through every code check; the new rule's text goes through the prompt audit | Same as above | Done |\n| 3 | Review rounds (Codex, independent readers, own), until nothing serious remains | `review-links-*.md` | Done (Codex 7 rounds, independent readers 3 times; round 7 found nothing serious) |\n| 4 | Draw and freeze the held-out set; write down and freeze this test's predictions and decision rule | `heldout.json`, `predictions-links.md` | Done (sha256 recorded for both) |\n| 5 | Test once: development set + held-out set, the new rule and the same-day control 3 times each | `answers-links.json`, `log.md` | Done (2026-11-04): failed: facts that matter reaching the right question, development set +7 (p 0.26); every other condition passed |\n| 6 | If passed: to Alex, to decide whether to run three 14-day end-to-end draws; if failed: stop, to Alex | `design-links.md` §8 | Stopped, to Alex |\n| 7 | (D361, Alex: \"按照你的提以来，但是你一定是自己反思多轮之后才给出的行动\") Measure the ceiling first: hand the source passages the gold cites straight to the frozen judgement; the custom-chip, operating-margin and capital-spending questions judged 3 times each at the window end, the custom-chip question as it was 3 times as the control; the fallback sentence is not deleted for now | `reflection-v74l.md`, `oracle-design.md` | Done: operating margin grounded 3/3, capital spending 2/3, custom chips 0/3 (the gap is in interpretation); the next step waits for Alex |\n| 8 | (D362, Alex: \"你继续啊不要scope drift and strongly follow evidence of your discovery and learning\") Two small changes where facts are not delivered, one at a time: ① when a quote is rewritten, code matches it back to the source sentence (changing nothing the model sees; verified by replaying the archive); ② a figure question's description states which figures it is derived from by definition (first sent 3 times each on the article that motivated it) | `delivery-fixes.md` | Stopped: an independent check overturned the first change's code (it would take in wrong figures); the second failed its trial send (1/3). The root: the signed standard's §5.3 does not keep two key facts, treating them as \"a restatement of known facts\" (proposal 6, awaiting approval); to Alex |\n| 9 | **Historical; superseded by D370.** (D363, Alex: \"乙\") At coverage start, record each question's starting figure once from the company's latest earnings report; the daily path still takes no restatements: design, rounds of self-checks and independent checks, validated first on the archive in the simplest way | `baseline-design.md` | Validated on two questions: the frozen extraction does not read the report's tables (0/3); with each question reading the report once on its own, the operating-margin question read its starting figure 2/3 and the capital-spending question 3/3, and the judgement then judged right (2/3, 3/3), about 2.5 cents. All 22 questions tested once (2026-11-05, Alex: \"你充分验证\"; files chosen by code, a same-day control, about $0.43): v10 and v12 judged right 9/9 each; no question already judged right turned wrong; but 5 of the 53 figures read were other quantities (the Vera CPU question and the Blackwell and Rubin question took a larger or neighbouring revenue line), so it failed condition 3, written beforehand. The model itself pointed to the reading instructions' sentence \"write the lines it is computed from, or the line the report uses for it\"; stopped, to Alex. Alex: \"改\"; only this sentence was changed (\"write only the lines it is computed from, or the same quantity under another name; never a broader line or a line next to it\"), tried on 4 questions first, then tested on all 22 (about $0.36): all three conditions passed: v10 and v12 9/9 each, no question judged right turned wrong, and all 46 figures were the questions' own quantities. Checked after an independent reader's challenge: the new sentence made the reading miss the capital-spending line in 3 of 12 reads, and it had been tested only on the one May report; the fourth version (a line-by-line re-check after writing) removed too much and failed; after a census of every failure trace (Alex: \"我需要你深度看完所有失败trace给我准信\"), the third version with the repaired quote check was chosen (D365): 0 wrong figures on the held-out August report; built into the production system (poc-v116; the $0 checks and the tests all pass). The full acceptance, three 14-day draws (Alex: \"跑验收\"; a same-day control; about $4.79): all passed: operating margin and capital spending judged right 3/3 each (the control never judged them), 53 against 45 in total (of 57); for the China-share question, the only one two fewer, both readers found the starting figures did not lead it astray. Alex: \"都做\": poc-v116 became the default (D366); the code was committed and a PR opened |\n\n## 9. Alex's own words on this matter (in time order)\n\n- \"你这个单独开一个专题出来解决 … 是promot，是系统结构设计还是到底哪里有问题挂错一堆东西，机制是不是有问题（一定要挂吗？有没有可能是杂音\" (2026-11-03)\n- \"So if it's a customer problem or other companies that you think is relating NVIDIA and it's the wrong evidence, then what is the standard for it? … from a hedge fund analyst standpoint, are these even relevant? Do you think your ruler is wrong in this aspect?\" (2026-11-04)\n- \"你确定下你接下来要改进的量化目标，以及你要看到端到端我们的管线是要做什么的，不要乱硬优化，要看到目标\" (2026-11-04)\n- \"就测试一种，一次 … 能不能自己有个超级自信的定论和证据再给我提建议\" (2026-11-04)\n- \"这些只是类型题，你需要抽象拔高我们现在的核心问题和卡点。这个很重要这样改下去才是对的\" (2026-11-04)\n- \"你把这个写好成下一阶段核心PRD然后不要drift, 把所有需要的context 全部充分记录，每次开始前都需要revisit, 然后开始做\" (2026-11-04)\n- \"go\" (2026-11-04, on the proposed amendments to §5.2 and §6 and on this test)\n| 39 | 11-05 | 第 3 步：做进正式系统（设计四张表，Codex 审 3 轮：第 1 轮 4 条严重、第 2 轮 2 条严重，都改；第 3 轮无严重问题） | 建成 `baseline.run` + poc-v116 + baseline-v3 提示词文件；$0 核对：44 份请求和验证时逐字节相同，3 次存下的回答走正式代码写出的记录完全一样，第一天判断正好判这些题，网页从开始跟踪那天可见；新测试 12 条、6 个弄坏版本都抓出、全套 272 条通过；设计页和 PRD 00/03/04/05/06 更新，`docs/check.py` 通过 | 交 Alex：要不要跑 14 天三遍完整验收（约 5 美元） |\n| 40 | 11-05 | Alex：\"跑验收\"。14 天三遍完整验收：poc-v116（开始跟踪时读财报）三遍、当天重跑 poc-v108 三遍作对照，冻结尺子打分（事先锁定 `acceptance-design.md`）；电脑中途在电池上睡了约 5 小时，6 遍被暂停，插电后自己接着跑完 | **全过**：v10、v12 各 3/3（对照从没判过）；合计 53 对 45（满分 57，+8）；唯一少两次的 v17（0/3 对 2/3）两人都认定不是起点数：同一份抽取请求，新配置两遍碰巧留下\"中国份额归零\"那句，对照没留；约 4.79 美元 | 交 Alex：poc-v116 是否成为默认 |\n| 41 | 11-05 | Alex：\"都做\"（poc-v116 设成默认；提交代码、开 PR）。默认配置改成 poc-v116，默认的条目数据库换成带财报网页原文的那一份（旧的没有原文，新默认会在它上面拒跑）；测试按用户的用法补上：不给种子题就拒绝，给了就先读财报再跑第一天 | 测试全过，5 个弄坏的版本都抓出；配置的设置和验收三遍记下的校验码一致（只改了配置里的注释）；花钱前的检查脚本在新配置的运行数据上照常跑完，但不复查读财报那一步的引用检查（记为未补的缺口）；D366 | 提交、开 PR；下一类问题等 Alex |\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'd=docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers; nl -ba $d/blind5.py; nl -ba $d/blind_rules.py; nl -ba $d/blind_adjudicate.py'","aggregated_output":"     1\t\"\"\"The D749 $0 writer test (SMALL-DESIGN.md §5 [D749]): cases S, E and P-v8 built from the repaired request (judging-v13s.txt, the\n     2\t\"decide the side before you size it\" sentence), a fresh Claude and Codex writer each, readers under the D747 definitions, and two\n     3\treader controls carried from the targeted test with the request they were written for: U05 (E Codex, an invented 10% cut; expected\n     4\tnot supported) and U02 (S Claude, a floor above its own total; expected the part-above-whole defect upheld).\n     5\t\n     6\t  python3 blind5.py build <out>      # the three requests and writer prompts (refuses an existing folder)\n     7\t  python3 blind5.py check <out>      # every first answer through the schema, from_answer, check and publish_decision\n     8\t  python3 blind5.py packets <out>    # 6 replies + 2 controls, shuffled X01–X08; key beside\n     9\t  python3 blind5.py tally <out>      # the registered lines, after readings, recorded reasons and any adjudication\n    10\t\"\"\"\n    11\timport hashlib\n    12\timport json\n    13\timport random\n    14\timport sys\n    15\tfrom pathlib import Path\n    16\t\n    17\tHERE = Path(__file__).resolve().parent\n    18\tsys.path.insert(0, str(HERE))\n    19\timport blind4 as b4  # noqa: E402\n    20\tfrom blind_rules import format_material  # noqa: E402\n    21\t\n    22\tCASES = b4.CASES                                                     # ('S', 'E', 'P-v8')\n    23\tBLIND4 = HERE.parents[2] / 'poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23'\n    24\tCONTROLS = {'K-invented-cut': ('E', 'codex'), 'K-impossible-floor': ('S', 'claude')}     # blind4 U05 and U02, unchanged\n    25\tFLOOR_DEFECT = ('part above or added into its whole', ('F11', 'F12', 'F15'))\n    26\tREADER_BRIEF = b4.REREAD_BRIEF.replace('re-read under the comparison ruling, D747', 'writer test, D749')\n    27\tREPAIR = 'decide which side the evidence favours before you size it'\n    28\tif 'D749' not in READER_BRIEF:\n    29\t    raise SystemExit('the D749 reader brief is not built')\n    30\t\n    31\t\n    32\tdef build(out):\n    33\t    if REPAIR not in (HERE / 'judging-v13s.txt').read_text():\n    34\t        raise SystemExit('judging-v13s.txt does not hold the D749 repair')\n    35\t    b4.build(out)\n    36\t    for name in CASES:\n    37\t        if REPAIR not in (out / name / 'system.txt').read_text():\n    38\t            raise SystemExit(f'{name}: the built system text does not hold the D749 repair')\n    39\t\n    40\t\n    41\tdef check(out):\n    42\t    b4.check(out)\n    43\t\n    44\t\n    45\tdef packets(out):\n    46\t    rows = json.loads((out / 'CHECKED.json').read_text())\n    47\t    items = [{'label': f'fresh {r[\"case\"]}/{r[\"writer\"]}', 'case': r['case'], 'writer': r['writer'],\n    48\t              'answer': str(out / r['case'] / 'answers' / f'{r[\"writer\"]}.json'), 'case_dir': str(out / r['case'])} for r in rows]\n    49\t    for name, (case, writer) in CONTROLS.items():\n    50\t        items.append({'label': name, 'case': case, 'writer': name, 'answer': str(BLIND4 / case / 'answers' / f'{writer}.json'),\n    51\t                      'case_dir': str(BLIND4 / case)})\n    52\t    order = list(range(len(items)))\n    53\t    random.Random(hashlib.sha256(b'D749 ' + (out / 'CHECKED.json').read_bytes()).hexdigest()).shuffle(order)\n    54\t    folder = out / 'reader-packets'\n    55\t    folder.mkdir(exist_ok=False)\n    56\t    key = {}\n    57\t    for n, i in enumerate(order, 1):\n    58\t        item = items[i]\n    59\t        case_dir = Path(item['case_dir'])\n    60\t        pid = f'X{n:02d}'\n    61\t        key[pid] = item\n    62\t        (folder / f'{pid}.md').write_text('\\n'.join([READER_BRIEF, format_material(case_dir), '', '# The request as the writer saw it', '',\n    63\t                                                     (case_dir / 'user.txt').read_text(), '', '# The answer', '',\n    64\t                                                     b4._display(item['answer']), '']))\n    65\t        (folder / f'{pid}.codex-prompt.txt').write_text(\n    66\t            f'Read the file {folder / f\"{pid}.md\"} and nothing else (no other file, no web). Follow the brief at its top: read the request '\n    67\t            'and the answer it contains, and return only the JSON the brief asks for, with no text before or after it.\\n')\n    68\t    (out / 'reader-key.json').write_text(json.dumps(key, indent=1))\n    69\t    print(f'{len(order)} packets in {folder}; key in reader-key.json (keep from readers)')\n    70\t\n    71\t\n    72\tdef tally(out):\n    73\t    \"\"\"[D749] Pass: every fresh S and E reply has a settled yes on its forecast or its unresolved reason; both fresh P-v8 replies a\n    74\t    settled yes on a forecast; every fresh reply accepted and publishable; no settled defect on a fresh reply; K-invented-cut settled\n    75\t    not supported; K-impossible-floor's floor defect settled (both readers mark it, or both adjudicators uphold it). A settled vote is\n    76\t    final only after Claude records its two reasons as compatible (`reasons.json`). A support split left after adjudication is\n    77\t    inconclusive.\"\"\"\n    78\t    key = json.loads((out / 'reader-key.json').read_text())\n    79\t    expected_labels = {f'fresh {c}/{w}' for c in CASES for w in ('claude', 'codex')} | set(CONTROLS)\n    80\t    if {i['label'] for i in key.values()} != expected_labels or len(key) != len(expected_labels):\n    81\t        raise SystemExit(f'the key does not hold exactly the registered answers: {sorted(i[\"label\"] for i in key.values())}')\n    82\t    checked = {(r['case'], r['writer']): r for r in json.loads((out / 'CHECKED.json').read_text())}\n    83\t    reasons_path = out / 'reasons.json'\n    84\t    reasons = json.loads(reasons_path.read_text()) if reasons_path.exists() else {}\n    85\t    lines, need, verdict, settled_defects, split_defects = [], [], 'pass', [], []\n    86\t    for pid, item in sorted(key.items()):\n    87\t        first = [b4._reading(out / 'reader-packets' / f'{pid}.{w}.json') for w in ('codex', 'claude')]\n    88\t        adjudicated = [out / 'adjudication' / f'{pid}.{w}.json' for w in ('codex', 'claude')]\n    89\t        adj = [b4._reading(p) for p in adjudicated] if all(p.exists() for p in adjudicated) else None\n    90\t        answer = json.loads(b4._display(item['answer']))\n    91\t        record = reasons.get(pid, {})\n    92\t        marks = [{b4._defect_key(d) for d in r.get('defects', [])} for r in first]\n    93\t        for d in marks[0] & marks[1]:\n    94\t            settled_defects.append([pid, item['label'], d[0], list(d[1]), 'both readers'])\n    95\t        either = (marks[0] | marks[1]) - (marks[0] & marks[1])\n    96\t        if either and adj is None:\n    97\t            need.append(pid)\n    98\t        elif either:\n    99\t            decisions = [{b4._defect_key(d): d.get('decision') for d in x.get('defects', [])} for x in adj]\n   100\t            for d in sorted(either, key=str):\n   101\t                pair = [decisions[0].get(d), decisions[1].get(d)]\n   102\t                if pair == ['uphold', 'uphold']:\n   103\t                    settled_defects.append([pid, item['label'], d[0], list(d[1]), pair])\n   104\t                elif pair != ['reject', 'reject']:\n   105\t                    split_defects.append([pid, item['label'], d[0], list(d[1]), pair])\n   106\t        for a in answer['answers']:\n   107\t            view = a['view_id']\n   108\t            votes = [r['members'].get(view, {}).get('supported') for r in first]\n   109\t            if any(v not in ('yes', 'no') for v in votes):\n   110\t                raise SystemExit(f'{pid} {view}: a first reading has no valid vote: {votes}')\n   111\t            if votes[0] == votes[1] and record.get('first') == 'compatible':\n   112\t                value = votes[0]\n   113\t            elif votes[0] == votes[1] and 'first' not in record:\n   114\t                need.append(f'{pid} (reasons not yet recorded)')\n   115\t                continue\n   116\t            elif adj is None:\n   117\t                need.append(pid)\n   118\t                continue\n   119\t            else:\n   120\t                second = [x['members'].get(view, {}).get('supported') for x in adj]\n   121\t                votes += second\n   122\t                if second[0] != second[1]:\n   123\t                    value = 'split'\n   124\t                elif 'adjudicated' not in record:\n   125\t                    need.append(f'{pid} (adjudicated reasons not yet recorded)')\n   126\t                    continue\n   127\t                else:\n   128\t                    value = second[0] if record['adjudicated'] == 'compatible' else 'split'\n   129\t            if item['label'] == 'K-invented-cut':\n   130\t                ok, rule = value == 'no', 'control expects not supported'\n   131\t            elif item['label'] == 'K-impossible-floor':\n   132\t                ok, rule = True, 'support not scored; the floor defect is'\n   133\t            elif item['case'] == 'P-v8':\n   134\t                ok, rule = a['shape'] == 'forecast' and value == 'yes', 'a supported forecast'\n   135\t            else:\n   136\t                ok, rule = value == 'yes', 'a supported forecast or a justified unresolved answer'\n   137\t            if not ok:\n   138\t                verdict = 'inconclusive' if value == 'split' and verdict == 'pass' else ('fail' if value != 'split' else verdict)\n   139\t            lines.append(f'{pid} {item[\"label\"]:22} {view} {a[\"shape\"]:10} settled={value:6} votes={votes} [{rule}] {\"ok\" if ok else \"MISS\"}')\n   140\t    if need:\n   141\t        print('not final; needed:', ' | '.join(sorted(set(need))))\n   142\t        return\n   143\t    floor_pid = next(p for p, i in key.items() if i['label'] == 'K-impossible-floor')\n   144\t    def is_floor(d):        # the floor defect: its type, naming the floor figure F12 (readers may list the other figures differently)\n   145\t        return d[0] == floor_pid and d[2] == FLOOR_DEFECT[0] and 'F12' in d[3]\n   146\t    floor_caught = any(is_floor(d) for d in settled_defects)\n   147\t    floor_split = any(is_floor(d) for d in split_defects)\n   148\t    if not floor_caught:\n   149\t        verdict = 'inconclusive' if floor_split and verdict == 'pass' else ('fail' if not floor_split else verdict)\n   150\t    fresh_defects = [d for d in settled_defects if d[1].startswith('fresh ')]\n   151\t    refused = [f'{k[0]}/{k[1]}' for k, v in checked.items() if v.get('decision') != 'publish']\n   152\t    if fresh_defects or refused:\n   153\t        verdict = 'fail'\n   154\t    result = {'verdict': verdict, 'lines': lines, 'settled_defects': settled_defects, 'split_defects': split_defects,\n   155\t              'floor_control_caught': floor_caught, 'refused_or_held': refused}\n   156\t    (out / 'TALLY.json').write_text(json.dumps(result, indent=1, ensure_ascii=False))\n   157\t    print('\\n'.join(lines))\n   158\t    print('settled defects:', settled_defects)\n   159\t    print('split defects:', split_defects)\n   160\t    print('floor control caught:', floor_caught, '| refused or held:', refused)\n   161\t    print('VERDICT:', verdict)\n   162\t\n   163\t\n   164\tif __name__ == '__main__':\n   165\t    cmd = sys.argv[1] if len(sys.argv) == 3 else ''\n   166\t    target = Path(sys.argv[2]).resolve() if len(sys.argv) == 3 else None\n   167\t    {'build': build, 'check': check, 'packets': packets, 'tally': tally}.get(\n   168\t        cmd, lambda _: (_ for _ in ()).throw(SystemExit(__doc__)))(target)\n     1\t\"\"\"The one source of the defect definitions and the writer's format material for judging-v13s blind readers and adjudicators (D744).\n     2\t\n     3\tReaders and adjudicators get the same text: the writer's system text, every schema description, and these definitions, so that what\n     4\tthe format rules permit is never marked as a defect by one and cleared by the other.\n     5\t\"\"\"\n     6\timport json\n     7\tfrom pathlib import Path\n     8\t\n     9\tDEFINITIONS = \"\"\"Definitions (judge each against the passages and the format rules the writer was given, shown below; what the format rules permit is\n    10\tnot a defect):\n    11\t- supported: the forecast follows from the shown passages and the answer's stated assumptions; a reader could check it against them.\n    12\t  For a forecast, the assumption that decides the band must rest on the passages in one of three ways: its size, a bound that\n    13\t  justifies the rung, or, when the passages do not give the asked quantity, a related figure that the answer explains. An explained\n    14\t  related figure satisfies this requirement even though the other side of the line remains possible. It is explained when the\n    15\t  answer says why the comparison applies to the companies, the period and the quantity asked, and why, under the answer's own stated\n    16\t  assumptions, the chosen side of the line is more likely; an answer that does not explain this is not supported (a ruling). For an unresolved\n    17\t  member, supported means its reason holds: after the strongest relevant passages, a gap remains that keeps the rung unsettled (a\n    18\t  ruling).\n    19\t- number misread: a source figure's number, scale, sign or qualifier differs from what its passage's quote writes, allowing what the\n    20\t  format rules permit.\n    21\t- wrong arithmetic: a computed figure does not equal its operation on its inputs, within the tolerance the format rules state.\n    22\t- part above or added into its whole: a segment is larger than the total it belongs to, or is added again into a total that already\n    23\t  includes it (directly or through an intermediate figure or an estimate).\n    24\t- two forecasts of one quarter: two different figures are used as the value of one quantity and period. A growth rate computed from\n    25\t  guidance and used only as a stated benchmark for an estimate is not a second value, when one figure supplies the quarter in the\n    26\t  calculation (a ruling). Look through any rate or multiple, including inside a stated assumption, for a second dollar value of the\n    27\t  quarter.\n    28\t- own estimate passed off as the market's reference: a figure marked as the market's reference is, or rests on, the writer's own\n    29\t  estimate or forecast. A reference that applies a past period's reported share or multiple to another period (a quarter's mix\n    30\t  applied to the year) is our comparison estimate, not the market's (a ruling).\n    31\t- broken structure: a link that does not resolve, a figure that depends on itself, a missing or doubled answer, an outcome that is not\n    32\t  the question's quantity, or a rung whose band does not hold the outcome. For a range outcome, the format allows \"a band it overlaps,\n    33\t  with the reason in reasons\": such a rung is not broken structure; a band the range does not reach is. A total used for a narrower\n    34\t  quantity (total capital expenditure for data-center spending) without a stated deduction or a supported bound is an outcome not of\n    35\t  the question's quantity (a ruling).\"\"\"\n    36\t\n    37\t\n    38\tdef descriptions(schema, path=''):\n    39\t    \"\"\"Every description in the schema, keyed by its field path.\"\"\"\n    40\t    found = {}\n    41\t    if isinstance(schema, dict):\n    42\t        if isinstance(schema.get('description'), str):\n    43\t            found[path.strip('/') or 'answer'] = schema['description']\n    44\t        for key, value in schema.items():\n    45\t            if key != 'description':\n    46\t                step = '' if key in ('properties', 'items', 'anyOf') else key\n    47\t                found.update(descriptions(value, f'{path}/{step}' if step else path))\n    48\t    elif isinstance(schema, list):\n    49\t        for value in schema:\n    50\t            found.update(descriptions(value, path))\n    51\t    return found\n    52\t\n    53\t\n    54\tdef allowed_qualifiers(schema):\n    55\t    \"\"\"The qualifier values the writer's own schema allowed (D747: an older run's schema, not today's list).\"\"\"\n    56\t    options = schema['properties']['figures']['items']['properties']['qualifier']['anyOf']\n    57\t    found = [o['enum'] for o in options if isinstance(o, dict) and 'enum' in o]\n    58\t    if len(found) != 1:\n    59\t        raise SystemExit('the schema does not hold exactly one qualifier list')\n    60\t    return found[0]\n    61\t\n    62\t\n    63\tdef format_material(case_folder):\n    64\t    \"\"\"The writer's system text and every schema description, as shown to readers and adjudicators.\"\"\"\n    65\t    case_folder = Path(case_folder)\n    66\t    schema = json.loads((case_folder / 'schema.json').read_text())\n    67\t    return '\\n'.join(['# The system text the writer was given', (case_folder / 'system.txt').read_text(), '',\n    68\t                      '# The format rules the writer was given (schema descriptions)',\n    69\t                      json.dumps(descriptions(schema), indent=1, ensure_ascii=False), '',\n    70\t                      'The qualifier values the format allows (a qualifier not in this list cannot be recorded): '\n    71\t                      + ', '.join(allowed_qualifiers(schema)) + '.'])\n     1\t\"\"\"Adjudication and refusal-review packets for a judging-v13s blind test (SMALL-DESIGN.md §5, D742).\n     2\t\n     3\tAdjudication: for every reply whose two readers disagree (a member's \"supported\", or any defect one reader marks), one packet holding\n     4\tthe two readings, the system text, the format rules the writer was given (every schema description), the request as the writer saw it\n     5\tand the answer. Reader 1 is the Codex reading and Reader 2 the Claude reading; the adjudicators are not told which.\n     6\t\n     7\tRefusal review: for every refused first reply, one packet with the refusal messages, the system text, the schema, the user text and the\n     8\tanswer, asking the two registered judgements apart: is the forecast and its reasoning financially sound, and does the exact request\n     9\tclearly state the condition the refusal enforces (D742: a sound answer refused under a clearly stated condition still stops the run).\n    10\t\n    11\tUsage: python3 blind_adjudicate.py <blind test folder> <packet id> [<packet id> ...]\n    12\t       python3 blind_adjudicate.py refusal <blind test folder> <case> <writer>\n    13\t\"\"\"\n    14\timport json\n    15\timport sys\n    16\tfrom pathlib import Path\n    17\t\n    18\tsys.path.insert(0, str(Path(__file__).resolve().parent))\n    19\tfrom blind_rules import DEFINITIONS, descriptions, format_material  # noqa: E402\n    20\t\n    21\tHEADER = \"\"\"# Adjudication (judging-v13s blind test)\n    22\t\n    23\tTwo readers disagreed about the answer below. Settle each point against the passages in the request and the format rules quoted\n    24\tbelow, not against either reader's threshold. Read only what is given here.\n    25\t\n    26\tFor each member, decide **supported** (yes or no). For each defect a reader marked, decide **uphold** or **reject**, quoting the\n    27\tpassage words or the format rule that decides it.\n    28\t\n    29\t\"\"\" + DEFINITIONS + \"\"\"\n    30\t\n    31\tReturn only JSON: {\"members\": {\"<view id>\": {\"supported\": \"yes|no\", \"why\": \"...\"}},\n    32\t \"defects\": [{\"reader\": \"Reader 1|Reader 2\", \"type\": \"...\", \"figures\": [...], \"decision\": \"uphold|reject\", \"why\": \"...\"}]}\n    33\t\"\"\"\n    34\t\n    35\t\n    36\tdef _json(text):\n    37\t    \"\"\"A reading: the outermost JSON object in the file.\"\"\"\n    38\t    return json.loads(text[text.find('{'):text.rfind('}') + 1])\n    39\t\n    40\t\n    41\tdef reading_lines(label, reading):\n    42\t    lines = []\n    43\t    for member, mark in sorted(reading.get('members', {}).items()):\n    44\t        line = f\"- {label} on member {member}: supported = {mark.get('supported')}. Ground: {mark.get('ground', '')}\"\n    45\t        if mark.get('why_not'):\n    46\t            line += f\" Why not: {mark['why_not']}\"\n    47\t        lines.append(line)\n    48\t    defects = reading.get('defects', [])\n    49\t    if not defects:\n    50\t        lines.append(f'- {label} marks no defect.')\n    51\t    for defect in defects:\n    52\t        lines.append(f\"- {label} marks a defect: {defect.get('type')} (figures {defect.get('figures', [])}): {defect.get('detail', '')}\")\n    53\t    return lines\n    54\t\n    55\t\n    56\tdef build(folder, packet_ids, key_name='reader-key.json', packets='reader-packets', out_name='adjudication'):\n    57\t    key = json.loads((folder / key_name).read_text())\n    58\t    out = folder / out_name\n    59\t    out.mkdir(exist_ok=True)\n    60\t    for pid in packet_ids:\n    61\t        case, writer = key[pid]['case'], key[pid]['writer']\n    62\t        codex = _json((folder / packets / f'{pid}.codex.json').read_text())\n    63\t        claude = _json((folder / packets / f'{pid}.claude.json').read_text())\n    64\t        answer_path = folder / key[pid]['answer'] if key[pid].get('answer') else folder / case / 'answers' / f'{writer}.json'\n    65\t        case_dir = Path(key[pid]['case_dir']) if key[pid].get('case_dir') else folder / case   # D747: the writer's own request\n    66\t        text = '\\n'.join([HEADER, '# The two readings', *reading_lines('Reader 1', codex), *reading_lines('Reader 2', claude), '',\n    67\t                          format_material(case_dir), '',\n    68\t                          '# The request (questions, ladders and every passage)', (case_dir / 'user.txt').read_text(), '',\n    69\t                          '# The answer', answer_path.read_text()])\n    70\t        target = out / f'{pid}.md'\n    71\t        if target.exists():\n    72\t            raise SystemExit(f'{target} exists')\n    73\t        target.write_text(text)\n    74\t        (out / f'{pid}.prompt.txt').write_text(f'Read the file {target} and nothing else (no other file, no web). Follow its '\n    75\t                                               'instructions and return only the JSON it asks for.\\n')\n    76\t        print(f'{pid}: {case}/{writer}, {len(text)} chars')\n    77\t\n    78\t\n    79\tREFUSAL_HEADER = \"\"\"# Refusal review (judging-v13s blind test)\n    80\t\n    81\tA code check refused the answer below. For each refusal message, give two judgements apart, from the request text and the passages\n    82\talone:\n    83\t1. sound: is the forecast and its reasoning financially sound against the passages (would an analyst accept the number and the reasons\n    84\t   that produce it)? Judge this on its own, apart from the format rule the check enforces. Answer yes or no, with why.\n    85\t2. stated: does the exact request (system text, schema or user text below) clearly state the condition the refusal enforces? Answer\n    86\t   yes or no, and quote the sentence that states it, or say none does.\n    87\tReturn only JSON: {\"refusals\": [{\"message\": \"...\", \"sound\": \"yes|no\", \"why\": \"...\", \"stated\": \"yes|no\", \"sentence\": \"...\"}]}\n    88\t\"\"\"\n    89\t\n    90\t\n    91\tdef refusal(folder, case, writer):\n    92\t    checked = {(r['case'], r['writer']): r for r in json.loads((folder / 'CHECKED.json').read_text())}\n    93\t    row = checked[(case, writer)]\n    94\t    if row.get('decision') == 'publish' or not row.get('errors'):\n    95\t        raise SystemExit(f'{case}/{writer} was not refused')\n    96\t    out = folder / 'refusal-review'\n    97\t    out.mkdir(exist_ok=True)\n    98\t    text = '\\n'.join([REFUSAL_HEADER, '# Refusal messages', json.dumps(row['errors'], ensure_ascii=False), '',\n    99\t                      '# The system text', (folder / case / 'system.txt').read_text(), '',\n   100\t                      '# The schema', (folder / case / 'schema.json').read_text(), '',\n   101\t                      '# The user text (the request, with every passage)', (folder / case / 'user.txt').read_text(), '',\n   102\t                      '# The answer', (folder / case / 'answers' / f'{writer}.json').read_text()])\n   103\t    target = out / f'{case}-{writer}.md'\n   104\t    if target.exists():\n   105\t        raise SystemExit(f'{target} exists')\n   106\t    target.write_text(text)\n   107\t    (out / f'{case}-{writer}.prompt.txt').write_text(f'Read the file {target} and nothing else (no other file, no web). Follow its '\n   108\t                                                    'instructions and return only the JSON it asks for.\\n')\n   109\t    print(f'refusal review {case}/{writer}: {len(text)} chars')\n   110\t\n   111\t\n   112\tif __name__ == '__main__':\n   113\t    if len(sys.argv) == 5 and sys.argv[1] == 'refusal':\n   114\t        refusal(Path(sys.argv[2]).resolve(), sys.argv[3], sys.argv[4])\n   115\t    elif len(sys.argv) >= 4 and sys.argv[2] == '--reread':        # D743: the re-read's packets, key and adjudication folder\n   116\t        build(Path(sys.argv[1]).resolve(), sys.argv[3:], 'reread-key.json', 'reread-packets', 'reread-adjudication')\n   117\t    elif len(sys.argv) >= 4 and sys.argv[2] == '--reread2':       # D744: the second re-read under the shared rule text\n   118\t        build(Path(sys.argv[1]).resolve(), sys.argv[3:], 'reread2-key.json', 'reread2-packets', 'reread2-adjudication')\n   119\t    elif len(sys.argv) >= 4 and sys.argv[2] == '--reread-d747':   # D747: the re-read under Alex's comparison ruling\n   120\t        build(Path(sys.argv[1]).resolve(), sys.argv[3:], 'reread-key.json', 'reread-packets', 'reread-adjudication')\n   121\t    elif len(sys.argv) >= 3 and sys.argv[1] != 'refusal':\n   122\t        build(Path(sys.argv[1]).resolve(), sys.argv[2:])\n   123\t    else:\n   124\t        raise SystemExit(__doc__)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'd=docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers; nl -ba $d/judging-v13s.txt; nl -ba $d/judging-v13s-d745.txt | tail -1; nl -ba $d/SMALL-DESIGN.md | sed -n '\"'360,500p'; nl -ba poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/schema.json\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'd=docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers; nl -ba $d/judging-v13s.txt; nl -ba $d/judging-v13s-d745.txt | tail -1; nl -ba $d/SMALL-DESIGN.md | sed -n '\"'360,500p'; nl -ba poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/schema.json\"","aggregated_output":"     1\tYou are a senior hedge-fund analyst judging the current rungs of a family of related number theses: one company's quantities that overlap, such as a total and its segment, a level and its growth, or a later year that rests on an earlier year's base. Use only the supplied evidence and themes in force. Each rung is your forecast: the outcome the evidence in force makes most likely at the due date, not the one it has proved.\n     2\t\n     3\t- Judge every member from the evidence shown (what recent judgements relied on and what arrived recently) and the themes in force, as if for the first time. Earlier forecasts are not shown: whether a conclusion changed is compared afterwards.\n     4\t- Judge each member against a reference: what the market expects for that question (consensus, the company's guidance, the stated plan or deadline). Say where the reference sits on its ladder. A consensus or guidance figure by itself keeps the judgement at the reference's rung; once other evidence has arrived it is never the reason to stay. Then derive the outcome the evidence implies: reported figures and guidance give a run-rate whose pace you forecast, informed by guidance, never the last quarter annualised flat; a quantified revision, a customer's demand number, a supplier's constraint, a confirmed delay or cancellation shift it. A target for a product with no shipments yet is discounted subject to the evidence requirement below; it is taken in full only when the evidence shows the volume in hand. Build the family's figures once and read every member's outcome from them: a part cannot exceed its whole, a figure that spans several periods or products gives a question only its share, and a quantity the family shares has one forecast. Compare each outcome with its rung boundaries, and move when it crosses one.\n     5\t- Weigh by tier and by novelty. Only fact can establish that a rung's outcome already holds; \"not established\" is a statement of confidence, never a reason to stay. Guidance for the current or next quarter is the strongest forecast input. A management target for a product not yet shipping, or an ambition spanning years, is an input to be discounted for time and execution (how many selling quarters remain, what volume and price the figure implies, what the company's own choices say), not an answer; consensus is where the market stands, not the answer either. third_party_view and rumour move the forecast in proportion to their specificity (a number, a date, a named party): a research firm's shipment or revenue tracking with a number counts. A commitment or deal that is announced but not sized is sized from its disclosed comparable (the same programme's other deal, scaled by capacity or units); a figure no one states is derived from the accounting identities in the evidence when they allow it. A repeat, another outlet for the same claim, or an opinion that adds no new fact or calculation moves nothing. Missing confirmation alone is not grounds for the lowest or highest rung: an event not being confirmed is not confirmation that it did not happen.\n     6\t- An evidence item that replaces another includes its predecessor for context, such as guidance revised from 12% to 25% growth. The predecessor is no longer evidence in force.\n     7\t- \"Themes in force\" lists the library's open theme theses (policy stages, shared quantities) with their current conclusions. They are our own judgements, never evidence: do not cite them in cites. Weigh each one that bears on a member and report it in that member's theme_pushes with the direction it pushes that member (toward_first, toward_last or none); leave out a theme that does not bear on it. A theme marked \"updated since this thesis was last judged\" may change the conclusion; one that is not marked already stood at the previous judgement. When no themes are listed, theme_pushes is empty.\n     8\t- Your conclusions must be consistent with each other and with the themes in force as shown: a share cannot be judged at zero while the policy that gates it is judged likely to open.\n     9\t- Return the family's figures and one answer per member, as the JSON schema defines them. Every number an answer rests on is a figure; a number you judge rather than read or compute is an estimate, stated as yours. When a share, deduction, discount or pace could put the outcome on either side of a rung line, decide which side the evidence favours before you size it: the passages give its size, a bound that justifies the rung, or a related figure, with why it applies to the companies, period and quantity asked and why, under your own assumptions, that side is more likely. If you can say only that the outcome might fall short or overshoot, not which side is more likely, answer unresolved and name what is unknown. Write explanations in English; preserve IDs and any quoted source text.\n     9\t- Return the family's figures and one answer per member, as the JSON schema defines them. Every number an answer rests on is a figure; a number you judge rather than read or compute is an estimate, stated as yours. If the chosen rung depends on a share, deduction or discount, show what in the passages supports its size or gives a bound that justifies the rung. If the evidence cannot support a forecast, answer unresolved and explain the gap. Write explanations in English; preserve IDs and any quoted source text.\n   360\t  - for a forecast, the evidence for the band-deciding assumption;\n   361\t  - for an unresolved answer, the gap left after the strongest passages.\n   362\t  Adjudicators get the same definitions.\n   363\t- **Scope** (`blind4.py`; run folder `poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/`):\n   364\t  - cases S, E and P-v8;\n   365\t  - 6 unedited first replies (a fresh Claude subagent and a fresh read-only Codex session per case);\n   366\t  - 9 blinded packets (6 replies + 3 controls), each read by one Claude and one Codex reader: 18 first readings, plus adjudication\n   367\t    where they disagree.\n   368\t- **Controls**, each checked by the code before use:\n   369\t\n   370\t  | Control | Built from | Expected reading |\n   371\t  |---|---|---|\n   372\t  | K-unsupported | the saved third-run E Codex reply, with an unsized 15% cut | not supported |\n   373\t  | K-unresolved-unjustified | P-v8 Claude made unresolved with a vague reason | not supported |\n   374\t  | K-unresolved-justified | S made unresolved with the specific gap | supported |\n   375\t- **Pass:**\n   376\t  - every S and E reply has a settled \"yes\" on its forecast or on its reason for leaving the answer unresolved;\n   377\t  - both P-v8 replies have a settled \"yes\" on a forecast;\n   378\t  - all three controls read as expected;\n   379\t  - the refusal and defect stop lines hold as before.\n   380\t- **Otherwise:**\n   381\t  - a support split that adjudication does not settle leaves the run inconclusive, and it goes back to council;\n   382\t  - any failure goes back to council;\n   383\t  - a clean result supports asking Alex to authorize the 36-request paid re-send.\n   384\t\n   385\t**[D747] The re-read under Alex's comparison ruling (Alex: \"可以发布，要讲清楚\"; council rounds 61–61b), registered before any packet is\n   386\tbuilt.**\n   387\t- **The one change.** The shared definitions (`blind_rules.py`, \"supported\") add Alex's ruling. The band-deciding assumption must\n   388\t  rest on the passages in one of three ways:\n   389\t  - its size;\n   390\t  - a bound that justifies the rung;\n   391\t  - or, when the passages do not give the asked quantity, a related figure that the answer explains.\n   392\t\n   393\t  An explained related figure satisfies this even though the other side of the line remains possible. It is explained when the answer\n   394\t  says why the comparison applies to the companies, the period and the quantity asked, and why, under the answer's own stated\n   395\t  assumptions, the chosen side of the line is more likely. An answer that does not explain this is not supported.\n   396\t  - The first readers' step 2 asks, for a forecast, whether the passages support the deciding assumption's size or a bound, or whether\n   397\t    the answer explains a related figure as the definitions require.\n   398\t  - Adjudicators get the same definitions.\n   399\t  - The requests and the answers are unchanged. Each packet carries its writer's own request.\n   400\t- **What is re-read.** Nine stored answers under a fresh shuffle, W01–W09 (`blind4.py reread`). The key is in `reread-key.json`\n   401\t  beside, never in a packet.\n   402\t\n   403\t  | Answer | Expected reading |\n   404\t  |---|---|\n   405\t  | blind4 U01: P-v8, vague unresolved control | not supported |\n   406\t  | blind4 U02: S Claude, 98% share. It calls \"more than $730B\" a floor for data-center spending, then forecasts $705.6B of data-center spending on $720B of total spending, so its own assumptions cannot all hold (council 62) | not supported |\n   407\t  | blind4 U03: E, unsized 15% cut control | not supported |\n   408\t  | blind4 U05: E Codex, unsized 10% cut | not supported |\n   409\t  | blind3 T15: E Claude, a bracket between the market pace and the guidance, with Vera as all CPU revenue | supported |\n   410\t  | blind4 U08 (S Codex, $25B deduction) and U04 (S justified unresolved); blind3 T10 (S Claude, read for its spending argument only: its settled quotation error stands, and its defect marks are not scored); blind4 U06 (E Claude, 1.8x with a 90% Vera share, judged with its own share) | must settle either way: both first readers agree, or both adjudicators do |\n   411\t- **Changes from council round 62** (`codex-r62.md`), made before any packet was built:\n   412\t  - The definition says outright that an explained related figure satisfies the support requirement, even though the other side of\n   413\t    the line stays possible. Readers and adjudicators get the same words.\n   414\t  - Each packet carries the request its answer was written for. U03, T10 and T15 get the third run's request. The constructed\n   415\t    controls U01 and U04 are read under the request they were built for.\n   416\t  - The qualifier list shown comes from each packet's own schema, so the third run's packets do not list \"estimated\".\n   417\t  - All nine answers are shown in one layout, parsed and printed with one indent, so a control cannot be told apart by its layout.\n   418\t  - The tally refuses a key that is not exactly the nine answers, or a reading without a valid vote. A settled vote is final only\n   419\t    after Claude records its two reasons as compatible and applying Alex's rule (`reread-reasons.json`).\n   420\t    - Matching votes whose reasons are incompatible go to adjudication.\n   421\t    - An adjudicated agreement with incompatible reasons is a split.\n   422\t    - A \"yes\" on U06 that rests on the whole range staying at $30B or more does not apply the rule, since $20B × 1.5 × 90% = $27B.\n   423\t  - Checked on a scratch copy:\n   424\t    - without recorded reasons, the tally gives no verdict;\n   425\t    - with incompatible reasons, it asks for adjudication;\n   426\t    - with compatible reasons, it passes;\n   427\t    - a fixed \"no\" read as yes fails.\n   428\t- **Readers.** Fresh readers: one Claude subagent and one read-only Codex session per packet. Where they disagree, two adjudicators\n   429\t  read the packet, one per family.\n   430\t- **Scoring** (`blind4.py reread-tally`):\n   431\t  - It passes when the five fixed readings come out as expected and the four others settle.\n   432\t  - Matching votes whose stated reasons contradict each other on the comparison are recorded as unsettled. Claude reads them by hand.\n   433\t  - A settled defect on a writer's reply other than T10 is a stop.\n   434\t- **What the result decides:**\n   435\t  - Pass: the reading standard under D747 is settled. The next step is the E writer repair, with the council before any send, then a\n   436\t    $0 writer test, then Alex for the paid re-send.\n   437\t  - A split: Alex's words do not yet fix the reading. The split answers go back to the council, and to Alex as boundary cases.\n   438\t  - A failed fixed reading goes back to the council.\n   439\t  - A clean re-read does not turn D745 into a pass.\n   440\t\n   441\t**[D749] The $0 writer test of the E writer repair (Alex: \"批准修写手再小测\"; council round 63), registered before any writer is\n   442\tsent.**\n   443\t- **The request change** (`judging-v13s.txt`, last bullet; the D745 text is kept in `judging-v13s-d745.txt`). The two D745 sentences\n   444\t  are replaced by: \"When a share, deduction, discount or pace could put the outcome on either side of a rung line, decide which side\n   445\t  the evidence favours before you size it: the passages give its size, a bound that justifies the rung, or a related figure, with why\n   446\t  it applies to the companies, period and quantity asked and why, under your own assumptions, that side is more likely. If you can say\n   447\t  only that the outcome might fall short or overshoot, not which side is more likely, answer unresolved and name what is unknown.\"\n   448\t  - Sources:\n   449\t    - D745 (show the support);\n   450\t    - D747 (an explained related figure counts);\n   451\t    - D749 (decide the side before sizing, else unresolved naming the unknown);\n   452\t    - D745's ruling (a justified unresolved answer is usable).\n   453\t  - Size: 4,540 → 4,816 characters (+276). The writer's request now carries the same three routes the readers judge by, and the\n   454\t    decision order Alex approved.\n   455\t  - Checks at $0: cases 111 of 111, rehearsal 19 of 19, and the pinned v13 plan unchanged (`paid-plan.sha256`). The schema and the code\n   456\t    are unchanged.\n   457\t- **Scope** (`blind5.py`; run folder `poc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/`):\n   458\t  - cases S, E and P-v8, built from the repaired request;\n   459\t  - one fresh Claude subagent and one fresh read-only Codex session per case, 6 writers, each reading only `system.txt`, `user.txt` and\n   460\t    `schema.json`.\n   461\t- **Readers.** 8 blinded packets, shuffled X01–X08, with the key in `reader-key.json`:\n   462\t  - the 6 fresh replies;\n   463\t  - 2 controls carried unchanged from the targeted test, each shown with the request it was written for;\n   464\t  - each packet read by one fresh Claude and one fresh Codex reader under the D747 definitions.\n   465\t\n   466\t  Where they disagree, on support or on any defect, two adjudicators read the packet, one per family. The reasons behind every\n   467\t  settled vote are recorded by Claude as compatible or not (`reasons.json`). Matching votes with incompatible reasons go to\n   468\t  adjudication.\n   469\t\n   470\t  | Control | Built from | Expected reading |\n   471\t  |---|---|---|\n   472\t  | K-invented-cut | blind4 U05, E Codex: 90% of $40B with no order size or shipment pace | not supported |\n   473\t  | K-impossible-floor | blind4 U02, S Claude: \"more than $730B\" called a floor above its own $720B total | \"part above or added into its whole\" on F12, settled; its support is not scored |\n   474\t- **Pass:**\n   475\t  - every fresh S and E reply has a settled yes, on its forecast or on its unresolved reason;\n   476\t  - both fresh P-v8 replies have a settled yes on a forecast;\n   477\t  - every fresh reply is accepted and publishable by the code;\n   478\t  - no settled defect on a fresh reply;\n   479\t  - both controls read as expected.\n   480\t- **Otherwise:**\n   481\t  - a settled no on a fresh S or E reply, a settled defect on a fresh reply, a refused fresh reply, or a missed control fails;\n   482\t  - a support split left after adjudication, or a split on the floor control, is inconclusive.\n   483\t  - Either outcome goes back to the council, and the paid re-send stays on hold.\n   484\t  - Only a clean pass supports asking Alex to authorize the paid re-send.\n   485\t- **Known risk, disclosed now.** Readers split on boundary answers (D748). A fresh answer that sits on a boundary can leave the run\n   486\t  inconclusive even when the repair works.\n     1\t{\n     2\t \"type\": \"object\",\n     3\t \"properties\": {\n     4\t  \"figures\": {\n     5\t   \"type\": \"array\",\n     6\t   \"items\": {\n     7\t    \"type\": \"object\",\n     8\t    \"properties\": {\n     9\t     \"id\": {\n    10\t      \"type\": \"string\",\n    11\t      \"description\": \"Short and unique, such as F1; never a passage id.\"\n    12\t     },\n    13\t     \"what\": {\n    14\t      \"type\": \"object\",\n    15\t      \"properties\": {\n    16\t       \"population\": {\n    17\t        \"type\": \"string\"\n    18\t       },\n    19\t       \"measure\": {\n    20\t        \"type\": \"string\",\n    21\t        \"description\": \"The quantity, such as revenue or capital expenditure; never its basis.\"\n    22\t       },\n    23\t       \"scope\": {\n    24\t        \"type\": \"string\"\n    25\t       },\n    26\t       \"period\": {\n    27\t        \"type\": \"string\",\n    28\t        \"description\": \"For example FY2027, FY2027 Q3, FY2027 H1, FY2027 Q1-Q3, CY2026, end of FY2027.\"\n    29\t       }\n    30\t      },\n    31\t      \"required\": [\n    32\t       \"population\",\n    33\t       \"measure\",\n    34\t       \"scope\",\n    35\t       \"period\"\n    36\t      ],\n    37\t      \"additionalProperties\": false,\n    38\t      \"description\": \"What the number measures. Where the request lists keys (outcome keys, shared quantities), copy them exactly.\"\n    39\t     },\n    40\t     \"kind\": {\n    41\t      \"type\": \"string\",\n    42\t      \"enum\": [\n    43\t       \"source\",\n    44\t       \"computed\",\n    45\t       \"estimate\"\n    46\t      ],\n    47\t      \"description\": \"source: a number written in a shown passage's quote; computed: one operation on other figures; estimate: your judgement, with its assumption in basis_text.\"\n    48\t     },\n    49\t     \"basis\": {\n    50\t      \"type\": \"string\",\n    51\t      \"enum\": [\n    52\t       \"reported\",\n    53\t       \"guidance\",\n    54\t       \"consensus\",\n    55\t       \"third_party\",\n    56\t       \"forecast\"\n    57\t      ],\n    58\t      \"description\": \"reported: an actual; guidance: the company's outlook; consensus: analysts' average; third_party: one outside party; forecast: this family's own forecast, on a computed or estimate figure only.\"\n    59\t     },\n    60\t     \"value\": {\n    61\t      \"anyOf\": [\n    62\t       {\n    63\t        \"type\": \"number\",\n    64\t        \"description\": \"A point. Give value, or low and high, never both.\"\n    65\t       },\n    66\t       {\n    67\t        \"type\": \"null\"\n    68\t       }\n    69\t      ]\n    70\t     },\n    71\t     \"low\": {\n    72\t      \"anyOf\": [\n    73\t       {\n    74\t        \"type\": \"number\",\n    75\t        \"description\": \"A range, low at most high, with value null.\"\n    76\t       },\n    77\t       {\n    78\t        \"type\": \"null\"\n    79\t       }\n    80\t      ]\n    81\t     },\n    82\t     \"high\": {\n    83\t      \"anyOf\": [\n    84\t       {\n    85\t        \"type\": \"number\",\n    86\t        \"description\": \"A range, low at most high, with value null.\"\n    87\t       },\n    88\t       {\n    89\t        \"type\": \"null\"\n    90\t       }\n    91\t      ]\n    92\t     },\n    93\t     \"unit\": {\n    94\t      \"type\": \"string\",\n    95\t      \"enum\": [\n    96\t       \"USD bn\",\n    97\t       \"%\",\n    98\t       \"x\"\n    99\t      ],\n   100\t      \"description\": \"USD bn: money in billions of US dollars ($1.2 trillion = 1200; $500 million = 0.5). %: a percentage as its number (7.5% = 7.5). x: a multiple (double = 2).\"\n   101\t     },\n   102\t     \"evidence_id\": {\n   103\t      \"anyOf\": [\n   104\t       {\n   105\t        \"type\": \"string\",\n   106\t        \"enum\": [\n   107\t         \"e3660\",\n   108\t         \"e4687\",\n   109\t         \"e4689\",\n   110\t         \"e4691\",\n   111\t         \"e4692\",\n   112\t         \"e4764\",\n   113\t         \"e4856\",\n   114\t         \"e4857\",\n   115\t         \"e5044\",\n   116\t         \"e5399\",\n   117\t         \"e5400\",\n   118\t         \"e5401\",\n   119\t         \"e5402\",\n   120\t         \"e5403\",\n   121\t         \"e5404\"\n   122\t        ],\n   123\t        \"description\": \"Source only: the passage whose quote holds the number.\"\n   124\t       },\n   125\t       {\n   126\t        \"type\": \"null\"\n   127\t       }\n   128\t      ]\n   129\t     },\n   130\t     \"quote_number\": {\n   131\t      \"anyOf\": [\n   132\t       {\n   133\t        \"type\": \"string\",\n   134\t        \"description\": \"Source only: a complete number exactly as the quote writes it, never a piece of a longer number, range or 'plus or minus' expression, with its currency and scale or its percent sign, and nothing else: '$4.5 billion', 'US$300 million', '$2.1bn', '40%', '-3%', 'double', 'twice'. The value is the number as written (a minus only when the quote writes one). A written range in full ('$3–4 billion', 'between $3 billion and $4 billion', '$3 billion to $4 billion'), given as low and high; a change written 'from $3 billion to $5 billion' is two numbers, each copied alone. '$4.5 billion, plus or minus 3%' in full, given as low and high or its midpoint as value. Only US dollars.\"\n   135\t       },\n   136\t       {\n   137\t        \"type\": \"null\"\n   138\t       }\n   139\t      ]\n   140\t     },\n   141\t     \"qualifier\": {\n   142\t      \"anyOf\": [\n   143\t       {\n   144\t        \"type\": \"string\",\n   145\t        \"enum\": [\n   146\t         \"none\",\n   147\t         \"approximately\",\n   148\t         \"as much as\",\n   149\t         \"more than\",\n   150\t         \"less than\",\n   151\t         \"estimated\",\n   152\t         \"at least\",\n   153\t         \"roughly\",\n   154\t         \"around\",\n   155\t         \"nearly\",\n   156\t         \"almost\",\n   157\t         \"up to\",\n   158\t         \"about\",\n   159\t         \"below\",\n   160\t         \"under\",\n   161\t         \"over\",\n   162\t         \"near\",\n   163\t         \"or more\",\n   164\t         \"or less\"\n   165\t        ],\n   166\t        \"description\": \"Source only: the qualifier the quote writes just before the number (or 'or more' / 'or less' just after); none when there is none.\"\n   167\t       },\n   168\t       {\n   169\t        \"type\": \"null\"\n   170\t       }\n   171\t      ]\n   172\t     },\n   173\t     \"inputs\": {\n   174\t      \"anyOf\": [\n   175\t       {\n   176\t        \"type\": \"array\",\n   177\t        \"items\": {\n   178\t         \"type\": \"string\"\n   179\t        },\n   180\t        \"description\": \"Computed only: ids of figures, never passages, in the operation's order.\"\n   181\t       },\n   182\t       {\n   183\t        \"type\": \"null\"\n   184\t       }\n   185\t      ]\n   186\t     },\n   187\t     \"operation\": {\n   188\t      \"anyOf\": [\n   189\t       {\n   190\t        \"type\": \"string\",\n   191\t        \"enum\": [\n   192\t         \"sum\",\n   193\t         \"difference\",\n   194\t         \"product\",\n   195\t         \"ratio\",\n   196\t         \"share\",\n   197\t         \"growth\",\n   198\t         \"apply_growth\"\n   199\t        ],\n   200\t        \"description\": \"Computed only. sum(a, b, …) = a + b + … (one unit); difference(a, b) = a − b (one unit); product(a, b, …) = a × b × … (USD bn × x, x × x or % × x; never USD bn × %); ratio(a, b) = a ÷ b (same units give x; a ÷ x keeps a's unit); share(whole, percent) = whole × percent ÷ 100, the percent from 0 to 100; growth(old, new) = the % change, in one unit; apply_growth(base, rate %) = base × (1 + rate ÷ 100). sum and product take two or more inputs; the others exactly two. Never divide by a figure that can be zero. A range input gives a range: compute both extremes, a figure used twice taking one value; you may declare the midpoint as value. The figure equals the result within 0.5% (at least 0.1, or 0.05 for %). A sum never adds a figure of the part's quantity to one of the whole's for the same period (the whole already includes the part); a difference may take the part from the whole of the same period only to give the rest. A sum never adds one quantity for the same period twice. When a sum or difference combines periods of one quantity, write them FY2027, FY2027 Q3, FY2027 H1 or FY2027 Q1-Q3 (or CY likewise), and they make up the output's period exactly (Q1 + Q2 = H1; FY − Q1-Q3 = Q4).\"\n   201\t       },\n   202\t       {\n   203\t        \"type\": \"null\"\n   204\t       }\n   205\t      ]\n   206\t     },\n   207\t     \"basis_text\": {\n   208\t      \"anyOf\": [\n   209\t       {\n   210\t        \"type\": \"string\",\n   211\t        \"description\": \"Estimate only: the assumption, in words.\"\n   212\t       },\n   213\t       {\n   214\t        \"type\": \"null\"\n   215\t       }\n   216\t      ]\n   217\t     },\n   218\t     \"rests_on\": {\n   219\t      \"anyOf\": [\n   220\t       {\n   221\t        \"type\": \"array\",\n   222\t        \"items\": {\n   223\t         \"type\": \"string\"\n   224\t        },\n   225\t        \"description\": \"Estimate only: the ids of the figures and passages it rests on.\"\n   226\t       },\n   227\t       {\n   228\t        \"type\": \"null\"\n   229\t       }\n   230\t      ]\n   231\t     },\n   232\t     \"outcome_for\": {\n   233\t      \"type\": \"array\",\n   234\t      \"items\": {\n   235\t       \"type\": \"string\",\n   236\t       \"enum\": [\n   237\t        \"v41\"\n   238\t       ]\n   239\t      },\n   240\t      \"description\": \"The members whose forecast this figure is: your own forecast (a computed or estimate figure with basis forecast, even when it agrees with consensus), exactly one for each member answered as a forecast, none for an unresolved one. Its what is the member's outcome key, in USD bn.\"\n   241\t     },\n   242\t     \"reference_for\": {\n   243\t      \"type\": \"array\",\n   244\t      \"items\": {\n   245\t       \"type\": \"string\",\n   246\t       \"enum\": [\n   247\t        \"v41\"\n   248\t       ]\n   249\t      },\n   250\t      \"description\": \"The members for which this figure is the market's reference. It and every figure beneath it is a source or a computed figure, never an estimate or a forecast, and at least one of them is a quoted consensus, guidance or outside figure. A past period's reported share or multiple carried to another period (a quarter's mix applied to the year) makes it our comparison estimate, not the market's reference.\"\n   251\t     }\n   252\t    },\n   253\t    \"required\": [\n   254\t     \"id\",\n   255\t     \"what\",\n   256\t     \"kind\",\n   257\t     \"basis\",\n   258\t     \"value\",\n   259\t     \"low\",\n   260\t     \"high\",\n   261\t     \"unit\",\n   262\t     \"evidence_id\",\n   263\t     \"quote_number\",\n   264\t     \"qualifier\",\n   265\t     \"inputs\",\n   266\t     \"operation\",\n   267\t     \"basis_text\",\n   268\t     \"rests_on\",\n   269\t     \"outcome_for\",\n   270\t     \"reference_for\"\n   271\t    ],\n   272\t    \"additionalProperties\": false\n   273\t   },\n   274\t   \"description\": \"The family's calculation. Use one forecast figure for each quantity and period, as a point or a range, wherever that quantity is needed (outside figures beneath it, or a point a final range rests on, are fine). Guidance may inform a growth pace: record the pace you forecast as an estimate, say why it applies, name the benchmark in rests_on, and use that estimate in the calculation. A rate computed from a shared quantity's guidance or outside figure is never itself an input of the calculation. To turn a total into a narrower quantity (data-center spending out of total capital expenditure), write the deduction or share as its own figure, or give a supported bound; never relabel a total as the narrower quantity. Figures name each other by id; no figure may depend on itself.\"\n   275\t  },\n   276\t  \"answers\": {\n   277\t   \"type\": \"array\",\n   278\t   \"items\": {\n   279\t    \"type\": \"object\",\n   280\t    \"properties\": {\n   281\t     \"view_id\": {\n   282\t      \"type\": \"string\",\n   283\t      \"enum\": [\n   284\t       \"v41\"\n   285\t      ]\n   286\t     },\n   287\t     \"shape\": {\n   288\t      \"type\": \"string\",\n   289\t      \"enum\": [\n   290\t       \"forecast\",\n   291\t       \"unresolved\"\n   292\t      ],\n   293\t      \"description\": \"forecast: a figure marked in outcome_for, a rung, and a reference (a figure marked in reference_for, or reference_unavailable). unresolved: why; rung and reference_unavailable null; no figure marked for it.\"\n   294\t     },\n   295\t     \"rung\": {\n   296\t      \"anyOf\": [\n   297\t       {\n   298\t        \"type\": \"integer\",\n   299\t        \"description\": \"Forecast only: the rung whose band holds the outcome as the code recomputes it (a band includes its lower edge, not its upper); for a range outcome, a band it overlaps, with the reason in reasons.\"\n   300\t       },\n   301\t       {\n   302\t        \"type\": \"null\"\n   303\t       }\n   304\t      ]\n   305\t     },\n   306\t     \"reference_unavailable\": {\n   307\t      \"anyOf\": [\n   308\t       {\n   309\t        \"type\": \"string\",\n   310\t        \"description\": \"Forecast only, when no figure is marked as its reference: why.\"\n   311\t       },\n   312\t       {\n   313\t        \"type\": \"null\"\n   314\t       }\n   315\t      ]\n   316\t     },\n   317\t     \"reasons\": {\n   318\t      \"type\": \"string\"\n   319\t     },\n   320\t     \"why\": {\n   321\t      \"anyOf\": [\n   322\t       {\n   323\t        \"type\": \"string\",\n   324\t        \"description\": \"Unresolved only: why the evidence cannot support a forecast.\"\n   325\t       },\n   326\t       {\n   327\t        \"type\": \"null\"\n   328\t       }\n   329\t      ]\n   330\t     },\n   331\t     \"cites\": {\n   332\t      \"type\": \"array\",\n   333\t      \"items\": {\n   334\t       \"type\": \"string\",\n   335\t       \"enum\": [\n   336\t        \"e3660\",\n   337\t        \"e4687\",\n   338\t        \"e4689\",\n   339\t        \"e4691\",\n   340\t        \"e4692\",\n   341\t        \"e4764\",\n   342\t        \"e4856\",\n   343\t        \"e4857\",\n   344\t        \"e5044\",\n   345\t        \"e5399\",\n   346\t        \"e5400\",\n   347\t        \"e5401\",\n   348\t        \"e5402\",\n   349\t        \"e5403\",\n   350\t        \"e5404\"\n   351\t       ]\n   352\t      }\n   353\t     },\n   354\t     \"pushes\": {\n   355\t      \"type\": \"array\",\n   356\t      \"items\": {\n   357\t       \"type\": \"object\",\n   358\t       \"properties\": {\n   359\t        \"evidence_id\": {\n   360\t         \"type\": \"string\",\n   361\t         \"enum\": [\n   362\t          \"e3660\",\n   363\t          \"e4687\",\n   364\t          \"e4689\",\n   365\t          \"e4691\",\n   366\t          \"e4692\",\n   367\t          \"e4764\",\n   368\t          \"e4856\",\n   369\t          \"e4857\",\n   370\t          \"e5044\",\n   371\t          \"e5399\",\n   372\t          \"e5400\",\n   373\t          \"e5401\",\n   374\t          \"e5402\",\n   375\t          \"e5403\",\n   376\t          \"e5404\"\n   377\t         ]\n   378\t        },\n   379\t        \"push\": {\n   380\t         \"type\": \"string\",\n   381\t         \"enum\": [\n   382\t          \"toward_first\",\n   383\t          \"toward_last\",\n   384\t          \"none\"\n   385\t         ]\n   386\t        }\n   387\t       },\n   388\t       \"required\": [\n   389\t        \"evidence_id\",\n   390\t        \"push\"\n   391\t       ],\n   392\t       \"additionalProperties\": false\n   393\t      }\n   394\t     },\n   395\t     \"theme_pushes\": {\n   396\t      \"type\": \"array\",\n   397\t      \"items\": {\n   398\t       \"type\": \"object\",\n   399\t       \"properties\": {\n   400\t        \"view_id\": {\n   401\t         \"type\": \"string\",\n   402\t         \"enum\": [\n   403\t          \"v10\",\n   404\t          \"v18\",\n   405\t          \"v25\",\n   406\t          \"v26\",\n   407\t          \"v32\",\n   408\t          \"v40\",\n   409\t          \"v49\",\n   410\t          \"v6\",\n   411\t          \"v7\",\n   412\t          \"v9\"\n   413\t         ]\n   414\t        },\n   415\t        \"push\": {\n   416\t         \"type\": \"string\",\n   417\t         \"enum\": [\n   418\t          \"toward_first\",\n   419\t          \"toward_last\",\n   420\t          \"none\"\n   421\t         ]\n   422\t        }\n   423\t       },\n   424\t       \"required\": [\n   425\t        \"view_id\",\n   426\t        \"push\"\n   427\t       ],\n   428\t       \"additionalProperties\": false\n   429\t      }\n   430\t     }\n   431\t    },\n   432\t    \"required\": [\n   433\t     \"view_id\",\n   434\t     \"shape\",\n   435\t     \"rung\",\n   436\t     \"reference_unavailable\",\n   437\t     \"reasons\",\n   438\t     \"why\",\n   439\t     \"cites\",\n   440\t     \"pushes\",\n   441\t     \"theme_pushes\"\n   442\t    ],\n   443\t    \"additionalProperties\": false\n   444\t   }\n   445\t  }\n   446\t },\n   447\t \"required\": [\n   448\t  \"figures\",\n   449\t  \"answers\"\n   450\t ],\n   451\t \"additionalProperties\": false\n   452\t}","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/DECISIONS-log.md | sed -n '11484,11654p'; nl -ba docs/CURRENT_STATE.md | sed -n '448,463p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/DECISIONS-log.md | sed -n '11484,11654p'; nl -ba docs/CURRENT_STATE.md | sed -n '448,463p'\"","aggregated_output":" 11484\t## D745 · 2026-11-23 · The D744 re-read calibrates the readers but stops on a qualifier-list gap and on S's unsupported share; council 59 recommends a targeted $0 test of one clarification, for Alex to authorize\n 11485\t\n 11486\t- **The result** (`docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND3-RESULT.md` §5; `REREAD2-TALLY.json`):\n 11487\t  - **The readers are calibrated.**\n 11488\t    - All five controls passed: midpoint, 108.5 misread, overlap, disjoint, unsupported assumption.\n 11489\t    - No defect was marked by both readers on the 12 replies.\n 11490\t  - **STOP on two lines, as written:**\n 11491\t    1. T10 (S, Claude writer) F11: \"an estimated $750 billion\" recorded with qualifier \"none\". Both adjudicators upheld it. \"estimated\" is\n 11492\t       not in the closed qualifier list, so the format forced \"none\". This is 1 affected figure of 251 source figures in the three writer\n 11493\t       runs.\n 11494\t    2. S has zero usable replies. Both writers state a data-center share of total capex (90%; 95–100%) that the passages do not size, and\n 11495\t       the share decides the band.\n 11496\t  - **E's Codex reply** is now \"not supported\": its 15% cut from the guided floor decides the band. This is the same weakness.\n 11497\t- **Council round 59** (`codex-r59.md`) chose a targeted $0 test, (c), over Claude's lean to go to Alex for the paid re-send, (b):\n 11498\t  - Going straight to the paid re-send would measure the known weakness in gpt-6-luna without the request saying what a supported size\n 11499\t    is.\n 11500\t  - Skipping a clean result needs Alex to change the registered sequence anyway.\n 11501\t  - Claude agrees: the weakness is unsupported precision in a number that decides the rung, which is the goal's own failure (D725).\n 11502\t- **Proposed test (for Alex to authorize):**\n 11503\t  1. Add \"estimated\" to the qualifier list, and show readers the allowed values. Prove it on the saved replies, with a case and a\n 11504\t     mutant.\n 11505\t  2. Add one clarification to the existing instruction: when a deduction or cut decides the band, show the evidence for its size or a\n 11506\t     bound the passages support that justifies the band; if neither, answer unresolved.\n 11507\t  3. Test on S and on one stored case where the passages do support a narrower figure.\n 11508\t  4. Before the test, Alex rules whether a justified unresolved answer counts as usable.\n 11509\t  - Falsifier: a fresh S answer still picks a band on an unsupported share, or the supported case is withheld.\n 11510\t- **Alex's answers** (2026-11-23):\n 11511\t  - to \"怎么走？\": \"批准针对性小测 (推荐)\". The other options were \"不小测，直接议花钱重发\" and \"先停在这里\".\n 11512\t  - to whether a justified \"无法判断\" counts: \"算合格 (推荐)\". The other option was \"不算\".\n 11513\t- **Decided (Alex):**\n 11514\t  1. Run the targeted $0 test.\n 11515\t  2. An unresolved answer whose reason shows the passages cannot settle the rung counts as a usable answer.\n 11516\t- **Council round 60** (`codex-r60.md`) set the wording and the registration (`SMALL-DESIGN.md` §5 [D745]):\n 11517\t  - Rung-deciding shares, deductions and discounts must show their passage support or a justifying bound, else unresolved.\n 11518\t  - The default discount is made subject to that rule.\n 11519\t  - The three controls are the saved E 15% cut, a vague P-v8 unresolved answer and a justified S unresolved answer (expected no, no,\n 11520\t    yes).\n 11521\t  - Pass: every S and E reply settled yes on a forecast or its reason; both P-v8 replies settled yes on a forecast. A support split\n 11522\t    is inconclusive.\n 11523\t\n 11524\t## D746 · 2026-11-23 · The D745 targeted test fails on E's unsized cut; S splits on how far an indirect comparison can support a forecast; council 61–61b: one general question to Alex, then a $0 stored-answer re-read; the paid re-send stays on hold\n 11525\t\n 11526\t- **The result** (`docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND4-RESULT.md`; run `poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/`,\n 11527\t  `TALLY.json`):\n 11528\t  - **FAIL on a registered line.** E's Codex reply (rung 3, $36B) realises 90% of the $40B reference and itself says \"the passages\n 11529\t    give no order sizes or shipment pace\". Both first readers marked it not supported.\n 11530\t  - **Four splits, all along model family** (8 of 8 adjudicator votes went with their own family): S Claude (98% share), S Codex ($25B\n 11531\t    deduction), E Claude (1.8x with a 90% Vera share) and the justified-unresolved S control.\n 11532\t  - **The rest:**\n 11533\t    - P-v8: 2 of 2 supported;\n 11534\t    - the unsupported-cut and vague-unresolved controls read as expected;\n 11535\t    - no refusal, no defect;\n 11536\t    - 0 of 6 replies withheld.\n 11537\t  - **One construction change, disclosed.** The justified-unresolved control was rebuilt on the third run's S Codex reply, because the\n 11538\t    S Claude reply is refused by the current code (S1). Its reason text is unchanged.\n 11539\t- **Council rounds 61 and 61b** (`claude-r61.md`, `codex-r61.md`, `codex-r61b.md`) reached consensus:\n 11540\t  - **The FAIL stands.** Resolving every split would still leave E's Codex reply failed.\n 11541\t  - **Two problems.**\n 11542\t    1. **The reading standard is not settled.** The open point is how far a related figure can support a forecast when the passages do\n 11543\t       not measure the asked quantity on the deciding side.\n 11544\t       - S has split this way in three readings: \"more than $730B on AI infrastructure\" for the same four companies, as a floor for\n 11545\t         their data-center spending.\n 11546\t       - E Claude's bracket fails on its own assumptions ($20B × 1.5 × 90% = $27B, rung 4). That point is arithmetic.\n 11547\t       - D741 ruling 3 still requires a stated deduction or a supported bound. The threshold for \"supported\" when the support is an\n 11548\t         indirect comparison is Alex's to set.\n 11549\t    2. **Writers size a risk without evidence.** 3 of 4 E replies across two runs carry a disputed cut or share, and D745's sentence\n 11550\t       did not stop it.\n 11551\t  - **Corrections.**\n 11552\t    - Claude withdrew two reasons for the lean. D725 does not promise that code catches every invented figure. AGENTS §8's \"no data to\n 11553\t      verify\" concerns outcomes after the fact.\n 11554\t    - The result page was corrected in three places: U05 had two first readers; unresolved singletons are published and inspected; the\n 11555\t      paid S asks about CY2027.\n 11556\t    - Whether a paid STOP is predictable: it is a conditional risk, not a measured rate (Codex; Claude agrees).\n 11557\t  - **The recommendation.** Ask Alex one general question (below), with a lean to allow the forecast when the answer explains why the\n 11558\t    comparison applies to the companies, the period and the quantity asked, and why, under its own assumptions, that side of the line\n 11559\t    is more likely.\n 11560\t  - **The one test after his ruling.** A $0 blinded re-read of nine stored answers under his words, added to the shared definitions:\n 11561\t    - the answers: U01–U06, U08, the third run's T10 (for its spending argument only), and T15 (the accepted E bracket);\n 11562\t    - fresh readers, one per family;\n 11563\t    - what his choice implies for each answer is recorded before reading.\n 11564\t    - It moves on to a writer repair only if both families apply his words alike. A continuing split leaves the publication question\n 11565\t      open in practice. A clean re-read does not turn D745 into a pass.\n 11566\t  - **The paid re-send stays on hold** until an E writer repair passes a clean $0 test. The proposed repair: the writer chooses between\n 11567\t    a forecast and \"can't tell\" before it assigns a discount, naming what makes one side more likely under its own assumptions. It is a\n 11568\t    new arm and goes to council before any send. Publishing management's figure whenever it sits on a cutoff would change the product,\n 11569\t    and needs Alex's own decision.\n 11570\t- **The question to Alex** (pending): \"资料没直接给出所问的数。用一个相关数字作比较，能解释为什么结果更可能在线的这一边，但另一边仍有可能。这时可以发布预测，还是要等到能从资料算出结果落在线的哪一边？\"\n 11571\t- **Not done.** Nothing was paid. Nothing was tuned on the 36 stored answers. The request was not changed after the registration.\n 11572\t\n 11573\t## D747 · 2026-11-23 · Alex rules that a forecast may rest on an explained indirect comparison\n 11574\t\n 11575\t- **The question** (after D746): \"资料没直接给出题目问的那个数，只能拿一个相关的数字来比。比如问四大云厂商今年的数据中心开支能不能过 7000 亿，资料只说“这四家 AI 基础设施开支超过 7300 亿”。这种比较能说明结果更可能落在线的某一边，但另一边也还有可能。这时系统可以发布预测吗，还是要等资料能直接算出落在哪边？\"\n 11576\t  - Options:\n 11577\t    - \"可以发布，要讲清楚 (推荐)\": the answer must explain why the comparison applies to the companies, the year and the quantity\n 11578\t      asked, and why, under its own assumptions, that side is more likely; an answer that cannot explain it is not supported;\n 11579\t    - \"先说“还判断不了”\";\n 11580\t    - \"先停在这里\".\n 11581\t- **Alex's answer** (2026-11-23): \"可以发布，要讲清楚 (推荐)\".\n 11582\t- **Decided (Alex).** When the passages do not give the asked quantity, a forecast may rest on a related figure. It is supported\n 11583\t  only when the answer explains two things: why the comparison applies to the companies, the period and the quantity asked; and why,\n 11584\t  under the answer's own stated assumptions, the chosen side of the line is more likely. An answer that cannot explain this is not\n 11585\t  supported.\n 11586\t  - D741 ruling 3 still holds: a total used for a narrower quantity states the deduction as its own figure, or gives a supported\n 11587\t    bound.\n 11588\t  - D745's ruling still holds: a justified unresolved answer counts as usable.\n 11589\t- **Next (D746).** The ruling's words go into the shared reader definitions (`blind_rules.py`). Then one $0 blinded re-read of nine\n 11590\t  stored answers, registered with its expected readings and reviewed by the council before any packet is read.\n 11591\t\n 11592\t## D748 · 2026-11-23 · The D747 re-read fails on a self-contradicting floor; three of four earlier splits settle under the ruling; council 63: ask Alex to authorize the E writer repair; the paid re-send stays on hold\n 11593\t\n 11594\t- **The result** (`docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND4-RESULT.md` §6; `REREAD-TALLY.json`, `reread-reasons.json`):\n 11595\t  - **FAIL on the stop line.** On U02 (S, Claude writer), both adjudicators upheld \"part above or added into its whole\". The answer\n 11596\t    calls \"more than $730B\" of AI infrastructure \"the supported floor\" for data-center spending, while its own total spending is $720B\n 11597\t    and its data-center outcome $705.6B.\n 11598\t    - The code did not catch it. The figure is linked only to the 98% share, and the floor is claimed in prose. D738 leaves that\n 11599\t      reading to readers.\n 11600\t    - In the 18 stored blind replies this explicit defect occurs once (Codex, council 63).\n 11601\t  - **Six of nine settle with compatible reasons**, and each fixed reading that settled matched its registration:\n 11602\t    - U01, U03 and U05: no;\n 11603\t    - U06: no;\n 11604\t    - U04 (justified unresolved): yes;\n 11605\t    - U08 ($25B deduction explained by the same companies' AI-infrastructure figure and Meta's share): yes.\n 11606\t  - **Three of the four answers that split in the targeted test now settle** (U04, U06, U08).\n 11607\t  - **Three splits remain:** T10, T15 and U02's support vote.\n 11608\t    - All six adjudicator votes followed their own family's first vote. Which family was stricter changed with the question: Codex on\n 11609\t      S, Claude on E.\n 11610\t    - T15 had been accepted in all three earlier readings.\n 11611\t- **A process error** (Claude and council 62). The registration required five boundary answers to settle, or to settle as expected,\n 11612\t  without estimating how often readers split on boundary answers (AGENTS §7.1, \"a conjunction over many items fails by chance\"). The\n 11613\t  stop on U02 does not depend on it.\n 11614\t- **Council round 63** (`claude-r63.md`, `codex-r63.md`) reached consensus:\n 11615\t  - **The FAIL stands. No further reader round.** Another vote on the same nine answers leaves the writers untouched.\n 11616\t  - **Claude conceded three points.**\n 11617\t    - The remaining splits are not shown to be random variation. T10 and T15 turn on judgement over \"explains why\" and \"more likely\".\n 11618\t      On U02, one side accepted the band and the other required the writer's stated route. D747 already rejects U02's route. No\n 11619\t      missing general clause was found.\n 11620\t    - A writer test with registered lines only on stable readings would let a repeat of U05's invented cut through. Each fresh E and\n 11621\t      S answer must earn agreement for its forecast or for its specific reason for \"can't tell\".\n 11622\t    - Changing the calculation format for prose-claimed bounds is deferred. One case does not establish it, and U08 shows that an\n 11623\t      indirect comparison must stay able to inform a forecast without becoming a binding minimum. U02 becomes a control instead.\n 11624\t  - **The recommendation.** Ask Alex to authorize the E writer repair despite the three disclosed splits. D746 made the repair\n 11625\t    conditional on reader agreement, so going ahead needs his decision.\n 11626\t    - The repair: the writer decides whether the evidence favours one side of the line before it chooses a discount. If it can\n 11627\t      explain only why sales might fall short, it says what remains unknown.\n 11628\t    - The changed request goes to the council before any send.\n 11629\t  - **The one test after approval.** A $0 blind test:\n 11630\t    - fresh Codex and Claude writers on E, S and P-v8;\n 11631\t    - the saved U05 and U02 mixed in as controls for an invented cut and an impossible floor.\n 11632\t    - To pass:\n 11633\t      - each fresh E and S answer earns agreement for its forecast or its specific \"can't tell\" reason;\n 11634\t      - both P-v8 answers are supported forecasts;\n 11635\t      - the code accepts the fresh replies;\n 11636\t      - no confirmed defect survives.\n 11637\t    - A settled unsupported answer or a contradictory floor fails. A persistent split is inconclusive. Only a clean result supports\n 11638\t      asking Alex for the paid re-send.\n 11639\t- **Not done.** Nothing was paid. No request text was changed.\n 11640\t\n 11641\t## D749 · 2026-11-23 · Alex authorizes the E writer repair and its $0 writer test\n 11642\t\n 11643\t- **The question** (after D748): \"下一步怎么走？\"\n 11644\t  - Options:\n 11645\t    - \"批准修写手再小测 (推荐)\": the writer first decides whether the evidence favours one side of the line, then whether to discount;\n 11646\t      if it can only say sales might fall short, it answers \"can't tell\" and says what is missing. The changed request goes to the\n 11647\t      council, then one $0 writer test on E, S and P with the two failed answers as controls. A clean pass comes back to Alex for the\n 11648\t      paid re-send.\n 11649\t    - \"不修，直接议花钱重发\".\n 11650\t    - \"先停在这里\".\n 11651\t- **Alex's answer** (2026-11-23): \"批准修写手再小测 (推荐)\".\n 11652\t- **Decided (Alex).** Repair the writer request as council 63 described. Review it in council before any send. Then run one $0\n 11653\t  blind writer test on E, S and P-v8, with the saved U05 (invented cut) and U02 (impossible floor) as reader controls, under D748's\n 11654\t  pass lines. The paid re-send stays on hold until that test passes clean.\n   448\t**The one next task: the $0 writer test of the E writer repair (D749), after council round 64 reviews the request change.** Alex\n   449\tauthorized the repair: \"approve the writer repair, then the small test\".\n   450\t\n   451\t- **The repair** (`research/2026-11-22-number-answers/judging-v13s.txt` line 9; the D745 text is kept in `judging-v13s-d745.txt`).\n   452\t  When a share, deduction, discount or pace could put the outcome on either side of a rung line, the writer decides which side the\n   453\t  evidence favours before it sizes it. If it can say only that the outcome might fall short or overshoot, it answers unresolved and\n   454\t  names what is unknown.\n   455\t- **Size and checks.** 4,540 → 4,816 characters. Cases 111 of 111, rehearsal 19 of 19, pinned v13 plan unchanged.\n   456\t- **The test** is registered in `SMALL-DESIGN.md` §5 [D749] and built by `blind5.py`:\n   457\t  - S, E and P-v8, each written by fresh Claude and Codex writers;\n   458\t  - two reader controls (an invented cut, an impossible floor);\n   459\t  - every fresh E and S answer must earn agreement.\n   460\t\n   461\tThe record of the D748 step:\n   462\t\n   463\t**Earlier next task (done, D749): Alex chooses whether to authorize the E writer repair (D748).**\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'd=docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers; nl -ba $d/judging-v13s.txt; nl -ba $d/judging-v13s-d745.txt | tail -1; nl -ba $d/SMALL-DESIGN.md | tail -62; nl -ba $d/blind5.py | head -90; nl -ba docs/DECISIONS-log.md | sed -n '\"'11573,11654p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'd=docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers; nl -ba $d/judging-v13s.txt; nl -ba $d/judging-v13s-d745.txt | tail -1; nl -ba $d/SMALL-DESIGN.md | tail -62; nl -ba $d/blind5.py | head -90; nl -ba docs/DECISIONS-log.md | sed -n '\"'11573,11654p'\"","aggregated_output":"     1\tYou are a senior hedge-fund analyst judging the current rungs of a family of related number theses: one company's quantities that overlap, such as a total and its segment, a level and its growth, or a later year that rests on an earlier year's base. Use only the supplied evidence and themes in force. Each rung is your forecast: the outcome the evidence in force makes most likely at the due date, not the one it has proved.\n     2\t\n     3\t- Judge every member from the evidence shown (what recent judgements relied on and what arrived recently) and the themes in force, as if for the first time. Earlier forecasts are not shown: whether a conclusion changed is compared afterwards.\n     4\t- Judge each member against a reference: what the market expects for that question (consensus, the company's guidance, the stated plan or deadline). Say where the reference sits on its ladder. A consensus or guidance figure by itself keeps the judgement at the reference's rung; once other evidence has arrived it is never the reason to stay. Then derive the outcome the evidence implies: reported figures and guidance give a run-rate whose pace you forecast, informed by guidance, never the last quarter annualised flat; a quantified revision, a customer's demand number, a supplier's constraint, a confirmed delay or cancellation shift it. A target for a product with no shipments yet is discounted subject to the evidence requirement below; it is taken in full only when the evidence shows the volume in hand. Build the family's figures once and read every member's outcome from them: a part cannot exceed its whole, a figure that spans several periods or products gives a question only its share, and a quantity the family shares has one forecast. Compare each outcome with its rung boundaries, and move when it crosses one.\n     5\t- Weigh by tier and by novelty. Only fact can establish that a rung's outcome already holds; \"not established\" is a statement of confidence, never a reason to stay. Guidance for the current or next quarter is the strongest forecast input. A management target for a product not yet shipping, or an ambition spanning years, is an input to be discounted for time and execution (how many selling quarters remain, what volume and price the figure implies, what the company's own choices say), not an answer; consensus is where the market stands, not the answer either. third_party_view and rumour move the forecast in proportion to their specificity (a number, a date, a named party): a research firm's shipment or revenue tracking with a number counts. A commitment or deal that is announced but not sized is sized from its disclosed comparable (the same programme's other deal, scaled by capacity or units); a figure no one states is derived from the accounting identities in the evidence when they allow it. A repeat, another outlet for the same claim, or an opinion that adds no new fact or calculation moves nothing. Missing confirmation alone is not grounds for the lowest or highest rung: an event not being confirmed is not confirmation that it did not happen.\n     6\t- An evidence item that replaces another includes its predecessor for context, such as guidance revised from 12% to 25% growth. The predecessor is no longer evidence in force.\n     7\t- \"Themes in force\" lists the library's open theme theses (policy stages, shared quantities) with their current conclusions. They are our own judgements, never evidence: do not cite them in cites. Weigh each one that bears on a member and report it in that member's theme_pushes with the direction it pushes that member (toward_first, toward_last or none); leave out a theme that does not bear on it. A theme marked \"updated since this thesis was last judged\" may change the conclusion; one that is not marked already stood at the previous judgement. When no themes are listed, theme_pushes is empty.\n     8\t- Your conclusions must be consistent with each other and with the themes in force as shown: a share cannot be judged at zero while the policy that gates it is judged likely to open.\n     9\t- Return the family's figures and one answer per member, as the JSON schema defines them. Every number an answer rests on is a figure; a number you judge rather than read or compute is an estimate, stated as yours. When a share, deduction, discount or pace could put the outcome on either side of a rung line, decide which side the evidence favours before you size it: the passages give its size, a bound that justifies the rung, or a related figure, with why it applies to the companies, period and quantity asked and why, under your own assumptions, that side is more likely. If you can say only that the outcome might fall short or overshoot, not which side is more likely, answer unresolved and name what is unknown. Write explanations in English; preserve IDs and any quoted source text.\n     9\t- Return the family's figures and one answer per member, as the JSON schema defines them. Every number an answer rests on is a figure; a number you judge rather than read or compute is an estimate, stated as yours. If the chosen rung depends on a share, deduction or discount, show what in the passages supports its size or gives a bound that justifies the rung. If the evidence cannot support a forecast, answer unresolved and explain the gap. Write explanations in English; preserve IDs and any quoted source text.\n   425\t    - with incompatible reasons, it asks for adjudication;\n   426\t    - with compatible reasons, it passes;\n   427\t    - a fixed \"no\" read as yes fails.\n   428\t- **Readers.** Fresh readers: one Claude subagent and one read-only Codex session per packet. Where they disagree, two adjudicators\n   429\t  read the packet, one per family.\n   430\t- **Scoring** (`blind4.py reread-tally`):\n   431\t  - It passes when the five fixed readings come out as expected and the four others settle.\n   432\t  - Matching votes whose stated reasons contradict each other on the comparison are recorded as unsettled. Claude reads them by hand.\n   433\t  - A settled defect on a writer's reply other than T10 is a stop.\n   434\t- **What the result decides:**\n   435\t  - Pass: the reading standard under D747 is settled. The next step is the E writer repair, with the council before any send, then a\n   436\t    $0 writer test, then Alex for the paid re-send.\n   437\t  - A split: Alex's words do not yet fix the reading. The split answers go back to the council, and to Alex as boundary cases.\n   438\t  - A failed fixed reading goes back to the council.\n   439\t  - A clean re-read does not turn D745 into a pass.\n   440\t\n   441\t**[D749] The $0 writer test of the E writer repair (Alex: \"批准修写手再小测\"; council round 63), registered before any writer is\n   442\tsent.**\n   443\t- **The request change** (`judging-v13s.txt`, last bullet; the D745 text is kept in `judging-v13s-d745.txt`). The two D745 sentences\n   444\t  are replaced by: \"When a share, deduction, discount or pace could put the outcome on either side of a rung line, decide which side\n   445\t  the evidence favours before you size it: the passages give its size, a bound that justifies the rung, or a related figure, with why\n   446\t  it applies to the companies, period and quantity asked and why, under your own assumptions, that side is more likely. If you can say\n   447\t  only that the outcome might fall short or overshoot, not which side is more likely, answer unresolved and name what is unknown.\"\n   448\t  - Sources:\n   449\t    - D745 (show the support);\n   450\t    - D747 (an explained related figure counts);\n   451\t    - D749 (decide the side before sizing, else unresolved naming the unknown);\n   452\t    - D745's ruling (a justified unresolved answer is usable).\n   453\t  - Size: 4,540 → 4,816 characters (+276). The writer's request now carries the same three routes the readers judge by, and the\n   454\t    decision order Alex approved.\n   455\t  - Checks at $0: cases 111 of 111, rehearsal 19 of 19, and the pinned v13 plan unchanged (`paid-plan.sha256`). The schema and the code\n   456\t    are unchanged.\n   457\t- **Scope** (`blind5.py`; run folder `poc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/`):\n   458\t  - cases S, E and P-v8, built from the repaired request;\n   459\t  - one fresh Claude subagent and one fresh read-only Codex session per case, 6 writers, each reading only `system.txt`, `user.txt` and\n   460\t    `schema.json`.\n   461\t- **Readers.** 8 blinded packets, shuffled X01–X08, with the key in `reader-key.json`:\n   462\t  - the 6 fresh replies;\n   463\t  - 2 controls carried unchanged from the targeted test, each shown with the request it was written for;\n   464\t  - each packet read by one fresh Claude and one fresh Codex reader under the D747 definitions.\n   465\t\n   466\t  Where they disagree, on support or on any defect, two adjudicators read the packet, one per family. The reasons behind every\n   467\t  settled vote are recorded by Claude as compatible or not (`reasons.json`). Matching votes with incompatible reasons go to\n   468\t  adjudication.\n   469\t\n   470\t  | Control | Built from | Expected reading |\n   471\t  |---|---|---|\n   472\t  | K-invented-cut | blind4 U05, E Codex: 90% of $40B with no order size or shipment pace | not supported |\n   473\t  | K-impossible-floor | blind4 U02, S Claude: \"more than $730B\" called a floor above its own $720B total | \"part above or added into its whole\" on F12, settled; its support is not scored |\n   474\t- **Pass:**\n   475\t  - every fresh S and E reply has a settled yes, on its forecast or on its unresolved reason;\n   476\t  - both fresh P-v8 replies have a settled yes on a forecast;\n   477\t  - every fresh reply is accepted and publishable by the code;\n   478\t  - no settled defect on a fresh reply;\n   479\t  - both controls read as expected.\n   480\t- **Otherwise:**\n   481\t  - a settled no on a fresh S or E reply, a settled defect on a fresh reply, a refused fresh reply, or a missed control fails;\n   482\t  - a support split left after adjudication, or a split on the floor control, is inconclusive.\n   483\t  - Either outcome goes back to the council, and the paid re-send stays on hold.\n   484\t  - Only a clean pass supports asking Alex to authorize the paid re-send.\n   485\t- **Known risk, disclosed now.** Readers split on boundary answers (D748). A fresh answer that sits on a boundary can leave the run\n   486\t  inconclusive even when the repair works.\n     1\t\"\"\"The D749 $0 writer test (SMALL-DESIGN.md §5 [D749]): cases S, E and P-v8 built from the repaired request (judging-v13s.txt, the\n     2\t\"decide the side before you size it\" sentence), a fresh Claude and Codex writer each, readers under the D747 definitions, and two\n     3\treader controls carried from the targeted test with the request they were written for: U05 (E Codex, an invented 10% cut; expected\n     4\tnot supported) and U02 (S Claude, a floor above its own total; expected the part-above-whole defect upheld).\n     5\t\n     6\t  python3 blind5.py build <out>      # the three requests and writer prompts (refuses an existing folder)\n     7\t  python3 blind5.py check <out>      # every first answer through the schema, from_answer, check and publish_decision\n     8\t  python3 blind5.py packets <out>    # 6 replies + 2 controls, shuffled X01–X08; key beside\n     9\t  python3 blind5.py tally <out>      # the registered lines, after readings, recorded reasons and any adjudication\n    10\t\"\"\"\n    11\timport hashlib\n    12\timport json\n    13\timport random\n    14\timport sys\n    15\tfrom pathlib import Path\n    16\t\n    17\tHERE = Path(__file__).resolve().parent\n    18\tsys.path.insert(0, str(HERE))\n    19\timport blind4 as b4  # noqa: E402\n    20\tfrom blind_rules import format_material  # noqa: E402\n    21\t\n    22\tCASES = b4.CASES                                                     # ('S', 'E', 'P-v8')\n    23\tBLIND4 = HERE.parents[2] / 'poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23'\n    24\tCONTROLS = {'K-invented-cut': ('E', 'codex'), 'K-impossible-floor': ('S', 'claude')}     # blind4 U05 and U02, unchanged\n    25\tFLOOR_DEFECT = ('part above or added into its whole', ('F11', 'F12', 'F15'))\n    26\tREADER_BRIEF = b4.REREAD_BRIEF.replace('re-read under the comparison ruling, D747', 'writer test, D749')\n    27\tREPAIR = 'decide which side the evidence favours before you size it'\n    28\tif 'D749' not in READER_BRIEF:\n    29\t    raise SystemExit('the D749 reader brief is not built')\n    30\t\n    31\t\n    32\tdef build(out):\n    33\t    if REPAIR not in (HERE / 'judging-v13s.txt').read_text():\n    34\t        raise SystemExit('judging-v13s.txt does not hold the D749 repair')\n    35\t    b4.build(out)\n    36\t    for name in CASES:\n    37\t        if REPAIR not in (out / name / 'system.txt').read_text():\n    38\t            raise SystemExit(f'{name}: the built system text does not hold the D749 repair')\n    39\t\n    40\t\n    41\tdef check(out):\n    42\t    b4.check(out)\n    43\t\n    44\t\n    45\tdef packets(out):\n    46\t    rows = json.loads((out / 'CHECKED.json').read_text())\n    47\t    items = [{'label': f'fresh {r[\"case\"]}/{r[\"writer\"]}', 'case': r['case'], 'writer': r['writer'],\n    48\t              'answer': str(out / r['case'] / 'answers' / f'{r[\"writer\"]}.json'), 'case_dir': str(out / r['case'])} for r in rows]\n    49\t    for name, (case, writer) in CONTROLS.items():\n    50\t        items.append({'label': name, 'case': case, 'writer': name, 'answer': str(BLIND4 / case / 'answers' / f'{writer}.json'),\n    51\t                      'case_dir': str(BLIND4 / case)})\n    52\t    order = list(range(len(items)))\n    53\t    random.Random(hashlib.sha256(b'D749 ' + (out / 'CHECKED.json').read_bytes()).hexdigest()).shuffle(order)\n    54\t    folder = out / 'reader-packets'\n    55\t    folder.mkdir(exist_ok=False)\n    56\t    key = {}\n    57\t    for n, i in enumerate(order, 1):\n    58\t        item = items[i]\n    59\t        case_dir = Path(item['case_dir'])\n    60\t        pid = f'X{n:02d}'\n    61\t        key[pid] = item\n    62\t        (folder / f'{pid}.md').write_text('\\n'.join([READER_BRIEF, format_material(case_dir), '', '# The request as the writer saw it', '',\n    63\t                                                     (case_dir / 'user.txt').read_text(), '', '# The answer', '',\n    64\t                                                     b4._display(item['answer']), '']))\n    65\t        (folder / f'{pid}.codex-prompt.txt').write_text(\n    66\t            f'Read the file {folder / f\"{pid}.md\"} and nothing else (no other file, no web). Follow the brief at its top: read the request '\n    67\t            'and the answer it contains, and return only the JSON the brief asks for, with no text before or after it.\\n')\n    68\t    (out / 'reader-key.json').write_text(json.dumps(key, indent=1))\n    69\t    print(f'{len(order)} packets in {folder}; key in reader-key.json (keep from readers)')\n    70\t\n    71\t\n    72\tdef tally(out):\n    73\t    \"\"\"[D749] Pass: every fresh S and E reply has a settled yes on its forecast or its unresolved reason; both fresh P-v8 replies a\n    74\t    settled yes on a forecast; every fresh reply accepted and publishable; no settled defect on a fresh reply; K-invented-cut settled\n    75\t    not supported; K-impossible-floor's floor defect settled (both readers mark it, or both adjudicators uphold it). A settled vote is\n    76\t    final only after Claude records its two reasons as compatible (`reasons.json`). A support split left after adjudication is\n    77\t    inconclusive.\"\"\"\n    78\t    key = json.loads((out / 'reader-key.json').read_text())\n    79\t    expected_labels = {f'fresh {c}/{w}' for c in CASES for w in ('claude', 'codex')} | set(CONTROLS)\n    80\t    if {i['label'] for i in key.values()} != expected_labels or len(key) != len(expected_labels):\n    81\t        raise SystemExit(f'the key does not hold exactly the registered answers: {sorted(i[\"label\"] for i in key.values())}')\n    82\t    checked = {(r['case'], r['writer']): r for r in json.loads((out / 'CHECKED.json').read_text())}\n    83\t    reasons_path = out / 'reasons.json'\n    84\t    reasons = json.loads(reasons_path.read_text()) if reasons_path.exists() else {}\n    85\t    lines, need, verdict, settled_defects, split_defects = [], [], 'pass', [], []\n    86\t    for pid, item in sorted(key.items()):\n    87\t        first = [b4._reading(out / 'reader-packets' / f'{pid}.{w}.json') for w in ('codex', 'claude')]\n    88\t        adjudicated = [out / 'adjudication' / f'{pid}.{w}.json' for w in ('codex', 'claude')]\n    89\t        adj = [b4._reading(p) for p in adjudicated] if all(p.exists() for p in adjudicated) else None\n    90\t        answer = json.loads(b4._display(item['answer']))\n 11573\t## D747 · 2026-11-23 · Alex rules that a forecast may rest on an explained indirect comparison\n 11574\t\n 11575\t- **The question** (after D746): \"资料没直接给出题目问的那个数，只能拿一个相关的数字来比。比如问四大云厂商今年的数据中心开支能不能过 7000 亿，资料只说“这四家 AI 基础设施开支超过 7300 亿”。这种比较能说明结果更可能落在线的某一边，但另一边也还有可能。这时系统可以发布预测吗，还是要等资料能直接算出落在哪边？\"\n 11576\t  - Options:\n 11577\t    - \"可以发布，要讲清楚 (推荐)\": the answer must explain why the comparison applies to the companies, the year and the quantity\n 11578\t      asked, and why, under its own assumptions, that side is more likely; an answer that cannot explain it is not supported;\n 11579\t    - \"先说“还判断不了”\";\n 11580\t    - \"先停在这里\".\n 11581\t- **Alex's answer** (2026-11-23): \"可以发布，要讲清楚 (推荐)\".\n 11582\t- **Decided (Alex).** When the passages do not give the asked quantity, a forecast may rest on a related figure. It is supported\n 11583\t  only when the answer explains two things: why the comparison applies to the companies, the period and the quantity asked; and why,\n 11584\t  under the answer's own stated assumptions, the chosen side of the line is more likely. An answer that cannot explain this is not\n 11585\t  supported.\n 11586\t  - D741 ruling 3 still holds: a total used for a narrower quantity states the deduction as its own figure, or gives a supported\n 11587\t    bound.\n 11588\t  - D745's ruling still holds: a justified unresolved answer counts as usable.\n 11589\t- **Next (D746).** The ruling's words go into the shared reader definitions (`blind_rules.py`). Then one $0 blinded re-read of nine\n 11590\t  stored answers, registered with its expected readings and reviewed by the council before any packet is read.\n 11591\t\n 11592\t## D748 · 2026-11-23 · The D747 re-read fails on a self-contradicting floor; three of four earlier splits settle under the ruling; council 63: ask Alex to authorize the E writer repair; the paid re-send stays on hold\n 11593\t\n 11594\t- **The result** (`docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND4-RESULT.md` §6; `REREAD-TALLY.json`, `reread-reasons.json`):\n 11595\t  - **FAIL on the stop line.** On U02 (S, Claude writer), both adjudicators upheld \"part above or added into its whole\". The answer\n 11596\t    calls \"more than $730B\" of AI infrastructure \"the supported floor\" for data-center spending, while its own total spending is $720B\n 11597\t    and its data-center outcome $705.6B.\n 11598\t    - The code did not catch it. The figure is linked only to the 98% share, and the floor is claimed in prose. D738 leaves that\n 11599\t      reading to readers.\n 11600\t    - In the 18 stored blind replies this explicit defect occurs once (Codex, council 63).\n 11601\t  - **Six of nine settle with compatible reasons**, and each fixed reading that settled matched its registration:\n 11602\t    - U01, U03 and U05: no;\n 11603\t    - U06: no;\n 11604\t    - U04 (justified unresolved): yes;\n 11605\t    - U08 ($25B deduction explained by the same companies' AI-infrastructure figure and Meta's share): yes.\n 11606\t  - **Three of the four answers that split in the targeted test now settle** (U04, U06, U08).\n 11607\t  - **Three splits remain:** T10, T15 and U02's support vote.\n 11608\t    - All six adjudicator votes followed their own family's first vote. Which family was stricter changed with the question: Codex on\n 11609\t      S, Claude on E.\n 11610\t    - T15 had been accepted in all three earlier readings.\n 11611\t- **A process error** (Claude and council 62). The registration required five boundary answers to settle, or to settle as expected,\n 11612\t  without estimating how often readers split on boundary answers (AGENTS §7.1, \"a conjunction over many items fails by chance\"). The\n 11613\t  stop on U02 does not depend on it.\n 11614\t- **Council round 63** (`claude-r63.md`, `codex-r63.md`) reached consensus:\n 11615\t  - **The FAIL stands. No further reader round.** Another vote on the same nine answers leaves the writers untouched.\n 11616\t  - **Claude conceded three points.**\n 11617\t    - The remaining splits are not shown to be random variation. T10 and T15 turn on judgement over \"explains why\" and \"more likely\".\n 11618\t      On U02, one side accepted the band and the other required the writer's stated route. D747 already rejects U02's route. No\n 11619\t      missing general clause was found.\n 11620\t    - A writer test with registered lines only on stable readings would let a repeat of U05's invented cut through. Each fresh E and\n 11621\t      S answer must earn agreement for its forecast or for its specific reason for \"can't tell\".\n 11622\t    - Changing the calculation format for prose-claimed bounds is deferred. One case does not establish it, and U08 shows that an\n 11623\t      indirect comparison must stay able to inform a forecast without becoming a binding minimum. U02 becomes a control instead.\n 11624\t  - **The recommendation.** Ask Alex to authorize the E writer repair despite the three disclosed splits. D746 made the repair\n 11625\t    conditional on reader agreement, so going ahead needs his decision.\n 11626\t    - The repair: the writer decides whether the evidence favours one side of the line before it chooses a discount. If it can\n 11627\t      explain only why sales might fall short, it says what remains unknown.\n 11628\t    - The changed request goes to the council before any send.\n 11629\t  - **The one test after approval.** A $0 blind test:\n 11630\t    - fresh Codex and Claude writers on E, S and P-v8;\n 11631\t    - the saved U05 and U02 mixed in as controls for an invented cut and an impossible floor.\n 11632\t    - To pass:\n 11633\t      - each fresh E and S answer earns agreement for its forecast or its specific \"can't tell\" reason;\n 11634\t      - both P-v8 answers are supported forecasts;\n 11635\t      - the code accepts the fresh replies;\n 11636\t      - no confirmed defect survives.\n 11637\t    - A settled unsupported answer or a contradictory floor fails. A persistent split is inconclusive. Only a clean result supports\n 11638\t      asking Alex for the paid re-send.\n 11639\t- **Not done.** Nothing was paid. No request text was changed.\n 11640\t\n 11641\t## D749 · 2026-11-23 · Alex authorizes the E writer repair and its $0 writer test\n 11642\t\n 11643\t- **The question** (after D748): \"下一步怎么走？\"\n 11644\t  - Options:\n 11645\t    - \"批准修写手再小测 (推荐)\": the writer first decides whether the evidence favours one side of the line, then whether to discount;\n 11646\t      if it can only say sales might fall short, it answers \"can't tell\" and says what is missing. The changed request goes to the\n 11647\t      council, then one $0 writer test on E, S and P with the two failed answers as controls. A clean pass comes back to Alex for the\n 11648\t      paid re-send.\n 11649\t    - \"不修，直接议花钱重发\".\n 11650\t    - \"先停在这里\".\n 11651\t- **Alex's answer** (2026-11-23): \"批准修写手再小测 (推荐)\".\n 11652\t- **Decided (Alex).** Repair the writer request as council 63 described. Review it in council before any send. Then run one $0\n 11653\t  blind writer test on E, S and P-v8, with the saved U05 (invented cut) and U02 (impossible floor) as reader controls, under D748's\n 11654\t  pass lines. The paid re-send stays on hold until that test passes clean.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'd=docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers; nl -ba $d/blind4.py | sed -n '\"'1,265p'; nl -ba \"'$d/BLIND4-RESULT.md'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'd=docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers; nl -ba $d/blind4.py | sed -n '\"'1,265p'; nl -ba \"'$d/BLIND4-RESULT.md'","aggregated_output":"     1\t\"\"\"The D745 targeted $0 test (SMALL-DESIGN.md §5 [D745]): cases S, E and P-v8 built from the current request, a fresh Claude and Codex\n     2\twriter each, readers with the shared rule text, and three controls built from stored third-run replies (K-unsupported: E with an\n     3\tunsized 15% cut; K-unresolved-unjustified: P-v8 turned unresolved with a vague reason; K-unresolved-justified: S turned unresolved with\n     4\ta reason naming the gap). Each control is checked by the code before use.\n     5\t\n     6\t  python3 blind4.py build <out>      # the three requests and writer prompts\n     7\t  python3 blind4.py check <out>      # every first answer through the schema, from_answer and check\n     8\t  python3 blind4.py packets <out>    # reader packets: 6 replies + 3 controls, shuffled; key beside\n     9\t  python3 blind4.py tally <out>      # the registered pass lines, after the readings (and adjudication where they disagree)\n    10\t\"\"\"\n    11\timport copy\n    12\timport hashlib\n    13\timport json\n    14\timport random\n    15\timport sys\n    16\tfrom pathlib import Path\n    17\t\n    18\tHERE = Path(__file__).resolve().parent\n    19\tsys.path.insert(0, str(HERE))\n    20\tsys.path.insert(0, str(HERE.parents[2] / 'src'))\n    21\timport blind_test as bt  # noqa: E402\n    22\timport calc_small as cs  # noqa: E402\n    23\tfrom blind_reread2 import READER_BRIEF as _BRIEF2  # noqa: E402\n    24\tfrom blind_rules import format_material  # noqa: E402\n    25\tfrom hivemind import model  # noqa: E402\n    26\t\n    27\tCASES = ('S', 'E', 'P-v8')\n    28\t_STEP2 = '2. For each member, name the evidence for the assumption that puts the forecast in its band, and say whether the passages support it.'\n    29\tif _BRIEF2.count(_STEP2) != 1:\n    30\t    raise SystemExit('the second re-read brief no longer holds its step 2')\n    31\tREADER_BRIEF = _BRIEF2.replace('second re-read, D744', 'targeted test, D745').replace(_STEP2, (\n    32\t    '2. For each member answered as a forecast, name the evidence for the assumption that decides its band, and say whether the\\n'\n    33\t    '   passages support its size or a bound that justifies the rung. For each member answered unresolved, name the gap that remains\\n'\n    34\t    '   after the strongest relevant passages, and say whether the passages indeed cannot settle the rung.'))\n    35\tBASE = HERE.parents[2] / 'poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22'\n    36\tPROMPT = \"\"\"You are a writer in a blind test of a forecasting request. In the folder {folder} there are three files you may read:\n    37\t- system.txt: the system message of the request;\n    38\t- user.txt: the user message of the request;\n    39\t- schema.json: the JSON schema your answer must satisfy (strict structured output: every property present, null where a field does not apply).\n    40\tRead those three files and nothing else: no other file in any folder, no web, no tools beyond reading those three files.\n    41\tAct as the model that receives this request: do the analysis the system and user messages ask for, and return your answer as one JSON object that satisfies schema.json exactly.\n    42\tReturn only the JSON object, with no text before or after it.\n    43\t\"\"\"\n    44\t\n    45\t\n    46\tdef build(out):\n    47\t    if out.exists():\n    48\t        raise SystemExit(f'{out} exists')\n    49\t    for name in CASES:\n    50\t        it = bt.build_case(name, bt.CASES[name])\n    51\t        folder = out / name\n    52\t        (folder / 'answers').mkdir(parents=True)\n    53\t        (folder / 'system.txt').write_text(it['system'])\n    54\t        (folder / 'user.txt').write_text(it['user'])\n    55\t        (folder / 'schema.json').write_text(json.dumps(it['schema'], indent=1, ensure_ascii=False))\n    56\t        (folder / 'item.json').write_text(json.dumps(it, ensure_ascii=False))\n    57\t        (folder / 'writer-prompt.txt').write_text(PROMPT.format(folder=folder))\n    58\t        print(f'{name}: {it[\"arm\"]}, members {it[\"members\"]}, request {len(it[\"system\"]) + len(it[\"user\"])} chars')\n    59\t\n    60\t\n    61\tdef check(out):\n    62\t    rows = []\n    63\t    for name in CASES:\n    64\t        it = json.loads((out / name / 'item.json').read_text())\n    65\t        for w in bt.WRITERS:\n    66\t            path = out / name / 'answers' / f'{w}.json'\n    67\t            if not path.exists():\n    68\t                raise SystemExit(f'INCOMPLETE: no first answer for {name}/{w}')\n    69\t            raw = path.read_text()\n    70\t            row = {'case': name, 'writer': w, 'sha256': hashlib.sha256(raw.encode()).hexdigest()}\n    71\t            try:\n    72\t                data = bt.first_json(raw)\n    73\t            except json.JSONDecodeError:\n    74\t                data = None\n    75\t            if data is None:\n    76\t                row.update(stage='not JSON', errors=[['format', 'not one JSON object as returned']])\n    77\t            elif model.check_schema(data, it['schema']):\n    78\t                row.update(stage='schema', errors=[['schema', e] for e in model.check_schema(data, it['schema'])])\n    79\t            else:\n    80\t                calc = cs.from_answer(data, it['family'])\n    81\t                result = cs.check(it['family'], it['passages'], calc)\n    82\t                row.update(stage='checked', errors=[list(e) for e in result['errors']], recorded=result['recorded'],\n    83\t                           published=result['published'], decision=cs.publish_decision(it['family'], calc, result),\n    84\t                           shapes={a['view_id']: a['shape'] for a in data['answers']})\n    85\t            rows.append(row)\n    86\t    (out / 'CHECKED.json').write_text(json.dumps(rows, indent=1, ensure_ascii=False))\n    87\t    for r in rows:\n    88\t        print(f'{r[\"case\"]:5} {r[\"writer\"]:7} {r[\"stage\"]:8} {r.get(\"decision\", \"-\"):9} {r.get(\"shapes\")} '\n    89\t              + '; '.join(f'{c}: {d}' for c, d in r['errors'])[:300])\n    90\t\n    91\t\n    92\tdef _fig(answer, fid):\n    93\t    return next(f for f in answer['figures'] if f['id'] == fid)\n    94\t\n    95\t\n    96\tdef _unresolved(answer, why):\n    97\t    a = copy.deepcopy(answer)\n    98\t    for f in a['figures']:\n    99\t        f['outcome_for'], f['reference_for'] = [], []\n   100\t    a['answers'][0].update(shape='unresolved', rung=None, reference_unavailable=None, reasons=why, why=why)\n   101\t    return a\n   102\t\n   103\t\n   104\tdef controls(out):\n   105\t    made = {'K-unsupported': ('E', json.loads((BASE / 'E' / 'answers' / 'codex.json').read_text()))}\n   106\t    made['K-unresolved-unjustified'] = ('P-v8', _unresolved(json.loads((BASE / 'P-v8' / 'answers' / 'claude.json').read_text()),\n   107\t                                                            'The outlook for the second half is uncertain.'))\n   108\t    # Built on the third run's S Codex reply: the S Claude reply is refused by the current code (S1, F11 \"estimated\").\n   109\t    made['K-unresolved-justified'] = ('S', _unresolved(json.loads((BASE / 'S' / 'answers' / 'codex.json').read_text()), (\n   110\t        'The question asks for the four companies\\' data-center capital expenditure, and the rung turns on whether it reaches $700 billion. '\n   111\t        'The passages give the four companies\\' total capital expenditure (about $720-745 billion, e5014; tracked near $745 billion and '\n   112\t        '$760 billion, e1856, e2458), AI-infrastructure spending of more than $730 billion (e1947), and data-center capex of nearly $800 '\n   113\t        'billion for five companies (e5376). None of them gives the four companies\\' data-center share: a total, a broader AI category '\n   114\t        'and a five-company figure do not settle whether the four-company data-center figure is above or below $700 billion, since a '\n   115\t        'share of about 94% of the total is the dividing line. So the rung cannot be settled from these passages.')))\n   116\t    for name, (case, answer) in made.items():\n   117\t        it = json.loads((out / case / 'item.json').read_text())\n   118\t        if model.check_schema(answer, it['schema']):\n   119\t            raise SystemExit(f'{name}: schema errors {model.check_schema(answer, it[\"schema\"])[:2]}')\n   120\t        codes = sorted({c for c, _ in cs.check(it['family'], it['passages'], cs.from_answer(answer, it['family']))['errors']})\n   121\t        if codes:\n   122\t            raise SystemExit(f'{name}: the code refuses it ({codes}); a reader control must be accepted by the code')\n   123\t    return made\n   124\t\n   125\t\n   126\tdef packets(out):\n   127\t    rows = json.loads((out / 'CHECKED.json').read_text())\n   128\t    items = [{'case': r['case'], 'writer': r['writer'], 'answer': f'{r[\"case\"]}/answers/{r[\"writer\"]}.json'} for r in rows]\n   129\t    folder = out / 'controls'\n   130\t    folder.mkdir(exist_ok=False)\n   131\t    for name, (case, answer) in controls(out).items():\n   132\t        (folder / f'{name}.json').write_text(json.dumps(answer, ensure_ascii=False))\n   133\t        items.append({'case': case, 'writer': name, 'answer': f'controls/{name}.json'})\n   134\t    order = list(range(len(items)))\n   135\t    random.Random(hashlib.sha256(b'D745 ' + (out / 'CHECKED.json').read_bytes()).hexdigest()).shuffle(order)\n   136\t    packets_dir = out / 'reader-packets'\n   137\t    packets_dir.mkdir(exist_ok=False)\n   138\t    key = {}\n   139\t    for n, i in enumerate(order, 1):\n   140\t        item = items[i]\n   141\t        it = json.loads((out / item['case'] / 'item.json').read_text())\n   142\t        pid = f'U{n:02d}'\n   143\t        key[pid] = item\n   144\t        (packets_dir / f'{pid}.md').write_text('\\n'.join([READER_BRIEF, format_material(out / item['case']), '',\n   145\t                                                          '# The request as the writer saw it', '', it['user'], '', '# The answer', '',\n   146\t                                                          (out / item['answer']).read_text(), '']))\n   147\t    (out / 'reader-key.json').write_text(json.dumps(key, indent=1))\n   148\t    print(f'{len(order)} packets in {packets_dir}; key in reader-key.json (keep from readers)')\n   149\t\n   150\t\n   151\tEXPECTED = {'K-unsupported': 'no', 'K-unresolved-unjustified': 'no', 'K-unresolved-justified': 'yes'}\n   152\t\n   153\t\n   154\tdef _reading(path):\n   155\t    text = path.read_text()\n   156\t    return json.loads(text[text.find('{'):text.rfind('}') + 1])\n   157\t\n   158\t\n   159\tdef _defect_key(defect):\n   160\t    return (defect.get('type'), tuple(sorted(defect.get('figures') or [])))\n   161\t\n   162\t\n   163\tdef tally(out):\n   164\t    \"\"\"The registered D745 pass lines, scored as written: support settled by the two readers or, where they split, by both\n   165\t    adjudicators; a defect settled when both readers mark it or both adjudicators uphold it.\"\"\"\n   166\t    key = json.loads((out / 'reader-key.json').read_text())\n   167\t    checked = {(r['case'], r['writer']): r for r in json.loads((out / 'CHECKED.json').read_text())}\n   168\t    rows, need, settled_defects, split_defects = {}, [], [], []\n   169\t    for pid, item in sorted(key.items()):\n   170\t        first = [_reading(out / 'reader-packets' / f'{pid}.{w}.json') for w in ('codex', 'claude')]\n   171\t        adjudicated = [out / 'adjudication' / f'{pid}.{w}.json' for w in ('codex', 'claude')]\n   172\t        adj = [_reading(p) for p in adjudicated] if all(p.exists() for p in adjudicated) else None\n   173\t        answer = json.loads((out / item['answer']).read_text())\n   174\t        shapes = {a['view_id']: a['shape'] for a in answer['answers']}\n   175\t        marks = [{_defect_key(d) for d in r.get('defects', [])} for r in first]\n   176\t        members = {}\n   177\t        for view in shapes:\n   178\t            votes = [r['members'].get(view, {}).get('supported') for r in first]\n   179\t            if votes[0] == votes[1]:\n   180\t                members[view] = (votes[0], votes)\n   181\t            elif adj is None:\n   182\t                members[view] = ('needs adjudication', votes)\n   183\t            else:\n   184\t                second = [a['members'].get(view, {}).get('supported') for a in adj]\n   185\t                members[view] = (second[0] if second[0] == second[1] else 'split', votes + second)\n   186\t        both = marks[0] & marks[1]\n   187\t        either = (marks[0] | marks[1]) - both\n   188\t        for d in both:\n   189\t            settled_defects.append([pid, item['case'], item['writer'], d[0], list(d[1]), 'both readers'])\n   190\t        if either and adj is None:\n   191\t            need.append(pid)\n   192\t        elif either:\n   193\t            decisions = [{_defect_key(d): d.get('decision') for d in a.get('defects', [])} for a in adj]\n   194\t            for d in sorted(either, key=str):\n   195\t                pair = [decisions[0].get(d), decisions[1].get(d)]\n   196\t                row = [pid, item['case'], item['writer'], d[0], list(d[1]), pair]\n   197\t                if pair == ['uphold', 'uphold']:\n   198\t                    settled_defects.append(row)\n   199\t                elif pair != ['reject', 'reject']:       # a split, or a defect an adjudicator did not decide: read by hand\n   200\t                    split_defects.append(row)\n   201\t        if any(v[0] == 'needs adjudication' for v in members.values()) and pid not in need:\n   202\t            need.append(pid)\n   203\t        rows[pid] = {'case': item['case'], 'writer': item['writer'], 'shapes': shapes, 'members': members}\n   204\t    if need:\n   205\t        print('adjudication needed for:', ' '.join(sorted(need)))\n   206\t        return\n   207\t    lines, verdict = [], 'pass'\n   208\t    for pid, r in rows.items():\n   209\t        for view, (value, votes) in r['members'].items():\n   210\t            shape = r['shapes'][view]\n   211\t            if r['writer'] in EXPECTED:\n   212\t                ok = value == EXPECTED[r['writer']]\n   213\t                rule = f'control expects {EXPECTED[r[\"writer\"]]}'\n   214\t            elif r['case'] == 'P-v8':\n   215\t                ok = shape == 'forecast' and value == 'yes'\n   216\t                rule = 'a supported forecast'\n   217\t            else:\n   218\t                ok = value == 'yes'\n   219\t                rule = 'a supported forecast or a justified unresolved answer'\n   220\t            if value == 'split':\n   221\t                verdict = 'inconclusive' if verdict == 'pass' else verdict\n   222\t            elif not ok:\n   223\t                verdict = 'fail'\n   224\t            lines.append(f'{pid} {r[\"case\"]:5} {r[\"writer\"]:25} {view} {shape:10} settled={value:6} votes={votes} [{rule}] '\n   225\t                         f'{\"ok\" if ok else \"MISS\"}')\n   226\t    stops = [d for d in settled_defects if d[2] not in EXPECTED]\n   227\t    refused = [k for k, v in checked.items() if v.get('decision') != 'publish']\n   228\t    if stops or refused:\n   229\t        verdict = 'fail'\n   230\t    result = {'verdict': verdict, 'lines': lines, 'settled_defects': settled_defects, 'split_defects': split_defects,\n   231\t              'refused': refused}\n   232\t    (out / 'TALLY.json').write_text(json.dumps(result, indent=1, ensure_ascii=False))\n   233\t    print('\\n'.join(lines))\n   234\t    print('settled defects:', settled_defects)\n   235\t    print('split defects (disclosed):', split_defects)\n   236\t    print('refused first replies:', refused)\n   237\t    print('VERDICT:', verdict)\n   238\t\n   239\t\n   240\t_STEP2_D745 = READER_BRIEF[READER_BRIEF.index('2. For each member answered as a forecast'):READER_BRIEF.index('Return JSON:')]\n   241\tREREAD_BRIEF = READER_BRIEF.replace('targeted test, D745', 're-read under the comparison ruling, D747').replace(_STEP2_D745, (\n   242\t    '2. For each member answered as a forecast, name the evidence for the assumption that decides its band, and say whether the\\n'\n   243\t    '   passages support its size or a bound that justifies the rung, or, where the passages do not give the asked quantity, whether\\n'\n   244\t    '   the answer explains a related figure as the definitions below require. For each member answered unresolved, name the gap that\\n'\n   245\t    '   remains after the strongest relevant passages, and say whether the passages indeed cannot settle the rung.\\n\\n'))\n   246\tif 'D747' not in REREAD_BRIEF or 'An explained related figure satisfies this requirement' not in ' '.join(REREAD_BRIEF.split()):\n   247\t    raise SystemExit('the D747 re-read brief does not hold the ruling')\n   248\tBLIND3_REREAD2 = {'T10': ('S', 'claude'), 'T15': ('E', 'claude')}                 # SMALL-DESIGN.md §5 [D747]\n   249\tREREAD_FROM_BLIND4 = ('U01', 'U02', 'U03', 'U04', 'U05', 'U06', 'U08')\n   250\tREREAD_EXPECTED = {'blind4 U01': 'no', 'blind4 U02': 'no', 'blind4 U03': 'no', 'blind4 U05': 'no', 'blind3 T15': 'yes'}\n   251\tREREAD_LABELS = {f'blind4 {p}' for p in REREAD_FROM_BLIND4} | {f'blind3 {p}' for p in BLIND3_REREAD2}\n   252\tORIGINAL_BLIND3 = {'K-unsupported': 'E'}      # U03 is the third run's E Codex reply unchanged: it is read under the request it was written for\n   253\t\n   254\t\n   255\tdef _display(path):\n   256\t    \"\"\"Every answer shown the same way (parsed and printed with one indent), so a control is not told apart by its layout.\"\"\"\n   257\t    return json.dumps(bt.first_json(Path(path).read_text()), indent=1, ensure_ascii=False)\n   258\t\n   259\t\n   260\tdef reread(out):\n   261\t    \"\"\"[D747] Nine stored answers re-read under the shared definitions with Alex's comparison ruling: seven from this run and the third\n   262\t    run's T10 (its spending argument only) and T15 (the bracket both families accepted). Each packet carries the request its answer\n   263\t    was written for; the constructed controls U01 and U04 are read under the request they were built for.\"\"\"\n   264\t    key4 = json.loads((out / 'reader-key.json').read_text())\n   265\t    key3 = json.loads((BASE / 'reread2-key.json').read_text())\n     1\t# The D745 targeted test: result\n     2\t\n     3\tRun folder: `poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/`. It was registered in `SMALL-DESIGN.md` §5 [D745] and scored by\n     4\t`blind4.py tally` (`TALLY.json`).\n     5\t\n     6\t## 1. Verdict: FAIL (registered line \"every S and E reply has a settled yes\")\n     7\t\n     8\t| Packet | Case | Writer | Shape | First readers (Codex, Claude) | Adjudicators (Codex, Claude) | Settled | Line |\n     9\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n    10\t| U05 | E | codex | forecast, rung 3 | no, no | (none) | **no** | **MISS** |\n    11\t| U02 | S | claude | forecast, rung 4 | no, yes | no, yes | split | MISS (inconclusive) |\n    12\t| U08 | S | codex | forecast, rung 4 | no, yes | no, yes | split | MISS (inconclusive) |\n    13\t| U06 | E | claude | forecast, rung 3 | no, yes | no, yes | split | MISS (inconclusive) |\n    14\t| U04 | S | control: justified unresolved | unresolved | yes, no | yes, no | split | control not confirmed |\n    15\t| U07 | P-v8 | codex | forecast, rung 2 | yes, yes | (none) | yes | ok |\n    16\t| U09 | P-v8 | claude | forecast, rung 2 | yes, yes | (none) | yes | ok |\n    17\t| U01 | P-v8 | control: unjustified unresolved | unresolved | no, no | (none) | no | ok |\n    18\t| U03 | E | control: unsupported (15% cut) | forecast | no, no | (none) | no | ok |\n    19\t\n    20\t- **Refusals and defects.** All 6 first replies passed the schema and the code checks and were publishable. No reader marked a defect,\n    21\t  so no defect is settled or split.\n    22\t- **Writers never withheld.** 0 of 6 replies answered unresolved, including on S and E, where the new sentence invites it.\n    23\t- **One construction change, disclosed.** The registered row for K-unresolved-justified reads \"S made unresolved with the specific\n    24\t  gap\". It was first built on the third run's S Claude reply, which the current code refuses (S1, F11 \"estimated\"). It was rebuilt on\n    25\t  the third run's S Codex reply with the same reason text, and the code accepted it. The reason's five passage citations were checked\n    26\t  against the request.\n    27\t\n    28\t## 2. What split, and along which line\n    29\t\n    30\tEvery disagreement split by model family, and the four adjudications did not settle any of them: 8 of 8 adjudicator votes went with\n    31\ttheir own family. On S and E, Codex read \"supported\" as \"a passage states the size of the band-deciding assumption, or bounds the\n    32\tsame quantity\". Claude read it as \"the passages give a checkable bound that makes the rung most likely\".\n    33\t\n    34\t- **S (data-center spending of the four largest cloud companies in 2026; the rung 3/4 edge is $700B).** Both writers forecast rung 4.\n    35\t  The Claude writer took $720B of total spending at a 98% data-center share. The Codex writer took $745B minus a $25B non-data-center\n    36\t  deduction. Every reader on both sides read the same passage as decisive: e1947, \"more than $730 billion combined on AI\n    37\t  infrastructure\", for the same four companies.\n    38\t  - Claude: AI infrastructure sits inside data-center spending, so e1947 is a floor above $700B.\n    39\t  - Codex: e1947 gives no data-center split, so it does not bound the share.\n    40\t  - The answer's own stated lower bound gives $691B, in rung 3 (Codex adjudicator, U02).\n    41\t- **E (FY2028 Vera CPU revenue; management's \"more than double\" of $20B sits on the $40B edge between rungs 2 and 3).**\n    42\t  - The Codex writer realised 90% of the $40B reference. Its basis was deployment plans, a customer evaluation and a competitor's\n    43\t    benchmarks, and it said itself that \"the passages give no order sizes or shipment pace\". Both first readers, one per family,\n    44\t    said not supported, so no adjudication was needed (corrected after council round 61, which caught \"all four readers\").\n    45\t  - The Claude writer bracketed the pace between the server-CPU market pace (about 1.5x, e4687 and e4857) and the guidance (2x), then\n    46\t    took a 90% Vera share.\n    47\t    - The Codex adjudicator showed that the bracket fails under the answer's own share: 1.5 × $20B × 90% = $27B, in rung 4.\n    48\t    - The Claude adjudicator rescued it by arguing a near-full Vera share from e4691 and e4856.\n    49\t- **The justified-unresolved control.** Codex accepted the reason \"no passage gives the share\". Claude rejected it on the same e1947\n    50\t  floor, so the control's expected reading is itself contested.\n    51\t\n    52\t## 3. What the frozen gold does on the same kind of question\n    53\t\n    54\tThese are read for reasoning only. The gold questions differ from S and E in ladder and window.\n    55\t- **q04 (the same four companies' 2026 data-center spending).** Reader B took the big four's total plans of about $650B as the\n    56\t  reference, \"the figure NVIDIA management also cites\", with no deduction, and bet $650–720B.\n    57\t- **q19 (FY2027 stand-alone Vera revenue).** Reader B discounted management's $20B to $8–18B. The reasoning was the launch timing\n    58\t  (about two selling quarters) and the units the figure implies ($5,000 per CPU means four million units), against one counter-signal.\n    59\t  No passage states the discount.\n    60\t\n    61\tThe frozen ruler's own answers are reasoned bets of the kind the Claude family accepts. D741 ruling 3 (Alex) is stricter for S: the\n    62\tdeduction must be stated.\n    63\t\n    64\t## 4. What the paid re-send would meet\n    65\t\n    66\tUnder the paid gate's lines (`PAID-GATE.md` §4), a published singleton with a \"deciding gap\" stops the run. A deciding gap includes \"an\n    67\tunsized adjustment decides its band\". Where the two readers disagree, the third reader is Codex. On blind4's evidence, a published E forecast built like\n    68\tthese replies risks being marked by Codex, and the run would stop.\n    69\t- **Two corrections from council round 61, both checked:**\n    70\t  - An unresolved singleton is published, shown as unresolved (`calc_small.publish_decision`: \"held\" only when a declared relation\n    71\t    joins it to an answered member). The paid verdict inspects published singleton cards for defects (`paid_gate.py`, verdict), so it\n    72\t    is not exempt.\n    73\t  - The paid S singleton asks about CY2027 (`paid_gate.py` SINGLETONS: old-2 v6 8595). This test's S asks about CY2026 (`SMALL-DESIGN.md`\n    74\t    §5: old-3 v6 9043).\n    75\t- These votes are about support. They show a risk to the paid run, not a measured rate at which it would stop.\n    76\t\n    77\t## 5. Not done\n    78\t\n    79\tNothing was paid. Nothing was tuned on the 36 stored answers. No request text changed after the registration.\n    80\t\n    81\t## 6. The re-read under Alex's comparison ruling (D747): FAIL on a stop line\n    82\t\n    83\tRegistered in `SMALL-DESIGN.md` §5 [D747], reviewed by council round 62 (`codex-r62.md`), and scored by `blind4.py reread-tally`\n    84\t(`REREAD-TALLY.json`, `reread-reasons.json`). Packets W01–W09 are in `reread-packets/`, adjudications in `reread-adjudication/`, and\n    85\tthe key is `reread-key.json`.\n    86\t\n    87\t| Packet | Answer | First readers (Codex, Claude) | Adjudicators (Codex, Claude) | Settled | Registered line |\n    88\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n    89\t| W01 | U01 vague unresolved control | no, no | (none) | no | expects no: ok |\n    90\t| W04 | U05 E Codex, unsized 10% cut | no, no | (none) | no | expects no: ok |\n    91\t| W09 | U03 E, unsized 15% cut | no, no | (none) | no | expects no: ok |\n    92\t| W02 | U06 E Claude, 1.8x × 90% Vera share | no, no | (none) | no | must settle: ok |\n    93\t| W05 | U04 S justified unresolved | yes, yes | (none) | yes | must settle: ok |\n    94\t| W08 | U08 S Codex, $25B deduction | yes, yes | (none) | yes | must settle: ok |\n    95\t| W07 | U02 S Claude, 98% share | no, yes | no, yes | split | expects no: MISS |\n    96\t| W06 | T15 E Claude, 85% growth bracket | yes, no | yes, no | split | expects yes: MISS |\n    97\t| W03 | T10 S Claude, 95–100% share | no, yes | no, yes | split | must settle: MISS |\n    98\t\n    99\t- **The stop.** On W07 (U02), both adjudicators upheld Codex's \"part above or added into its whole\" (F12, F11, F15). The answer\n   100\t  calls F12's \"more than $730 billion\" of AI infrastructure \"the supported floor\" for data-center spending, while its own total\n   101\t  spending (F11) is $720B and its data-center outcome (F15) $705.6B. The code did not catch it, because F12 is a source figure the\n   102\t  answer uses as a floor only in its prose.\n   103\t- **The reasons behind the six settled answers were read by Claude and recorded as compatible** (`reread-reasons.json`). Each pair\n   104\t  applies the same reading. On W08, for example, both say the same four companies' AI-infrastructure figure and Meta's AI share explain\n   105\t  why a deduction under $45B is more likely, and both note that this does not measure the deduction directly.\n   106\t- **What the ruling changed.** Of the four answers that split in the targeted test, three now settle in both families, with\n   107\t  compatible reasons: U04 yes, U06 no, U08 yes. All four controls and fixed cuts read as expected.\n   108\t- **The remaining splits no longer follow model family.**\n   109\t  - Codex is the stricter reader on S (T10, U02). Claude is the stricter reader on E (T15).\n   110\t  - T15 was accepted by both families in the second re-read and now splits.\n   111\t  - Read with the third-run S Codex reply's earlier no → yes → no, this shows single readers varying on boundary answers.\n   112\t- **A process error, shared by Claude and the council.** The registration required five boundary answers to settle, or to settle as\n   113\t  expected, but nobody estimated how often single readers split on boundary answers. AGENTS §7.1 says \"a conjunction over many items\n   114\t  fails by chance\". That clause of the pass line could fail on reader variation alone. The stop on U02 does not depend on it.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'd=docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers; for f in $d/codex-r{61,61b,62,63}.md; do nl -ba \"$f\"; done; nl -ba docs/DECISIONS-log.md | sed -n '\"'11484,11526p'; rg -n 'description' poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/schema.json; ls poc/data | rg 'blind5'; rg -n 'def first_json|def build_case|CASES =|WRITERS =' \"'$d/blind_test.py'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'd=docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers; for f in $d/codex-r{61,61b,62,63}.md; do nl -ba \"$f\"; done; nl -ba docs/DECISIONS-log.md | sed -n '\"'11484,11526p'; rg -n 'description' poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/schema.json; ls poc/data | rg 'blind5'; rg -n 'def first_json|def build_case|CASES =|WRITERS =' \"'$d/blind_test.py'","aggregated_output":"     1\t**The FAIL is real. Ask Alex what evidence is enough to publish a forecast before requesting the paid re-send.** The decisive case is the $36B Vera forecast. Its writer identified a risk to sales, then assigned that risk a $4B cost. Neither reader found evidence for the amount. That one answer fails the registered test regardless of how the four disagreements are resolved. [E writer’s assumption](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/answers/codex.json:94) · [Recorded votes](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/TALLY.json:8) · [Registered pass conditions](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:375)\n     2\t\n     3\t1. **Serious — U05 deserves its settled “no.”** Management expected FY2028 CPU revenue to more than double a $20B base. The writer used $40B as its reference and forecast 90% realization. The deployment announcement supports demand. Anthropic’s evaluation and AMD’s benchmarks establish competitive risk. None gives the timing or volume of sales likely to slip. Calling $4B “less than half an average quarter” expresses the same assumption in another unit. It adds no evidence for it. At exactly $40B the answer enters the higher band, so the unsupported cut decides the result. **Wording correction:** the result report says four readers rejected U05. The record has two first readers, in agreement, and no adjudication. [E request and bands](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/user.txt:3) · [Writer’s conclusion](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/answers/codex.json:130) · [Codex reading](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/reader-packets/U05.codex.json:5) · [Claude reading](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/reader-packets/U05.claude.json:1) · [Report wording](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND4-RESULT.md:41)\n     4\t\n     5\t2. **Serious — The four splits have different merits.** I read both initial readings and both adjudications for each. All eight adjudicator votes followed their own family. My readings below leave the recorded splits intact. [Vote tally](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/TALLY.json:4)\n     6\t\n     7\t   | Reply | My reading | What decides it |\n     8\t   |---|---|---|\n     9\t   | **U02 · S forecast** | **Codex “no” on support. The range is valid.** | The writer applies 98% to $720B and gets $705.6B. Its own 96% lower share gives $691.2B. Crossing $700B is allowed: the checker actually publishes $690.9–720.3B, and the schema permits selecting an overlapping band with a reason. The weakness is the claimed independent floor. If all of the cited **more than $730B** of AI infrastructure really is data-center capex for these firms, it exceeds even the writer’s **total** spending midpoint of $720B. The writer needed to reconcile those estimates before using one to validate the other. [Writer’s reasoning](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/S/answers/claude.json:390) · [Checked range](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/CHECKED.json:62) · [Schema](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/S/schema.json:334) · [Both readings](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/adjudication/U02.md:36) · [Codex decision](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/adjudication/U02.codex.json:5) · [Claude decision](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/adjudication/U02.claude.json:1) |\n    10\t   | **U04 · S unresolved control** | **Codex “yes” is better supported.** | At $745B total, the four firms cross $700B at a data-center share of about 94%. The control names that boundary and addresses the strongest contrary passage. The $730B figure names the right four companies but measures AI infrastructure. The nearly $800B data-center figure covers five companies. Neither establishes how much of the four firms’ spending qualifies. Claude has a plausible reason to lean upward. Its claim that the first figure supplies a floor requires a relationship the passage leaves open. That makes this a defensible abstention and a poor control for an *undisputed* abstention. [Control](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/controls/K-unresolved-justified.json:1) · [Four-company passage](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/S/user.txt:29) · [Five-company passage](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/S/user.txt:128) · [Both readings](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/adjudication/U04.md:36) · [Codex decision](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/adjudication/U04.codex.json:5) · [Claude decision](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/adjudication/U04.claude.json:1) |\n    11\t   | **U06 · E forecast** | **Codex “no.” The existing definition settles the error in Claude’s justification.** | Keep the writer’s 90% Vera share and run its proposed lower growth rate. **$20B × 1.5 × 90% = $27B**, one band below the claimed $30–40B. Claude effectively substitutes a 100% share to obtain $30B. The definition requires judging the answer with the writer’s own assumptions. Claude is right on one factual detail: e4856 does connect Vera to the $20B potential. That passage still supplies neither the writer’s realized share nor a floor for NVIDIA’s growth equal to the whole market’s. The writer’s $32.4B arithmetic is correct. The reader’s claim about the whole interval is wrong. [Writer’s reasoning and sensitivity](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/answers/claude.json:162) · [Vera passage](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/user.txt:27) · [Definition](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind_rules.py:15) · [Both readings](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/adjudication/U06.md:36) · [Codex decision](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/adjudication/U06.codex.json:5) · [Claude decision](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/adjudication/U06.claude.json:1) |\n    12\t   | **U08 · S forecast** | **Codex “no” on the claimed bound. This is the closest call on the forecast itself.** | At $745B total, spending outside data centers can be at most $45B for the forecast to clear $700B. The writer deducts $25B and explicitly allows some AI infrastructure spending outside data centers. Claude’s comparison implies less than $15B **outside AI**. It leaves the amount **outside data centers** open on the writer’s own terms. Meta’s roughly 4% residual offers a concrete comparison, but the writer gives no reason the combined residual of all four firms stays below $45B. Alex’s open question bears directly on whether that comparison nevertheless supports a most-likely forecast. [Writer’s deduction](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/S/answers/codex.json:226) · [Meta passage](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/S/user.txt:45) · [Both readings](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/adjudication/U08.md:36) · [Codex decision](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/adjudication/U08.codex.json:5) · [Claude decision](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/adjudication/U08.claude.json:1) |\n    13\t\n    14\t3. **Serious — The recurring writer failure is assigning a size to a risk when that size selects the answer. The recurring reader disagreement is how much a comparison can support.** Counting each original reply once, excluding controls, gives the following. For run three I used its last authorized reading. A joint reply counts once when both forecasts were supported. [Third-run votes by reply](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/REREAD2-TALLY.json:92) · [Third-run usable replies by case](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/REREAD2-TALLY.json:198) · [Targeted-run votes](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/TALLY.json:4)\n    15\t\n    16\t   | Original replies | Settled yes | Settled no | Split |\n    17\t   |---|---:|---:|---:|\n    18\t   | Third run, 12 | 8 | 2 | 2 |\n    19\t   | Targeted run, 6 | 2 | 1 | 3 |\n    20\t   | **Total, 18** | **10** | **3** | **5** |\n    21\t\n    22\t   Across **eight S and E replies**, one had settled support, three had settled rejection, and four split. All eight forecast a number. The clearest recurrence is the E Codex writer cutting the guidance reference by 15% in run three and 10% after the request explicitly asked for evidence sizing the cut. All eight P-family replies across the runs had settled support. O accounts for the remaining yes and split, on whether $18B described funding of an existing commitment balance. The stored votes therefore establish three rejected replies, not eight writer errors. They also show reader variation: the *same* third-run S Codex answer went **no → yes → no** across its three readings. [Earlier E cut](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread2-packets/T17.codex.json:5) · [Revised request](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/system.txt:9) · [O disagreement](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread2-packets/T02.codex.json:5) · [S original vote](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/TALLY.json:494) · [First re-read](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/REREAD-TALLY.json:291) · [Second re-read](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/REREAD2-TALLY.json:123)\n    23\t\n    24\t4. **Serious — Alex settled the duty to justify an adjustment. How strong an indirect comparison must be is still his decision.** D741 requires the deduction to be stated or a supported bound given. D745 accepts an unresolved answer when its reason survives the strongest passages. The writer is also told to choose the most likely outcome and to size estimates from comparable deals, capacity, units, selling time, or price. The frozen examples illustrate the distinction. On q04, the reader used roughly $650B of plans and evidence about demand, costs, and delays to forecast $650–720B. On q19, two selling quarters and a $5,000 CPU price made the four million units implied by guidance assessable. No passage supplied the final discount. Those examples support reasoned estimates. They neither establish S’s category relationship nor provide the missing sales evidence for FY2028 Vera. [D741](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11348) · [D745](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11510) · [Writer’s sizing instructions](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/system.txt:4) · [q04 reasoning](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/judgement-gold/gold-v1.json:246) · [q19 reasoning](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/judgement-gold/gold-v1.json:897)\n    25\t\n    26\t5. **Minor — Two details change how to interpret the paid option.** Its third reader is Codex. A published singleton stops the test if a reader-confirmed unsized adjustment decides its band. The free test asked about *support*, so these votes indicate a risk, not a measured paid-stop rate. Also, paid S asks about **2027** while targeted S asks about **2026**. Finally, the report’s statement that an unresolved singleton is automatically held is inaccurate. I replayed all nine stored answers and controls in memory. Both unresolved controls returned `publish` with no numeric outcome. Such a card has no chosen band, but the paid verdict does inspect published singleton cards for defects. [Paid reading and stop rule](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/PAID-GATE.md:262) · [Paid cases](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid_gate.py:43) · [Targeted cases](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:166) · [Singleton publication rule](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_small.py:782) · [Paid verdict code](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid_gate.py:772)\n    27\t\n    28\t**One recommendation.** Put this question to Alex: **“When a related figure makes one band more likely, but the evidence still leaves the next band plausible, may we publish the forecast if we explain why the comparison applies, or should we say ‘unresolved’ until the evidence bounds the quantity on the deciding side?”** I favor allowing the forecast when the writer explains why the comparison applies to the companies, period, and quantity asked about, and why it favors that side of the cutoff. U05 fails even that reading.\n    29\t\n    30\tThat ruling costs no provider spend. A fresh read under unchanged instructions would sample the same disagreement. A code or request change now would spend engineering and review time implementing one side before Alex chooses it. Closing S and E wholesale costs both forecasts whenever the evidence later becomes sufficient. The 36-call paid re-send would measure Luna and the benefit of judging related questions together. It cannot settle what Alex wants published. The earlier plan estimated **$0.17** for first attempts at typical output length under a **$0.50** ceiling; a new send requires separate authorization. [Registered sequence](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:375) · [Paid cost assumptions](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/PAID-GATE.md:29)\n    31\t\n    32\t**One test.** After Alex rules, register one **$0 blinded contrast**. Give fresh writers the same spending question twice. One version has only the indirect comparison Alex ruled on. The other adds a clearly labeled test passage that explicitly bounds the four firms’ data-center spending above $700B and below $800B. Fix the expected decisions from Alex’s answer before showing either version. Mix in the saved U05 as the unsupported-cut control. The result moves the decision only if both reader families apply Alex’s rule to the indirect case, accept a forecast on the explicitly bounded case, and reject U05’s unsized cut. Agreement would justify returning to writer verification under a settled standard. Another split would keep the paid request closed. The original D745 FAIL stands either way.     1\t**Ask Alex whether an indirect comparison can justify publishing the more likely forecast. I lean yes. Keep the paid re-send on hold.** The $36B Vera answer shows why these are separate decisions. Its writer acknowledged that the passages gave no order size or shipment pace, then used a $4B shortfall to put sales below $40B. Both first readers rejected that answer. Resolving every disagreement about cloud spending would still leave D745 failed. [Vera answer:94](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/answers/codex.json:94) · [Recorded votes:8](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/TALLY.json:8) · [Registered pass conditions:375](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:375)\n     2\t\n     3\t1. **Agree with Claude’s revised lean. I dispute two of the reasons given for it.** The writer is asked for the most likely outcome. The frozen examples make that concrete. For q19, the price per CPU tells the reader that the target implies four million units in roughly two selling quarters. That supports a discount even though no passage supplies the final $8–18B range. For q04, the reader weighs spending plans, demand and delays. D741 still applies when a writer starts from total spending to forecast a narrower amount. It must state the deduction or give a supported bound. The open choice is whether an indirect comparison can support the stated deduction strongly enough to pick a side. Alex has not already approved that threshold. D725 does not promise that code catches every invented or mislabelled figure. And §8’s “no data to verify” concerns checking outcomes afterward. It gives no permission to publish an unsupported estimate today. [Writer instructions:1](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/system.txt:1) · [q19:897](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/judgement-gold/gold-v1.json:897) · [q04:246](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/judgement-gold/gold-v1.json:246) · [D741:11359](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11359) · [Limits of the code checks:11265](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11265) · [AGENTS.md:416](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/AGENTS.md:416)\n     4\t\n     5\t2. **Agree on asking one general question. Disagree with wording that assumes the related number already points to the answer.** The four companies said they expected to spend more than $730B on *AI infrastructure* in 2026. The question asks about *data-center capital spending*. A writer who starts from $745B of total spending has to explain why spending outside data centers is likely to be at most $45B to reach the $700B cutoff. That gives Alex a concrete choice without asking him to define “AI infrastructure” for all future cases. [Spending passages:27](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/S/user.txt:27) · [Two readings of the $45B difference:36](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/adjudication/U08.md:36)\n     6\t\n     7\t   **My question for Alex:** “资料没直接给出所问的数。用一个相关数字作比较，能解释为什么结果更可能在线的这一边，但另一边仍有可能。这时可以发布预测，还是要等到能从资料算出结果落在线的哪一边？”\n     8\t\n     9\t   - **可以发布（我推荐）。** 要讲清这个比较为什么适用于所问的公司、时间和数字，以及按答案自己的假设，为什么这一边更可能。\n    10\t   - **先说‘还判断不了’。** 等资料能直接给出或推算出所问的数在线的哪一边，再发布预测。\n    11\t\n    12\t   This covers using market growth as a comparison for one vendor. It does not make market growth a guaranteed minimum. Both readers accepted the earlier $37B Vera answer, which explained why NVIDIA might outgrow the market. The later answer added a 90% Vera share. At its lower growth estimate, $20B × 1.5 × 90% gives **$27B**. The writer itself acknowledges that sensitivity. The reader who claimed the whole range stayed at $30–40B dropped the writer’s assumption. That error is settled by arithmetic. Whether the writer gave enough reason to prefer its $32.4B forecast was still a split vote. [Earlier Vera reasoning:131](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/E/answers/claude.json:131) · [T15 votes:169](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/REREAD2-TALLY.json:169) · [Later Vera answer:162](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/answers/claude.json:162) · [Adjudicator’s claim:1](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/adjudication/U06.claude.json:1)\n    13\t\n    14\t3. **Agree with the stored-answer re-read as the one next test.** After Alex rules, keep the answers fixed and change only the shared reading instructions to include his words. The synthetic passage in round 61 would show whether a fresh writer can use an explicit bound. It would tell us little about why two readers looking at the *same* answer disagree. Claude’s list contains **eight distinct answers** because U04 is both a split and a control. I would add T15 as the real comparison for U06. That gives **nine answers, 18 independent first readings, zero new writer calls and $0 in paid sends**. T10 is there for its spending argument only. Its previously confirmed quotation error stands. [Round 61 proposal:32](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/codex-r61.md:32) · [U04 and other controls:4](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/TALLY.json:4) · [T10’s separate error:111](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND3-RESULT.md:111)\n    15\t\n    16\t   **What would move the decision:** Before reading, record what Alex’s choice implies for the examples and controls. Move on to writer repair if both families independently apply that choice to the spending forecasts and the “can’t tell” answer, reject the unsupported Vera cuts, and judge T15 and U06 using each writer’s actual assumptions. Matching votes for incompatible reasons do not settle the disagreement. A continuing split leaves the publication question unresolved in practice. Even a clean re-read cannot turn D745 into a pass. [Shared reading rule:15](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind_rules.py:15) · [D741’s sequence:11364](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11364)\n    17\t\n    18\t4. **Agree on waiting for an E repair. Disagree that a paid STOP is already predictable.** Of four Vera answers, **two were rejected by both readers, one split, and one was accepted by both**. Three contained a cut or share whose size is in question. Those four answers came from Codex and Claude. They give no measured failure rate for Luna, and the free readers voted on support while the paid readers separately mark whether a gap decided the band. Paid S also asks about **2027**. The stored S answers concern **2026**. [Earlier Vera votes:169](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/REREAD2-TALLY.json:169) · [Later votes:8](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/TALLY.json:8) · [Paid cases:15](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/PAID-GATE.md:15) · [Free cases:169](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:169)\n    19\t\n    20\t   **The attainable paid verdict is conditional.** On a complete run, one confirmed deciding gap in any of the three published Vera answers forces **STOP**, even if joint judging wins all nine pairs. Codex breaks a reader tie. With zero published defects across all joint and singleton answers, **PASS** remains possible with **8 joint wins and at most 5 separate wins**, or **9 joint wins and at most 6 separate wins**, with at least two usable joint pairs per snapshot. A properly explained “can’t tell” is published for a singleton and is still inspected. So the 36 calls could measure Luna and the benefit of judging related questions together. The estimated $0.17 cost and proposed $0.50 ceiling do not override the requirement for a clean free test before requesting authorization. [Paid reading and verdict:262](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/PAID-GATE.md:262) · [Verdict calculation:772](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid_gate.py:772) · [Singleton publication:782](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_small.py:782) · [Cost estimate:32](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/PAID-GATE.md:32) · [Authorization condition:380](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:380)\n    21\t\n    22\t   **The smallest writer change I would take forward after the ruling** is to make the writer choose between the existing “forecast” and “can’t tell” answers *before* it assigns a discount. At that choice, it must identify what makes one side more likely under its own assumptions. If it can explain only why sales might slip, it can report that gap directly. This is a proposed repair. D742 addressed a different problem involving quarterly guidance. D745 is the round that directly tried and failed to fix unsupported discounts. Automatically publishing management’s number whenever it sits on the cutoff would change the product and would require Alex’s separate decision. [Existing answer choices:287](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/schema.json:287) · [D742:11397](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11397) · [D745:11513](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11513) · [Recurring failures:85](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/AGENTS.md:85)     1\t**Run with the listed changes.** The current tally can declare agreement when the readers give opposite explanations. I verified that with invented votes in memory. No file was edited, and no D747 packet was built or read.\n     2\t\n     3\t1. **Serious — Alex’s new sentence is faithful, but the surrounding instructions still permit the old reading.** Alex allowed a forecast when the writer explains why the comparison fits the companies, period and quantity, and why its own assumptions favor one side. The other side can remain possible. The new sentence says that accurately. The preceding sentence still says the passages *must* support the size or a bound. The first-reader brief offers the comparison as an alternative, while adjudicators receive the two sentences without that clarification. Make the shared definition say explicitly that an explained comparison can satisfy the support requirement. D741 still requires a writer using a total for a narrower amount to state its deduction or give a supported bound. D745’s unresolved clause is still right. A reader should judge the gap the writer identifies after considering the strongest evidence. The mere availability of a defensible forecast does not disqualify a defensible “can’t tell.” [Alex’s question and chosen option:11575](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11575) · [D741:11359](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11359) · [D745:11515](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11515) · [Shared definition:15](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind_rules.py:15) · [First-reader brief:241](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind4.py:241) · [Adjudicator brief:23](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind_adjudicate.py:23)\n     4\t\n     5\t2. **Minor — Keep the four fixed expectations.** Each has a reason in the original answer and request.\n     6\t\n     7\t   | Answer | Expected | Decisive evidence |\n     8\t   |---|---|---|\n     9\t   | U01 | **No** | “The outlook for the second half is uncertain” never says what prevents a forecast of annual data-center revenue. The request includes reported results and next-quarter guidance. [Answer:1](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/controls/K-unresolved-unjustified.json:1) · [Request:25](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/P-v8/user.txt:25) |\n    10\t   | U03 | **No** | The writer reduces the $20B starting figure to $17B and doubles it to $34B. Customer interest and competition explain why sales might miss. The answer gives no comparison that explains the 15% cut that puts it below $40B. [Original answer:178](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/E/answers/codex.json:178) · [Request:27](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/E/user.txt:27) |\n    11\t   | U05 | **No** | The writer takes 90% of $40B and acknowledges that it has neither order sizes nor shipment pace. Calling the $4B shortfall “less than half an average quarter” restates the estimate without supporting it. [Answer:94](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/answers/codex.json:94) · [Request:33](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/user.txt:33) |\n    12\t   | T15 | **Yes** | The writer explains why announced adoption favors growth above the roughly 50% market pace and why execution risk favors growth below management’s doubling. On its stated Vera base, 85% growth gives $37B. This supports the selected band without treating market growth as a guaranteed floor. [Answer:131](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/E/answers/claude.json:131) · [Request:13](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/E/user.txt:13) · [Vera-specific guidance:27](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/E/user.txt:27) |\n    13\t\n    14\t3. **Serious — I would fix U02 at “no,” retain U04, and register what the other readings must get right.** This is my recommendation before the read, not an outcome from it. [Current expectations:398](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:398)\n    15\t\n    16\t   | Answer | Recommendation | Why |\n    17\t   |---|---|---|\n    18\t   | U02 | **Fix at no** | The writer explicitly calls more than $730B a *floor* for data-center spending, then forecasts $705.6B of data-center spending and $720B of total spending. All three numbers happen to point to the same band. They cannot all hold under the writer’s own explanation. Calling the $730B figure a directional comparison would repair the answer on the writer’s behalf. [Answer:390](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/S/answers/claude.json:390) · [What the $730B measures:29](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/S/user.txt:29) |\n    19\t   | U04 | **Keep open. I lean yes.** | It identifies the roughly 94% share needed to cross $700B and explains the mismatch between four firms’ AI spending and five firms’ data-center spending. It also retains the original writer’s 90% estimate and $670.5B calculation after the control removed their links to the answer. A reader can reasonably question that tension. Keep it as the disputed abstention, with no fixed vote. [Stored control:1](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/controls/K-unresolved-justified.json:1) · [How it was constructed:96](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind4.py:96) · [Five-firm passage:128](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/S/user.txt:128) |\n    20\t   | U08 | **Keep open** | At $745B of total spending, up to $45B can fall outside data centers before the forecast drops below $700B. The writer deducts $25B, uses Meta as a comparison and acknowledges that some AI spending may fall outside data centers. Whether that makes a deduction below $45B more likely is precisely the judgment Alex authorized. [Answer:226](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/S/answers/codex.json:226) · [Meta passage:45](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/S/user.txt:45) |\n    21\t   | T10 | **Keep open for the spending argument** | Its $679–745B range crosses $700B. The writer cites raised plans and higher tracking estimates to favor the upper portion. Readers should decide whether those comparisons support the 95–100% data-center share. Its range crossing the line is permitted, and its old quotation defect is already settled. [Answer:276](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/S/answers/claude.json:276) · [Four-firm passage:83](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/S/user.txt:83) · [Settled defect:11491](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11491) |\n    22\t   | U06 | **Keep open, with the arithmetic fixed** | At the writer’s 90% Vera share, its lower growth comparison gives $20B × 1.5 × 90% = **$27B**, below the selected band. Its preferred 1.8× gives **$32.4B**, inside it. The writer acknowledges the sensitivity. Readers may disagree about which outcome it justified. A “yes” based on the whole range staying above $30B cannot count as applying Alex’s rule. [Answer:162](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/answers/claude.json:162) · [Bands:3](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/user.txt:3) |\n    23\t\n    24\t4. **Serious — Two older answers get the wrong format rule, and U03 gets the wrong original request.** The shuffle assigns nine fresh W identifiers and keeps the key outside the packets. For T10 and T15, both the initial and adjudication builders correctly select the third run’s system text, user text and schema descriptions. But both append *today’s* list of allowed qualifiers. I checked both stored schemas. Neither allowed `estimated`. T10’s source says “an estimated $750 billion,” exactly where that difference mattered. Read the allowed values from the stored schema in `case_dir`. Separately, I verified that U03 is the unchanged third-run E answer. The builder pairs it with the fourth run’s system text, which added the very instruction about sizing discounts under examination. Use the third-run request for U03. Describe U01 and U04 as constructed controls evaluated under their designated request. **Minor blinding issue:** all three controls are displayed as single-line JSON while the original replies are multiline. Give all nine the same display formatting while preserving their content. [Shuffle and packet assembly:256](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind4.py:256) · [Adjudication paths:64](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind_adjudicate.py:64) · [D747 dispatch:119](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind_adjudicate.py:119) · [Qualifier source:60](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind_rules.py:60) · [Old allowed values:180](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/S/schema.json:180) · [Control provenance:104](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind4.py:104) · [Changed writer instruction:353](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:353) · [Control formatting:132](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind4.py:132)\n    25\t\n    26\t5. **Serious — Make the final verdict depend on the registered reading of the reasons.** I exercised the tally in memory with all nine labels, matching votes, and mutually incompatible reasons about whether AI spending supplies a floor. It returned **pass**. An empty key also returned **pass**. The ordinary vote rules otherwise match the registration. A settled wrong fixed vote fails, a continuing support split is inconclusive unless another case already fails, and a confirmed writer defect stops the run. T10 and the three controls are correctly excluded from defect stops. A one-reader defect on those excluded items can still delay completion by requesting adjudication. [Registered reason check and stops:407](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:407) · [Vote handling:288](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind4.py:288) · [Adjudication and exclusions:317](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind4.py:317)\n    27\t\n    28\t   Require the exact nine answers and both valid readings per answer. Keep the vote tally provisional until Claude records whether the deciding reasons are compatible and apply Alex’s rule, including after adjudication. Two readers who both insist on a guaranteed floor have made the same mistake. Give them the opportunity to settle a disagreement in reasons even when their votes match. If it persists, record **inconclusive**. The decisive pre-run check is that nine matching votes with opposing explanations cannot produce a final pass. [Round 61b decision condition:16](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/codex-r61b.md:16) · [Current final verdict:328](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind4.py:328)\n    29\t\n    30\t**Verdict — run with the listed changes.** A clean result can move the council to the E writer repair. A continuing disagreement should return to council with the exact passage and the two reasons. D745’s failure stands either way, and the paid re-send remains on hold until the writer repair passes its own free test. [Decision sequence:11560](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11560)     1\t**The FAIL is real.** W07 forecasts $705.6B of data-center spending, puts total spending at $720B, and calls **more than $730B** the floor for data centers. Both adjudicators upheld that contradiction. The three split votes affect what the council can conclude about reader agreement. They do not affect the stop. [W07 answer:390](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/S/answers/claude.json:390) · [Recorded stop:30](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/REREAD-TALLY.json:30)\n     2\t\n     3\t1. **Serious — The arithmetic passed because the writer used the $730B figure in two different ways.** The source says the four companies indicated more than $730B of **AI infrastructure** spending. The writer recorded that figure correctly and cited it to justify a 98% share. The calculation then took 98% of total spending. The word *floor* appears in the explanation, where it makes a stronger claim about a different quantity. Even the top of the code’s recomputed data-center range, $720.3B, falls below that floor. The request declares no part–whole relation for this single question, so the code has no pair to compare. **Merely linking the source figure would not fix this.** It is already linked to the 98% estimate. What is missing is the claim that it imposes a minimum on the outcome. D738 deliberately leaves that interpretation to readers. [Source and request:29](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/S/user.txt:29) · [Declared relations:5](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/S/user.txt:5) · [Existing link:264](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/S/answers/claude.json:264) · [Code comparison:655](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_small.py:655) · [Published range:62](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/CHECKED.json:62) · [D738:11265](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11265)\n     4\t\n     5\t   I compared the figures and explanations in all **18 original writer replies**. I found **0 of 12** in the third run and **1 of 6** in the targeted test with an explicit floor for a part above the writer’s own total. W08 is the closest counterexample. It uses the same $730B source, forecasts $720B out of $745B, and expressly allows some AI infrastructure spending to fall outside data centers. The third run also has a wrong $138B quarterly example in prose, a different arithmetic error. The narrow defect occurred once in this set. These replies cannot establish its production frequency. [Third-run replies:487](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/TALLY.json:487) · [Targeted replies:5](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/TALLY.json:5) · [W08’s distinction:226](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/S/answers/codex.json:226) · [Quarterly example:390](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/P/answers/claude.json:390)\n     6\t\n     7\t2. **Serious — The three splits have different explanations.** Alex allowed a related figure when the writer explains why it fits the companies, period and quantity, and why the writer’s assumptions favor the chosen side. The other side may remain possible. My readings are as follows. [D747:11582](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11582)\n     8\t\n     9\t   | Answer | My reading | What the earlier readings establish |\n    10\t   |---|---|---|\n    11\t   | **T10, four-company spending** | **No. Codex applies D747 better.** At $730B total, crossing $700B requires about 95.9% to go to data centers. The $750B estimates do not identify the four companies. The nearly $800B estimate covers five. The writer calls these “the same totals” and treats the passage naming the four as a repeat. Higher tracking estimates help explain *total* spending. The midpoint of a range built from the assumed share does little to explain the share itself. | Spending support was initially accepted, then split in the same direction in two earlier readings and again here. That is a recurring disagreement about company and spending coverage. T10’s earlier quotation defect was excluded from this vote. [Writer:245](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/S/answers/claude.json:245) · [First result:487](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/TALLY.json:487) · [First re-read:282](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/REREAD-TALLY.json:282) · [Second re-read:144](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/REREAD2-TALLY.json:144) · [Current disagreement:5](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/reread-adjudication/W03.codex.json:5) |\n    12\t   | **T15, Vera revenue** | **Yes on the $30–40B band. Codex applies D747 better, narrowly.** On the writer’s stated $20B Vera base, market-rate growth gives about $30B and doubling gives $40B. The writer explains why broad adoption favors gaining share and why deployment still under evaluation and competing products temper the doubling forecast. Claude identifies a real weakness. A market average through 2030 says little by itself about one product in FY2028. The writer supplies a reason for using the comparison, though, and D747 permits that judgment. The evidence does not establish 85% growth to that precision. | This answer was accepted in all three earlier readings, including by both families in the last one. It is the strongest evidence of reader variation. The instructions also changed, so the reversal cannot measure a random disagreement rate. [Writer:131](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/E/answers/claude.json:131) · [Earlier results:500](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/TALLY.json:500) · [First re-read:337](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/REREAD-TALLY.json:337) · [Second re-read:169](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/REREAD2-TALLY.json:169) · [Claude’s objection:1](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/reread-adjudication/W06.claude.json:1) |\n    13\t   | **U02, support for the $705.6B forecast** | **No. Codex applies D747 better.** Claude upheld the impossible floor but still counted it toward support because both dollar amounts landed in the same band. D747 asks whether the explanation works under the writer’s *own* assumptions. Removing the floor would leave a different argument. At $720B total, 98% clears the line and the writer’s own 96% gives $691.2B. | The same answer already split Codex no, Claude yes, at both levels. The new finding is that both adjudicators now recognize the contradiction. The support vote stayed split. [Earlier votes:5](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/TALLY.json:5) · [Claude’s yes and upheld defect:1](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/reread-adjudication/W07.claude.json:1) · [Codex’s reasoning:5](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/reread-adjudication/W07.codex.json:5) |\n    14\t\n    15\t3. **Minor — The registration demanded more agreement than the evidence justified.** Six of nine answers settled. All three controls came out as expected. Three of the four earlier targeted-test splits settled, which is genuine progress. All **six** adjudicator support votes on the three remaining splits still followed their own family’s first vote. Which family was stricter changed with the question. T10 and T15 expose judgment about the words *explains why* and *more likely*. U02 exposes a more specific difference between accepting a plausible band and accepting the writer’s stated route to it. D747 already gives a reason to reject U02. I see no missing general clause that another read of these answers would settle. Equally, the record does not support Claude’s conclusion that all three splits are random reader variation. Requiring every disputed answer to settle was a demanding, uncalibrated test of agreement. Its registered outcome still stands. [Votes:4](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/REREAD-TALLY.json:4) · [Registered conditions:428](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:428) · [Claude’s conclusion:9](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/claude-r63.md:9)\n    16\t\n    17\t4. **Serious — The weakest proposal is to pass a writer test on stable controls and confirmed defects alone.** The product question is whether a **fresh E answer** invents the adjustment that moves Vera below $40B. U05 shows why that outcome must count. Both readers rejected its $36B forecast, yet the test recorded zero named defects. Claude’s proposed scoring could let a repeat through. A second weakness is the proposed code change. W08 shows why an indirect comparison must be allowed to influence a forecast without becoming a binding minimum. One contradictory floor warrants a case in the test. It does not establish a need to change the calculation format. Finally, D746 made writer repair conditional on agreement, and the registration sends persistent splits back to Alex. Moving forward now needs his decision. [U05 and empty defect list:8](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/TALLY.json:8) · [Claude’s proposed scoring:44](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/claude-r63.md:44) · [D746 condition:11564](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11564) · [Registered split outcome:434](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:434)\n    18\t\n    19\t**One recommendation.** Ask Alex to authorize the E writer repair despite the three disclosed splits. Have the writer decide whether the evidence favors one side of $40B **before** choosing a discount. If it can explain only why sales *might* fall short, it should say what remains unknown. Carry W07 into verification and defer a change to the calculation format. Another vote on the same nine answers would leave the writer untouched. The paid re-send stays on hold. [D746’s proposed repair:11566](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11566)\n    20\t\n    21\t**One test.** After approval and council review of the changed request, run one $0 blind test with fresh Codex and Claude writers on E, S and the previously supported data-center revenue question, P-v8. Mix in saved U05 and W07 to verify readers catch an unsupported cut and an impossible floor. To move forward, **each fresh E and S answer must earn agreement for its forecast or its specific reason for saying “can’t tell.”** Both P-v8 answers must still produce supported forecasts. The code must accept the fresh replies, and no confirmed defect may survive. A settled unsupported answer or contradictory floor fails. A persistent support split is inconclusive and leaves spending on hold. Only a clean result supports asking Alex to authorize the paid re-send. [Existing writer-test conditions:375](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:375) 11484\t## D745 · 2026-11-23 · The D744 re-read calibrates the readers but stops on a qualifier-list gap and on S's unsupported share; council 59 recommends a targeted $0 test of one clarification, for Alex to authorize\n 11485\t\n 11486\t- **The result** (`docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND3-RESULT.md` §5; `REREAD2-TALLY.json`):\n 11487\t  - **The readers are calibrated.**\n 11488\t    - All five controls passed: midpoint, 108.5 misread, overlap, disjoint, unsupported assumption.\n 11489\t    - No defect was marked by both readers on the 12 replies.\n 11490\t  - **STOP on two lines, as written:**\n 11491\t    1. T10 (S, Claude writer) F11: \"an estimated $750 billion\" recorded with qualifier \"none\". Both adjudicators upheld it. \"estimated\" is\n 11492\t       not in the closed qualifier list, so the format forced \"none\". This is 1 affected figure of 251 source figures in the three writer\n 11493\t       runs.\n 11494\t    2. S has zero usable replies. Both writers state a data-center share of total capex (90%; 95–100%) that the passages do not size, and\n 11495\t       the share decides the band.\n 11496\t  - **E's Codex reply** is now \"not supported\": its 15% cut from the guided floor decides the band. This is the same weakness.\n 11497\t- **Council round 59** (`codex-r59.md`) chose a targeted $0 test, (c), over Claude's lean to go to Alex for the paid re-send, (b):\n 11498\t  - Going straight to the paid re-send would measure the known weakness in gpt-6-luna without the request saying what a supported size\n 11499\t    is.\n 11500\t  - Skipping a clean result needs Alex to change the registered sequence anyway.\n 11501\t  - Claude agrees: the weakness is unsupported precision in a number that decides the rung, which is the goal's own failure (D725).\n 11502\t- **Proposed test (for Alex to authorize):**\n 11503\t  1. Add \"estimated\" to the qualifier list, and show readers the allowed values. Prove it on the saved replies, with a case and a\n 11504\t     mutant.\n 11505\t  2. Add one clarification to the existing instruction: when a deduction or cut decides the band, show the evidence for its size or a\n 11506\t     bound the passages support that justifies the band; if neither, answer unresolved.\n 11507\t  3. Test on S and on one stored case where the passages do support a narrower figure.\n 11508\t  4. Before the test, Alex rules whether a justified unresolved answer counts as usable.\n 11509\t  - Falsifier: a fresh S answer still picks a band on an unsupported share, or the supported case is withheld.\n 11510\t- **Alex's answers** (2026-11-23):\n 11511\t  - to \"怎么走？\": \"批准针对性小测 (推荐)\". The other options were \"不小测，直接议花钱重发\" and \"先停在这里\".\n 11512\t  - to whether a justified \"无法判断\" counts: \"算合格 (推荐)\". The other option was \"不算\".\n 11513\t- **Decided (Alex):**\n 11514\t  1. Run the targeted $0 test.\n 11515\t  2. An unresolved answer whose reason shows the passages cannot settle the rung counts as a usable answer.\n 11516\t- **Council round 60** (`codex-r60.md`) set the wording and the registration (`SMALL-DESIGN.md` §5 [D745]):\n 11517\t  - Rung-deciding shares, deductions and discounts must show their passage support or a justifying bound, else unresolved.\n 11518\t  - The default discount is made subject to that rule.\n 11519\t  - The three controls are the saved E 15% cut, a vague P-v8 unresolved answer and a justified S unresolved answer (expected no, no,\n 11520\t    yes).\n 11521\t  - Pass: every S and E reply settled yes on a forecast or its reason; both P-v8 replies settled yes on a forecast. A support split\n 11522\t    is inconclusive.\n 11523\t\n 11524\t## D746 · 2026-11-23 · The D745 targeted test fails on E's unsized cut; S splits on how far an indirect comparison can support a forecast; council 61–61b: one general question to Alex, then a $0 stored-answer re-read; the paid re-send stays on hold\n 11525\t\n 11526\t- **The result** (`docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND4-RESULT.md`; run `poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/`,\n11:      \"description\": \"Short and unique, such as F1; never a passage id.\"\n21:        \"description\": \"The quantity, such as revenue or capital expenditure; never its basis.\"\n28:        \"description\": \"For example FY2027, FY2027 Q3, FY2027 H1, FY2027 Q1-Q3, CY2026, end of FY2027.\"\n38:      \"description\": \"What the number measures. Where the request lists keys (outcome keys, shared quantities), copy them exactly.\"\n47:      \"description\": \"source: a number written in a shown passage's quote; computed: one operation on other figures; estimate: your judgement, with its assumption in basis_text.\"\n58:      \"description\": \"reported: an actual; guidance: the company's outlook; consensus: analysts' average; third_party: one outside party; forecast: this family's own forecast, on a computed or estimate figure only.\"\n64:        \"description\": \"A point. Give value, or low and high, never both.\"\n75:        \"description\": \"A range, low at most high, with value null.\"\n86:        \"description\": \"A range, low at most high, with value null.\"\n100:      \"description\": \"USD bn: money in billions of US dollars ($1.2 trillion = 1200; $500 million = 0.5). %: a percentage as its number (7.5% = 7.5). x: a multiple (double = 2).\"\n123:        \"description\": \"Source only: the passage whose quote holds the number.\"\n134:        \"description\": \"Source only: a complete number exactly as the quote writes it, never a piece of a longer number, range or 'plus or minus' expression, with its currency and scale or its percent sign, and nothing else: '$4.5 billion', 'US$300 million', '$2.1bn', '40%', '-3%', 'double', 'twice'. The value is the number as written (a minus only when the quote writes one). A written range in full ('$3–4 billion', 'between $3 billion and $4 billion', '$3 billion to $4 billion'), given as low and high; a change written 'from $3 billion to $5 billion' is two numbers, each copied alone. '$4.5 billion, plus or minus 3%' in full, given as low and high or its midpoint as value. Only US dollars.\"\n166:        \"description\": \"Source only: the qualifier the quote writes just before the number (or 'or more' / 'or less' just after); none when there is none.\"\n180:        \"description\": \"Computed only: ids of figures, never passages, in the operation's order.\"\n200:        \"description\": \"Computed only. sum(a, b, …) = a + b + … (one unit); difference(a, b) = a − b (one unit); product(a, b, …) = a × b × … (USD bn × x, x × x or % × x; never USD bn × %); ratio(a, b) = a ÷ b (same units give x; a ÷ x keeps a's unit); share(whole, percent) = whole × percent ÷ 100, the percent from 0 to 100; growth(old, new) = the % change, in one unit; apply_growth(base, rate %) = base × (1 + rate ÷ 100). sum and product take two or more inputs; the others exactly two. Never divide by a figure that can be zero. A range input gives a range: compute both extremes, a figure used twice taking one value; you may declare the midpoint as value. The figure equals the result within 0.5% (at least 0.1, or 0.05 for %). A sum never adds a figure of the part's quantity to one of the whole's for the same period (the whole already includes the part); a difference may take the part from the whole of the same period only to give the rest. A sum never adds one quantity for the same period twice. When a sum or difference combines periods of one quantity, write them FY2027, FY2027 Q3, FY2027 H1 or FY2027 Q1-Q3 (or CY likewise), and they make up the output's period exactly (Q1 + Q2 = H1; FY − Q1-Q3 = Q4).\"\n211:        \"description\": \"Estimate only: the assumption, in words.\"\n225:        \"description\": \"Estimate only: the ids of the figures and passages it rests on.\"\n240:      \"description\": \"The members whose forecast this figure is: your own forecast (a computed or estimate figure with basis forecast, even when it agrees with consensus), exactly one for each member answered as a forecast, none for an unresolved one. Its what is the member's outcome key, in USD bn.\"\n250:      \"description\": \"The members for which this figure is the market's reference. It and every figure beneath it is a source or a computed figure, never an estimate or a forecast, and at least one of them is a quoted consensus, guidance or outside figure. A past period's reported share or multiple carried to another period (a quarter's mix applied to the year) makes it our comparison estimate, not the market's reference.\"\n274:   \"description\": \"The family's calculation. Use one forecast figure for each quantity and period, as a point or a range, wherever that quantity is needed (outside figures beneath it, or a point a final range rests on, are fine). Guidance may inform a growth pace: record the pace you forecast as an estimate, say why it applies, name the benchmark in rests_on, and use that estimate in the calculation. A rate computed from a shared quantity's guidance or outside figure is never itself an input of the calculation. To turn a total into a narrower quantity (data-center spending out of total capital expenditure), write the deduction or share as its own figure, or give a supported bound; never relabel a total as the narrower quantity. Figures name each other by id; no figure may depend on itself.\"\n293:      \"description\": \"forecast: a figure marked in outcome_for, a rung, and a reference (a figure marked in reference_for, or reference_unavailable). unresolved: why; rung and reference_unavailable null; no figure marked for it.\"\n299:        \"description\": \"Forecast only: the rung whose band holds the outcome as the code recomputes it (a band includes its lower edge, not its upper); for a range outcome, a band it overlaps, with the reason in reasons.\"\n310:        \"description\": \"Forecast only, when no figure is marked as its reference: why.\"\n324:        \"description\": \"Unresolved only: why the evidence cannot support a forecast.\"\n26:CASES = {   # registered in SMALL-DESIGN.md §5: archive, calls, member keys\n64:def build_case(name, spec):\n105:WRITERS = ('claude', 'codex')\n109:def first_json(text):\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_16","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'for d in poc/data/judging-v13s-blind{3-2026-11-22,4-2026-11-23}; do for c in E S P-v8; do for f in $d/$c/answers/*.json; do echo \"$f\"; rg -n '\"'\\\"reasons\\\"|\"'^ *\"why\"'\"' \\\"\"'$f\" | cut -c1-1550 | tail -2; done; done; done; d=docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers; nl -ba $d/blind_test.py | sed -n '\"'24,125p'; nl -ba \"'$d/blind_reread2.py | sed -n '\"'18,73p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_16","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'for d in poc/data/judging-v13s-blind{3-2026-11-22,4-2026-11-23}; do for c in E S P-v8; do for f in $d/$c/answers/*.json; do echo \"$f\"; rg -n '\"'\\\"reasons\\\"|\"'^ *\"why\"'\"' \\\"\"'$f\" | cut -c1-1550 | tail -2; done; done; done; d=docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers; nl -ba $d/blind_test.py | sed -n '\"'24,125p'; nl -ba \"'$d/blind_reread2.py | sed -n '\"'18,73p'\"","aggregated_output":"poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/E/answers/claude.json\n162:      \"reasons\": \"Reference: NVIDIA's own guidance. CFO Colette Kress reaffirmed about $20 billion of CPU revenue for FY2027 (F1, e3660) and said FY2028 would more than double it (F2, e4856); NVIDIA's wording ties both the base and the doubling to Vera CPU revenue (e4691, e4856). That gives more than $40 billion (F3), the bottom edge of rung 2 (Bullish, $40-50 billion). Forecast: this is a target for a full fiscal year that has not yet started, for a product line still ramping, so it is discounted for time and execution rather than taken in full; the whole of FY2028 is still to be sold. Outside benchmarks put CPU market growth near 50% a year (AMD's server CPU estimate, F4; Bank of America's path from $35 billion to $170 billion, F5 and F6), roughly half the pace NVIDIA needs, so a doubling requires large share gains. Adoption breadth supports a pace well above the market: Kress says every major hyperscaler, neocloud, AI lab and system OEM will deploy Vera (e4689), Anthropic is evaluating it for CPU-heavy agentic work (e5044), and Arm-based servers already hold a large share of data-center revenue (e4692). Against that, AMD's own benchmarks claim its Venice and EPYC 9996 parts beat Vera per core, per platform, per rack and in memory bandwidth (e5399, e5400, e5401, e5402, e5404); these are a competitor's marketing tests from one article and are weighed lightly, but they show standalone CPU share will be contested. The CEO's and other outlets' repeats of the doubling (e4764, e4691, e4856) add no new fact. The 2027 hypersca\n163:      \"why\": null,\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/E/answers/codex.json\n178:      \"reasons\": \"Management's reference starts at $40 billion, the lower edge of rung 2. Its more-than-double language points above that threshold without specifying by how much. My forecast is $34 billion, in rung 3. I estimate $17 billion of FY2027 Vera revenue from the $20 billion CPU outlook and expect Vera revenue to double as deployments broaden. NVIDIA expects broad deployment, and Anthropic's evaluation is a concrete sign of interest. Neither statement gives committed purchase volumes. AMD's own tests suggest that Vera faces competition for CPU-intensive workloads, with an uncertain effect on sales. The hyperscaler spending outlook, especially the updated calendar 2027 assessment, supports the ramp, but gives no Vera allocation.\",\n179:      \"why\": null,\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/S/answers/claude.json\n276:      \"reasons\": \"Rung 4 (at least $700bn and below $800bn). Reference: outside tallies of the four companies' own 2026 guidance put their combined capital expenditure at roughly $720-745bn (F8, e5014), which sits in rung 4. Build: Alphabet guides $195-205bn (raised from $180-190bn, e1254), Meta $125-145bn (raised, e1549) and Amazon $220bn (raised from $200bn, mostly on memory costs, e1649), $540-570bn together. Microsoft gives no calendar-2026 total in view: its January-March 2026 capex was $30.88bn, up 84% (e922), and its $678bn Azure backlog (e4405) supports a continued ramp, so I estimate about $175bn (F6), between a run-rate build of about $155bn and the roughly $175-190bn the outside tallies imply after the other three companies' guidance. Combined capital expenditure: $715-745bn (F7). Data-center part: almost all of this is servers, networking, buildings and leases for data centers, and outside sources describe the same totals as data-center spending (about $750bn, e1416, e4082; NVIDIA's nearly $800bn data-center figure for the top five, e2759 and e5376, of which these four are the bulk). I take a 95-100% share (F13, my estimate), giving $679-745bn, midpoint about $712bn (F14). The range reaches rung 3 only at its low end, which would need a non-data-center share no source indicates; the midpoint and most of the range lie in rung 4. Upward pressure: every company raised its guidance this year, memory costs keep rising (e4567, e4566), and independent trackers sit at $745-760bn (e1856, e2458); this keeps the outcome\n277:      \"why\": null,\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/S/answers/codex.json\n257:      \"reasons\": \"I forecast $670.5 billion in CY2026 data-center capex, which falls in rung 3. The closest market benchmark by company coverage is Zacks's $720 billion to $745 billion estimate for all capex, numerically in rung 4. The company spending revisions and Microsoft's reported spending growth support $745 billion of total capex. Project delays and grid constraints temper the pace, and the additional AWS GPU deployment is scheduled after CY2026. Applying my 90% data-center share to the total puts the forecast below $700 billion. The spending mix is the biggest uncertainty near that cutoff.\",\n258:      \"why\": null,\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/P-v8/answers/claude.json\n447:      \"reasons\": \"Reference: the only outside figure for this exact quantity is Morningstar's FY2027 Data Center estimate of $361 billion (F19, e2295), which sits in rung 2 ($350-400 billion); it was published before the Q2 report. For the whole company, analysts expect $411 billion of FY2027 revenue (F23); my company forecast is $418.2 billion (F22), slightly above, in line with NVIDIA's habit of beating guidance. Calculation: reported Data Center revenue was $75.25 billion in Q1 and $89.0 billion in Q2, so $164.25 billion in H1 (F3). Q3 company revenue is guided at $108.0 billion plus or minus 2%; NVIDIA beat the midpoint by 5.7% in Q2 and 4.6% in Q4 FY2026 and has beaten the high end for 12 straight quarters, so I forecast a 5% beat, $113.4 billion (F11, F12, my estimate). For Q4 I keep the guided 12% sequential pace (F13, my estimate): hyperscale growth is set to reaccelerate as Vera Rubin supply builds and supply commitments rose to $279 billion, but supply covers only about 70% of demand and stays tight through FY2028; that gives $127.0 billion (F14). Data Center was 92.5% of Q2 revenue and its share has risen every quarter; I assume 92.8% for H2 (F16), so H2 Data Center revenue is $223.1 billion (F17) and FY2027 Data Center revenue is $387.4 billion (F18), rung 2, the same rung as the reference but well above it, because Q2 and the Q3 guidance came in after the reference. Boundaries: falling below $350 billion would need H2 Data Center revenue under $185.8 billion, below the Q2 pace, although the Q3 guidance alone\n448:      \"why\": null,\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/P-v8/answers/codex.json\n154:      \"reasons\": \"I forecast about $388 billion of FY2027 Data Center revenue, in rung 2. Morningstar's $361 billion segment estimate is the direct outside reference and also falls in rung 2. The reported first half contributes $164.27 billion. I forecast about $104 billion in Q3 and $120 billion in Q4. NVIDIA's Q3 company guidance anchors the pace. The prior guidance beat and initial Rubin shipments support a modest beat, and the live CoreWeave deployment and management's expectation of faster hyperscaler growth support a further Q4 step-up. Memory constraints and long delivery lead times limit how much of that demand can ship by year-end, keeping the forecast below $400 billion. The calendar 2026 spending theme supports demand this fiscal year. The higher calendar 2027 spending theme offers some late-year upside, with most of the spending window falling after NVIDIA's fiscal close. Possible H200 authorization adds limited Q4 upside because authorization may come late and shipment timing is uncertain. The other export-policy themes leave near-term shipment assumptions unchanged.\",\n155:      \"why\": null,\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/answers/claude.json\n162:      \"reasons\": \"Rung 3 (Neutral, $30-40 billion). I forecast about $32 billion of Vera CPU revenue in FY2028 (F8). Reference: NVIDIA's own outlook. CFO Colette Kress reaffirmed about $20 billion of CPU revenue in FY2027 and said FY2028 should more than double it (e3660). Huang and other outlets carry the same claim (e4764, e4691, e4856), counted once. That puts the reference above $40 billion (F3), at rung 2. No consensus figure is supplied. How I got the outcome: the FY2027 base is current-year guidance, so I take it in full (F1). The FY2028 doubling is a full-year target for the next fiscal year. All four selling quarters are still ahead, and it depends on a Vera ramp that the evidence does not yet show in volume: Anthropic is still evaluating Vera (e5044), and deployments are described in the future tense (e4689). So the target is discounted for time and execution rather than taken as the answer. Bounds on the growth pace: a vendor that only held share in a server-CPU market growing about 50% a year (AMD, e4687; BofA's fivefold growth from 2026 to 2030 is a similar pace, e4857) would grow about 1.5x, giving $30 billion. The guidance floor is 2.0x, giving $40 billion. Broad deployment claims (e4689) and Arm's 45% revenue share of data-center servers (e4692) support gaining share above the market pace. AMD's benchmark claims that its EPYC parts beat Vera by 1.2x per core up to 3.4x per modeled rack (e5399-e5404; a competitor's own tests, weighed lightly as one signal) argue against taking the guidance in full. I forec\n163:      \"why\": null,\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/answers/codex.json\n130:      \"reasons\": \"Management's doubling outlook gives a $40 billion reference at the lower edge of rung 2. The Vera-specific reporting ties that outlook to the $20 billion FY2027 CPU baseline. Separate reports of potential to more than double describe the same outlook and leave the upside unsized. NVIDIA expects broad deployment, and rising hyperscaler spending supports demand. Anthropic is still evaluating Vera, while AMD's own benchmarks show a credible alternative for agentic workloads. Those benchmarks indicate competitive risk without quantifying lost sales. I expect most of the planned ramp to land, with a modest shortfall from deployment timing and customer choice. Realizing 90% of the reference puts my FY2028 forecast at $36 billion, within rung 3.\",\n131:      \"why\": null,\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/S/answers/claude.json\n390:      \"reasons\": \"Reference: outside tallies put the four companies' combined 2026 capital spending at roughly $720-745bn (Zacks, e5014; about $745bn tracking, e1856), inside rung 4 ($700-800bn). Build: the three companies with full-year guidance add to $540-570bn (F4): Alphabet $195-205bn after raising from $180-190bn (e1254), Meta $125-145bn after a raise (e1549), Amazon $220bn after raising from $200bn (e1649). Microsoft gives no calendar-year guidance in the evidence; its January-March 2026 quarter was $30.88bn, up 84% (e922), and a continued ramp of about 16% a quarter gives about $156bn, while the tally less the other three implies $150-205bn; I use $165bn (F10, my estimate). Total capital expenditure is therefore about $720bn (F11; $705-735bn across the guidance ranges). The question asks for data-center spending, which is narrower than total capex, so I apply a 98% data-center share (F14, my estimate): the companies indicated more than $730bn of AI-infrastructure spending (e1947), at or above the total tally; Meta's $130bn of AI capex (e3686) sits inside its $125-145bn guidance; Amazon's raise was mostly memory chips; outside estimates put spending on data centers near $750bn (e1416, e4082). The share is bounded at about 96-100% by these passages, giving data-center capex of about $706bn (F15), rung 4; the supported floor from e1947 (more than $730bn of AI infrastructure) also lies in rung 4. Rung 5 is not supported: the $795-800bn figures (NVIDIA, e2759; BofA, e5055) cover five companies, Oracle among them in Bo\n391:      \"why\": null,\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/S/answers/codex.json\n257:      \"reasons\": \"I forecast $720 billion of calendar 2026 data-center capex, in rung 4. The closest market reference, more than $730 billion of AI infrastructure spending for the same four companies, points to rung 4 and uses a broader spending label. Raised company outlooks, Microsoft's reported spending and strong cloud demand support $745 billion of total capex. After my $25 billion deduction for spending outside data centers, the forecast stays above $700 billion. Grid constraints and delayed projects put the lower boundary at risk, but the delay tally covers U.S. projects across developers and gives no amount attributable to these four companies' 2026 capex. NVIDIA's nearly $800 billion estimate covers five companies and serves as a cross-check. The AWS GPU expansion is scheduled for 2027–2028 and gives no amount to add to 2026.\",\n258:      \"why\": null,\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/P-v8/answers/claude.json\n314:      \"reasons\": \"Reference: the only outside forecast of this exact quantity is Morningstar's $361bn for FY2027 Data Center revenue (e2295, F13), which sits in rung 2 ($350bn to below $400bn); it predates the Q2 report and the Q3 guidance. Derivation: H1 is reported, $75.25bn in Q1 (e109) plus $89.0bn in Q2 (e2650), $164.25bn (F11; the $164bn in e5309 agrees). Q3 company revenue is guided at $108.0bn plus or minus 2% (e2652, e2720); NVIDIA has beaten the high end of guidance for 12 straight quarters (e5054, e5055) and beat the Q2 midpoint by $5.2bn (e3200), so I forecast Q3 at 4.5% above the midpoint (F4), $112.86bn (F5). For Q4 I forecast 12% sequential growth (F6), the pace the Q3 guide implies, held because management expects hyperscale growth to reaccelerate in Q4 as Vera Rubin supply builds (e3406, e5061) and supply commitments more than doubled to $279bn (e2759), and not raised because supply covers only about 70% of demand and stays constrained through FY2028 (e3733, e5067, e3115): $126.40bn (F7). Data Center has taken a rising share of revenue for five quarters, reaching 92.5% in Q2 (e3399, e5116); holding 92.5% for H2 (F9) gives H2 Data Center revenue of $221.32bn (F10) and FY2027 Data Center revenue of $385.57bn (F12), inside rung 2. The implied company total, $417.06bn (F15), sits close to the $411bn analyst consensus (F16, e4333), so the build is in line with where the market stands on the whole company. Boundaries: rung 1 would need Q4 revenue to jump far faster than any recent quarter, or a clearly higher \n315:      \"why\": null,\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/P-v8/answers/codex.json\n314:      \"reasons\": \"I forecast $385.6 billion in FY2027 Data Center revenue, within rung 2 ($350 billion to below $400 billion). Morningstar's $361 billion segment estimate is the direct outside reference and also sits in rung 2. NVIDIA reported $164.27 billion in the first half. I forecast $103.6 billion for Q3 from $112 billion of company revenue at a 92.5% Data Center share. The prior guidance beat and live Rubin deployment support a beat this quarter, with memory and deployment constraints limiting its size. For Q4, management's forecast of faster hyperscaler growth as Rubin supply expands supports 13% sequential company growth. A 93% Data Center share of $126.56 billion gives $117.7 billion for the segment. SemiAnalysis also points to second-half upside, but the passage gives no dollar baseline for its estimate. The stronger hyperscaler spending outlook supports near-term orders, with calendar 2027 spending bearing mainly on the end of this fiscal year. Limited H200 authorization adds a small amount of Q4 upside. The other export-policy themes leave current restrictions in place for this forecast. Long lead times and the timing of customer deployments keep the annual forecast below $400 billion.\",\n315:      \"why\": null,\n    24\t\n    25\tW = pg.W\n    26\tCASES = {   # registered in SMALL-DESIGN.md §5: archive, calls, member keys\n    27\t    'P': {'draw': 'old-1', 'calls': [('v4', 8810), ('v8', 8816)], 'arm': 'joint'},\n    28\t    'P-v4': {'draw': 'old-1', 'calls': [('v4', 8810), ('v8', 8816)], 'arm': 'separate', 'member': 'v4'},\n    29\t    'P-v8': {'draw': 'old-1', 'calls': [('v4', 8810), ('v8', 8816)], 'arm': 'separate', 'member': 'v8'},\n    30\t    'S': {'draw': 'old-3', 'calls': [('v6', 9043)], 'arm': 'single',\n    31\t          'what': W('four largest AI hyperscalers', 'data-center capex', 'combined', 'CY2026')},\n    32\t    'O': {'draw': 'old-2', 'calls': [('v29', 8507)], 'arm': 'single', 'what': W('NVIDIA', 'equity investment commitments', 'company', 'end of FY2027'),\n    33\t          'note': pg.O_NOTE},\n    34\t    'E': {'draw': 'old-3', 'calls': [('v41', 9071)], 'arm': 'single', 'what': W('NVIDIA', 'revenue', 'Vera CPU', 'FY2028')},\n    35\t}\n    36\tREGISTERED_QUESTIONS = {\n    37\t    ('old-1', 'v4'): \"What will NVIDIA's total revenue be in FY2027?\",\n    38\t    ('old-1', 'v8'): 'How much Data Center segment revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2027?',\n    39\t    ('old-3', 'v6'): 'How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026?',\n    40\t    ('old-2', 'v29'): \"How much will NVIDIA's equity investment commitments reach at the publication of its FY2027 annual report?\",\n    41\t    ('old-3', 'v41'): 'What will NVIDIA report as Vera CPU revenue for FY2028?',\n    42\t}\n    43\tREGISTERED_EDGES = {('old-1', 'v4'): [431.2, 392.0, 352.8, 313.6], ('old-1', 'v8'): [400.0, 350.0, 300.0, 250.0],\n    44\t                    ('old-3', 'v6'): [500.0, 600.0, 700.0, 800.0], ('old-2', 'v29'): [25.0, 50.0, 75.0, 100.0],\n    45\t                    ('old-3', 'v41'): [50.0, 40.0, 30.0, 20.0]}\n    46\t\n    47\t\n    48\tdef heading(draw, call_id):\n    49\t    a = sqlite3.connect(f'file:{pg.RUN / draw / \"prod/archive.sqlite\"}?mode=ro', uri=True)\n    50\t    user = json.loads(a.execute('select request_json from model_calls where call_id=?', (call_id,)).fetchone()[0])['messages'][1]['content']\n    51\t    m = re.match(r'Thesis (v\\d+) \\([^)]*\\): ([^\\n]*)', user)\n    52\t    return m.group(1), m.group(2)\n    53\t\n    54\t\n    55\tdef edges(bands):\n    56\t    out = []\n    57\t    for lo, hi in bands:\n    58\t        for e in (lo, hi):\n    59\t            if e is not None and e not in out:\n    60\t                out.append(e)\n    61\t    return out\n    62\t\n    63\t\n    64\tdef build_case(name, spec):\n    65\t    draw = spec['draw']\n    66\t    for view_id, call_id in spec['calls']:                     # the registered heading, question and ladder, or refuse\n    67\t        tid, question = heading(draw, call_id)\n    68\t        if tid != view_id or question != REGISTERED_QUESTIONS[(draw, view_id)]:\n    69\t            raise SystemExit(f'{name}: call {call_id} heads {tid} \"{question}\", not the registered {view_id}')\n    70\t        got = edges(pg.bands(draw, view_id))\n    71\t        if got != REGISTERED_EDGES[(draw, view_id)]:                   # [54] in rung order, not sorted\n    72\t            raise SystemExit(f'{name}: {view_id} ladder edges {got}, not the registered {REGISTERED_EDGES[(draw, view_id)]}')\n    73\t    if spec['arm'] in ('joint', 'separate'):\n    74\t        (tv, tc), (dv, dc) = spec['calls']\n    75\t        family = pg.small_family(pg.pair_family(draw, (tv, tc), (dv, dc)), pg.PAIR_SCOPES)\n    76\t        if spec['arm'] == 'joint':\n    77\t            return pg.item(f'blind-{name}', 'joint', draw, spec['calls'], family, [tv, dv], 'v13s')\n    78\t        return pg.item(f'blind-{name}', f'separate-{spec[\"member\"]}', draw, spec['calls'], pg.only(family, spec['member']), [spec['member']], 'v13s')\n    79\t    view_id, call_id = spec['calls'][0]\n    80\t    family = {'members': {view_id: {'what': spec['what'], 'bands': pg.bands(draw, view_id)}}, 'relations': [], 'shared': [], 'compare_periods': []}\n    81\t    family = pg.small_family(family, notes={view_id: spec['note']} if spec.get('note') else None)\n    82\t    return pg.item(f'blind-{name}', 'single', draw, spec['calls'], family, [view_id], 'v13s')\n    83\t\n    84\t\n    85\tdef build(out):\n    86\t    if out.exists():\n    87\t        raise SystemExit(f'{out} exists')\n    88\t    gate_quotes = {p['text'] for it in json.loads((HERE / 'paid-plan.json').read_text()) for p in it['passages'].values()}\n    89\t    report = []\n    90\t    for name, spec in CASES.items():\n    91\t        it = build_case(name, spec)\n    92\t        folder = out / name\n    93\t        (folder / 'answers').mkdir(parents=True)\n    94\t        (folder / 'system.txt').write_text(it['system'])\n    95\t        (folder / 'user.txt').write_text(it['user'])\n    96\t        (folder / 'schema.json').write_text(json.dumps(it['schema'], indent=1, ensure_ascii=False))\n    97\t        (folder / 'item.json').write_text(json.dumps(it, ensure_ascii=False))\n    98\t        fresh = sum(1 for p in it['passages'].values() if p['text'] not in gate_quotes)\n    99\t        report.append(f'{name:5} {it[\"arm\"]:13} members {it[\"members\"]} passages {len(it[\"passages\"])} ({fresh} quotes absent from the gate) '\n   100\t                      f'request {len(it[\"system\"]) + len(it[\"user\"]) + len(json.dumps(it[\"schema\"]))} chars')\n   101\t    (out / 'BUILD.txt').write_text('\\n'.join(report) + '\\n')\n   102\t    print('\\n'.join(report))\n   103\t\n   104\t\n   105\tWRITERS = ('claude', 'codex')\n   106\t_FENCE = re.compile(r'^\\s*```(?:json)?\\s*\\n(.*)\\n\\s*```\\s*$', re.S)\n   107\t\n   108\t\n   109\tdef first_json(text):\n   110\t    \"\"\"[54] The writer's first output exactly as returned: one JSON object, optionally inside a single ```json fence; anything else\n   111\t    (a preface, an array, two objects) is not an answer.\"\"\"\n   112\t    m = _FENCE.match(text)\n   113\t    body = m.group(1) if m else text\n   114\t    data = json.loads(body)\n   115\t    return data if isinstance(data, dict) else None\n   116\t\n   117\t\n   118\tdef inventory(out):\n   119\t    \"\"\"[54] Every registered case has one first answer from each writer.\"\"\"\n   120\t    return [f'{name}/{w}' for name in CASES for w in WRITERS if not (out / name / 'answers' / f'{w}.json').exists()]\n   121\t\n   122\t\n   123\tdef check(out):\n   124\t    missing = inventory(out)\n   125\t    if missing:\n    18\tsys.path.insert(0, str(HERE.parents[2] / 'src'))\n    19\timport calc_small as cs  # noqa: E402\n    20\tfrom blind_rules import DEFINITIONS, format_material  # noqa: E402\n    21\t\n    22\tREADER_BRIEF = \"\"\"# Blind reading (judging-v13s blind test, second re-read, D744)\n    23\t\n    24\tYou are given one forecasting request (the questions, their ladders, and every passage the writer was shown), the system text and the\n    25\tformat rules the writer was given, and one answer written in that format. You are not told who wrote the answer or whether any check\n    26\taccepted it. Read only what is given here.\n    27\t\n    28\tJudge in two steps:\n    29\t1. Check each source figure against its passage's quote and each computed figure against its inputs, using the format rules below.\n    30\t   What the format rules permit is not a defect.\n    31\t2. For each member, name the evidence for the assumption that puts the forecast in its band, and say whether the passages support it.\n    32\t\n    33\tReturn JSON:\n    34\t{\"members\": {\"<view id>\": {\"supported\": \"yes|no\", \"ground\": \"<the passage ids or the stated assumption that put the forecast in its band>\",\n    35\t                           \"why_not\": \"<if no: what is missing or wrong>\"}},\n    36\t \"defects\": [{\"type\": \"<one of: number misread | wrong arithmetic | part above or added into its whole | two forecasts of one quarter |\n    37\t               own estimate passed off as the market's reference | broken structure>\", \"figures\": [\"<figure ids>\"], \"detail\": \"...\"}]}\n    38\t\n    39\t\"\"\" + DEFINITIONS + \"\"\"\n    40\tReturn an empty \"defects\" list when you find none. Return only JSON.\n    41\t\"\"\"\n    42\t\n    43\t\n    44\tdef _fig(answer, fid):\n    45\t    return next(f for f in answer['figures'] if f['id'] == fid)\n    46\t\n    47\t\n    48\tdef _replace(text, old, new):\n    49\t    if text.count(old) != 1:\n    50\t        raise SystemExit(f'control text: \"{old[:60]}\" occurs {text.count(old)} times')\n    51\t    return text.replace(old, new)\n    52\t\n    53\t\n    54\tdef controls(out):\n    55\t    made = {}\n    56\t    p = json.loads((out / 'P' / 'answers' / 'claude.json').read_text())\n    57\t    guide = next(f for f in p['figures'] if f.get('quote_number') == '$108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%')\n    58\t    for name, value in (('K-midpoint', 108.0), ('K-misread', 108.5)):\n    59\t        a = copy.deepcopy(p)\n    60\t        _fig(a, guide['id']).update(value=value, low=None, high=None)\n    61\t        made[name] = ('P', a, guide['id'])\n    62\t    e = json.loads((out / 'E' / 'answers' / 'claude.json').read_text())\n    63\t    old_reason = 'I forecast 85% growth (F7) on the $20 billion base, giving $37 billion (F8): rung 3 (Neutral), just below the rung-2 line.'\n    64\t    for name, rung, reason in (\n    65\t            ('K-overlap', 3, 'I forecast 80% to 115% growth (F7) on the $20 billion base, giving $36 billion to $43 billion (F8). Its midpoint, '\n    66\t             'near $39.5 billion, and most of the range lie in rung 3 (Neutral), so I choose rung 3; the top of the range reaches rung 2.'),\n    67\t            ('K-disjoint', 4, 'I forecast 80% to 115% growth (F7) on the $20 billion base, giving $36 billion to $43 billion (F8): rung 4.')):\n    68\t        a = copy.deepcopy(e)\n    69\t        f7, f8 = _fig(a, 'F7'), _fig(a, 'F8')\n    70\t        f7.update(value=None, low=80.0, high=115.0, basis_text=_replace(f7['basis_text'], 'gives 85%.', 'gives 80% to 115%.'))\n    71\t        f8.update(value=None, low=36.0, high=43.0)\n    72\t        a['answers'][0].update(rung=rung, reasons=_replace(a['answers'][0]['reasons'], old_reason, reason))\n    73\t        made[name] = ('E', a, 'F8')\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'd=docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers; cat $d/judging-v13s{,-d745}.txt | wc -m; wc -m $d/judging-v13s{,-d745}.txt; nl -ba poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/user.txt | head -90; tail -4 poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/{S,P-v8}/user.txt | cut -c1-500; rg -n '\"'def item|judging-v13s|read_text\\\\(' \"'$d/{paid_gate,calc_small}.py | head -40; rg -n '\"'growth|prompt tokens|Check before' AGENTS.md; cat poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/{reread-reasons.json,reread-adjudication/W07.codex.json,reread-adjudication/W07.claude.json} | head -80; python3 -B -c 'import importlib.util as u; print(u.find_spec(\\\"tiktoken\\\"))'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'd=docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers; cat $d/judging-v13s{,-d745}.txt | wc -m; wc -m $d/judging-v13s{,-d745}.txt; nl -ba poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/user.txt | head -90; tail -4 poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/{S,P-v8}/user.txt | cut -c1-500; rg -n '\"'def item|judging-v13s|read_text\\\\(' \"'$d/{paid_gate,calc_small}.py | head -40; rg -n '\"'growth|prompt tokens|Check before' AGENTS.md; cat poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/{reread-reasons.json,reread-adjudication/W07.codex.json,reread-adjudication/W07.claude.json} | head -80; python3 -B -c 'import importlib.util as u; print(u.find_spec(\\\"tiktoken\\\"))'\"","aggregated_output":"    9356\n    4816 docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/judging-v13s.txt\n    4540 docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/judging-v13s-d745.txt\n    9356 total\n     1\t[Family] members:\n     2\tThesis v41 (NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA Vera CPU revenue\", FY2028, due 2028-02-29): What will NVIDIA report as Vera CPU revenue for FY2028?\n     3\tLadder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: FY2028 Vera CPU revenue is at least $50 billion. / rung 2 Bullish: FY2028 Vera CPU revenue is at least $40 billion and less than $50 billion. / rung 3 Neutral: FY2028 Vera CPU revenue is at least $30 billion and less than $40 billion. / rung 4 Bearish: FY2028 Vera CPU revenue is at least $20 billion and less than $30 billion. / rung 5 Strongly bearish: FY2028 Vera CPU revenue is less than $20 billion.\n     4\t\n     5\tRelations: none.\n     6\tShared quantities, one forecast figure each; on every arithmetic path from an outcome, through rates and multiples too, the first figure of that quantity reached is its forecast: none.\n     7\tOutcome keys (population | measure | scope | period), in USD bn, to be used exactly: v41 = NVIDIA | revenue | Vera CPU | FY2028.\n     8\t\n     9\t[Evidence in view for the family, 15 items]\n    10\t- e3660 [party_statement; Bram Berkowitz, fool.com, reporting Colette Kress's statement; original source NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress ; from gnews:fd25a8ea6f60290264bfbe55 2026-08-30] NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress said NVIDIA still expects $20 billion of CPU revenue in FY2027 and expects FY2028 CPU revenue to more than double that amount.\n    11\t    Quote: The bad news for AMD and Intel is that Kress said Nvidia not only still expects $20 billion in CPU revenue in its fiscal 2027, but expects that number to more than double in fiscal 2028.\n    12\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e3302, e4691, e4856; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    13\t- e4687 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source AMD ; from gnews:3c5c71e12ed2a7001ce14f1a 2026-09-10] AMD estimates that server CPU market revenue could grow at a 50% annual rate through 2030, reaching a $220 billion opportunity by the end of the decade.\n    14\t    Quote: AMD notes that the server CPU market's revenue could increase at an annual rate of 50% through 2030, creating a $220 billion revenue opportunity by the end of the decade.\n    15\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4084; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    16\t- e4689 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst), quoting Colette Kress; original source NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress ; from gnews:3c5c71e12ed2a7001ce14f1a 2026-09-10] NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress said Vera CPU deployments would include every major hyperscaler, neocloud, AI lab, and system OEM.\n    17\t    Quote: Kress added that the Vera CPU will be deployed by \"every major hyperscaler, neocloud, AI lab, and system OEM.\"\n    18\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2769; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    19\t- e4691 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:3c5c71e12ed2a7001ce14f1a 2026-09-10] NVIDIA expects Vera CPU revenue in FY2028 to be double its FY2027 level.\n    20\t    Quote: What's more, Nvidia expects its Vera CPU revenue to double next year.\n    21\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e3660, e4764; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    22\t- e4692 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst), citing Tom's Hardware; original source Tom's Hardware ; from gnews:3c5c71e12ed2a7001ce14f1a 2026-09-10] Tom's Hardware reported that Arm-based servers represented 45% of data-center market revenue share in June.\n    23\t    Quote: Tom's Hardware noted in June that Arm-based servers account for 45% of the data center market's revenue share.\n    24\t- e4764 [party_statement; fool.com (Keith Noonan); original source Jensen Huang ; from gnews:0ba93692dc5df4b999fe3729 2026-09-11] Jensen Huang forecast that NVIDIA CPU revenue would more than double in FY2028.\n    25\t    Quote: Huang expects CPU revenue to more than double next fiscal year\n    26\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4691; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    27\t- e4856 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:c70bc12967b9b53426b5fb10 2026-09-12] NVIDIA management said Vera CPU revenue in FY2028 could be more than double the $20 billion potential it cited for FY2027.\n    28\t    Quote: with the potential to more than double that number in fiscal year 2028.\n    29\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e3660; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    30\t- e4857 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source Bank of America analysts ; from gnews:c70bc12967b9b53426b5fb10 2026-09-12] Bank of America analysts forecast that the CPU market would grow fivefold, from $35 billion in 2026 to $170 billion in 2030.\n    31\t    Quote: Analysts at Bank of America also released a research report in June, saying they believe the CPU market will grow fivefold between this year and 2030, from $35 billion to $170 billion.\n    32\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e935; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    33\t- e5044 [party_statement; Insider Monkey; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:a1af1bb8840fa4ec9e52bcb0 2026-09-15] NVIDIA said Anthropic is evaluating NVIDIA’s Vera CPU for CPU-intensive agentic workloads.\n    34\t    Quote: Nvidia recently said Anthropic is evaluating its Vera CPU for CPU-intensive agentic workloads,\n    35\t- e5399 [party_statement; wccftech.com; original source AMD ; from gnews:83ffa482f76b03da59ac2674 2026-09-19] AMD reported that its 6th Gen EPYC Venice CPUs delivered 20% higher per-core performance than NVIDIA’s 88-core Vera CPU in SPEC 2026.\n    36\t    Quote: Here, the AMD 6th Gen EPYC Venice CPUs provide a 20% uplift over NVIDIA's 88-Core Vera CPU.\n    37\t- e5400 [party_statement; wccftech.com; original source AMD ; from gnews:83ffa482f76b03da59ac2674 2026-09-19] AMD reported that its EPYC 9996 delivered 1.2 times Vera-based platform per-core performance and 2.24 times its platform-level performance in SPECrate 2026 Integer testing.\n    38\t    Quote: In SPECrate 2026 Integer testing, the AMD EPYC 9996 server CPU delivers 1.2 times the per-core performance of an Nvidia Vera-based platform and 2.24 times its platform-level performance.\n    39\t- e5401 [party_statement; wccftech.com; original source AMD ; from gnews:83ffa482f76b03da59ac2674 2026-09-19] AMD estimated that an EPYC 9996 system in a modeled 100-kilowatt rack would deliver 3.4 times the throughput of a Vera-based platform.\n    40\t    Quote: In a modeled 100-kilowatt rack, it delivers an estimated 3.4 times the throughput of a Vera-based platform.\n    41\t- e5402 [party_statement; wccftech.com; original source AMD ; from gnews:83ffa482f76b03da59ac2674 2026-09-19] AMD reported that its 5th Gen EPYC CPUs with 192 cores had a 37% performance gain over NVIDIA Vera, while its 6th Gen EPYC Venice CPU with 256 cores had a 2.37-times gain in the overall SPEC 2026 suite.\n    42\t    Quote: Here, the 5th Gen EPYC CPUs with 192 cores provide a 37% gain over NVIDIA Vera & the 6th Gen EPYC Venice CPU with 256 cores provides a 2.37x gain.\n    43\t- e5403 [party_statement; wccftech.com; original source AMD ; from gnews:83ffa482f76b03da59ac2674 2026-09-19] AMD reported that its EPYC 9006 server CPUs averaged 12% higher per-core performance in Agentic AI-specific benchmarks, with a4 subtests averaging 20%.\n    44\t    Quote: Moving over to Agentic AI-specific benchmark tests, AMD's EPYC 9006 Server CPUs drive a 12% gain in per-core performance benchmarks, with the a4 subtests' performance averaging at 20%.\n    45\t- e5404 [party_statement; wccftech.com; original source AMD ; from gnews:83ffa482f76b03da59ac2674 2026-09-19] AMD reported that EPYC Venice had 18% faster memory bandwidth than NVIDIA Vera, with 1.6 TB/s versus Vera’s 1.2 TB/s, in STREAM Triad testing.\n    46\t    Quote: AMD's EPYC Venice delivered 18% faster memory bandwidth versus NVIDIA's Vera thanks to its 16-channel memory design, which supports up to 12,800 MT/s RDIMM modules. This provides the platform with 1.6 TB/s of memory bandwidth versus 1.2 TB/s bandwidth on the NVIDIA Vera CPUs (using LPDDR5x).\n    47\t\n    48\t[Themes in force, 10]\n    49\t- v10 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will Chinese authorities formally authorize purchases of Nvidia H200 chips by Chinese AI companies by January 31, 2027?: now rung 1 Yes; judged 2026-09-17; reasons: The reference is rung 2 (No): no market consensus, company guidance, or stated deadline for Chinese authorization is supplied, so the status-quo reference is no authorization by January 31, 2027. The evidence now makes rung 1 more likely: reports describe Chinese approval of H200 purchases for selec\n    50\t- v18 (chip-export-controls, due 2026-12-31) Will the Remote Access Security Act be enacted by December 31, 2026?: now rung 2 No; judged 2026-08-19; reasons: The reference is the latest supplied legislative status: the Act passed the House but had not passed the Senate as of August 19, 2026, placing the reference at rung 2 (No); no market-consensus estimate is supplied. With about six weeks remaining at the due date, the implied outcome remains non-enact\n    51\t- v25 (chip-export-controls, due 2026-12-31) Will Taiwan adopt restrictions on AI chip sales to China by December 31, 2026?: now rung 1 No; judged 2026-08-25; reasons: The reference is Taiwan's stated current policy status: no Taiwan-specific restrictions on AI chip sales to China, which is rung 1; no separate market consensus is supplied. The investigation and reports that officials are considering a criminal ban, followed by indictments and seizure of additional\n    52\t- v26 (chip-export-controls, due 2026-12-31) Will Taiwan adopt performance-threshold controls on exports of advanced AI chips by December 31, 2026?: now rung 2 No; judged 2026-07-28; reasons: No market consensus or formal target is supplied; the reference is the status-quo baseline of no adopted controls, rung 2. Consultations and active consideration make adoption more plausible than before, but the Ministry confirmed that no timeline is set. With the deadline only five months away, the\n    53\t- v32 (chip-export-controls, due 2026-12-31) Will the Commerce Department announce or adopt restrictions on Chinese AI developers' access to advanced chips through overseas data centers by December 31, 2026?: now rung 1 No; judged 2026-08-19; reasons: The reference is the status quo—no stated market consensus or official timetable—and sits at rung 1. The active review and the reported use of overseas data centers make restrictions more plausible, but the evidence still describes examination and alleged access, not a proposed rule or a dated commi\n    54\t- v40 (ai-capex, due 2026-10-05) Will the relevant regulator approve CME Group’s planned futures contracts tied to H100 and B200 hourly rental costs by October 5, 2026?: now rung 2 No; judged 2026-08-29; reasons: The stated plan points to rung 1: CME intends to launch on October 5, conditional on approval. But the evidence in force makes approval by that date less likely than not: the CFTC is still seeking comment on whether the market is sufficiently standardized and transparent, and that comment period run\n    55\t- v49 (semiconductor-cycle, due 2027-02-28) How much will global semiconductor sales total in calendar 2026?: now rung 4 High; judged 2026-09-16; reasons: The market reference is the WSTS and Gartner forecasts, both of which place 2026 sales in rung 4. Their quantified forecasts imply $1.51–$1.56 trillion, within rung 4's $1.50 trillion to below $1.60 trillion range; neither crosses a rung boundary. IDC's 52.8% growth forecast lacks a sales base, so i\n    56\t- v6 (ai-capex, due 2027-02-28) How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026?: now rung 4 Upper-middle range; judged 2026-09-19; reasons: The market reference is roughly $720–$745 billion for the four hyperscalers, which sits in rung 4 (e5014). The evidence still implies about $750 billion: the four-company tracking estimate near $745 billion and the separate top-four estimate of $760 billion bracket that level; company capex revision\n    57\t- v7 (ai-capex, due 2028-02-28) How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2027?: now rung 4 Upper-middle range; judged 2026-09-19; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The market reference is about $1.08 trillion for 2027 hyperscaler capex, which is rung 3. My implied estimate is just above the $1.1 trillion rung-4 boundary: NVIDIA's $1.3 trillion top-five forecast supports a higher spending path, while the four-company, data-center-only scope requires a discount;\n    58\t- v9 (chip-export-controls, due 2026-12-31) Will Congress enact legislation phasing out Chinese-made lidar technology in the United States by December 31, 2026?: now rung 2 No; judged 2026-07-07; reasons: The reference is No (rung 2): no market consensus or enacted legislation is supplied, and the stated deadline remains the test. The House committee’s proposal is a concrete step toward enactment, but it is not enactment by Congress; with only this evidence, it raises the chance of Yes without making\n    59\t\n    60\tAnswer per the JSON schema: one calculation for the family and one answer per member.==> poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/S/user.txt <==\n\n\n\nAnswer per the JSON schema: one calculation for the family and one answer per member.\n==> poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/P-v8/user.txt <==\n- v7 (ai-capex, due 2028-02-29) How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data center capital expenditures in calendar 2027?: now rung 4 $1.1 trillion to below $1.2 trillion; judged 2026-09-18; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The four-company market reference is about $930–$980 billion (rung 3): BofA's $1.08 trillion 2027 estimate covers five hyperscalers, and the carried estimate for Oracle is roughly $100–$150 billion. The higher NVIDIA forecast of $1.3 trilli\n- v9 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will China authorize purchases of Nvidia H200 chips by AI companies by January 31, 2027?: now rung 2 Yes; judged 2026-09-01; reasons: The reference is rung 1: the status quo of no Chinese authorization by the deadline, consistent with NVIDIA's zero-China-revenue Q3 outlook, though that guidance is not itself evidence that China will not authorize purchases. The evidence still makes limited authorization more likely than none: e244\n\nAnswer per the JSON schema: one calculation for the family and one answer per member.docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_small.py:1:\"\"\"judging-v13s checks: the small format (SMALL-DESIGN.md §2 [52] and [54], D737, D738). Harness-only. Code refuses an answer only for\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid_gate.py:34:SYSTEM = (HERE / 'judging-v13-draft.txt').read_text()\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid_gate.py:35:SYSTEM_S = (HERE / 'judging-v13s.txt').read_text()                      # the small format (D737): one sentence changed\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid_gate.py:38:PINNED_PLAN_SHA256 = (HERE / 'paid-plan.sha256').read_text().strip() if (HERE / 'paid-plan.sha256').exists() else None\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid_gate.py:126:def item(unit, arm, draw, calls, family, shown, fmt='v13'):\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid_gate.py:259:        text = path.read_text(encoding='utf-8', errors='replace') if path.exists() else ''\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid_gate.py:413:    items = json.loads((out / 'plan.json').read_text())\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid_gate.py:414:    ledger = json.loads((out / 'ledger.json').read_text()) if (out / 'ledger.json').exists() else None\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid_gate.py:433:    text = Path(plan_path).read_text()\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid_gate.py:598:    results = json.loads((out / 'results.json').read_text())\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid_gate.py:599:    items = {(i['unit'], i['arm']): i for i in json.loads((out / 'plan.json').read_text())}\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid_gate.py:632:    results = json.loads((out / 'results.json').read_text())\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid_gate.py:633:    items = {(i['unit'], i['arm']): i for i in json.loads((out / 'plan.json').read_text())}\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid_gate.py:655:    results = json.loads((out / 'results.json').read_text())\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid_gate.py:656:    items = {(i['unit'], i['arm']): i for i in json.loads((out / 'plan.json').read_text())}\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid_gate.py:718:    frozen = json.loads((key_dir / 'packet-key.json').read_text())\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid_gate.py:729:    record = json.loads((out / 'verdict.json').read_text())\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid_gate.py:732:            and record['key_commitment'] == key_commitment(json.loads((key_dir / 'packet-key.json').read_text())))\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid_gate.py:741:    frozen = json.loads((key_dir / 'packet-key.json').read_text())\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid_gate.py:742:    submitted = json.loads((out / 'readings.json').read_text())\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid_gate.py:749:    written = packets_path.read_text() if packets_path.exists() else ''\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid_gate.py:757:    results = json.loads((out / 'results.json').read_text())\n223:  and remove at least as much elsewhere. Report characters and prompt tokens before and after; growth needs a written reason.\n229:  items\"). An abstract clause (\"a material statement\") is a coin flip per send. The examples are not growth to avoid; the abstract clause\n231:- **Check before the send, at no cost.** Write one answer that follows the prompt and run every code check on it: a request never asks\n{\n \"W01\": {\"first\": \"compatible\", \"note\": \"Both: the reason names no gap; the passages place the outcome in rung 2 (Codex: no reason another band is as likely; Claude: the answer's own calculation and outside figures give rung 2).\"},\n \"W02\": {\"first\": \"compatible\", \"note\": \"Both reject on the band-deciding assumption left unexplained: Codex, nothing explains why non-Vera CPU sales stay at or under $6B at the 90% share; Claude, no size for the shortfall below 2x and an unsupported 90% share, which at the 1.5x anchor gives $27B. Neither rests a yes on the whole range staying at $30B or more.\"},\n \"W04\": {\"first\": \"compatible\", \"note\": \"Both: the 10% shortfall has no size or explained comparison; the cited passages show risk, not why revenue lands below $40B but at or above $30B.\"},\n \"W05\": {\"first\": \"compatible\", \"note\": \"Both: the gap holds; e1947 does not separate data-center spending from the four companies' total (Codex: no data-center breakdown; Claude: the total under another label), e5376 covers five companies, and the share needed (~94%) is not placed by the passages (Claude: Meta's 90-104% straddles it).\"},\n \"W08\": {\"first\": \"compatible\", \"note\": \"Both apply Alex's rule: e1947 (same four companies, same year, AI infrastructure close to total) and Meta's AI share explain why a deduction under $45B is more likely; Codex notes they do not measure it directly, which the rule allows.\"},\n \"W09\": {\"first\": \"compatible\", \"note\": \"Both: the 15% cut has no size or explained comparison; the passages explain why sales could fall short, not why the shortfall is small enough to stay at or above $30B.\"}\n}\n{\n  \"members\": {\n    \"v6\": {\n      \"supported\": \"no\",\n      \"why\": \"With $720 billion of total capex, rung 4 requires the four companies to spend at most $20 billion on everything outside data centers. e1947 covers the same four companies and year, but its “more than $730 billion” measures AI infrastructure. The answer does not explain how much qualifies as data-center capex. e5014 says only “a large portion” goes to AI infrastructure. Meta’s $130 billion covers one company, Amazon’s memory costs explain its increase, and the $750 billion estimates do not isolate these four. The answer’s own 96% share gives $691.2 billion. It gives no grounded reason to favor 98% over 96% at the $700 billion boundary.\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"defects\": [\n    {\n      \"reader\": \"Reader 1\",\n      \"type\": \"part above or added into its whole\",\n      \"figures\": [\"F12\", \"F11\", \"F15\"],\n      \"decision\": \"uphold\",\n      \"why\": \"The answer calls F12 “the supported floor from e1947” for the data-center forecast. Using more than $730 billion as that floor puts the data-center portion above F11’s $720 billion total and puts F15’s $705.6 billion outcome below its own floor. The governing rule says “a part cannot exceed its whole.”\"\n    }\n  ]\n}{\"members\": {\"v6\": {\"supported\": \"yes\", \"why\": \"The band is decided by the 98% data-center share (F14) applied to the $720bn total (F11). At 98%, rung 4 needs a total of at least about $714bn. At $720bn, it needs a share of at least about 97.2%. The share rests on a related figure that the answer explains, which the definition allows. e1947 names exactly these four companies ('Amazon, Meta Platforms, Microsoft, and Alphabet') and this period ('this year'), with 'more than $730 billion combined on AI infrastructure'. The answer explains why this figure applies to the quantity asked. It treats this spending as data-center spending ('data-center (AI infrastructure) spending'). It notes that the figure is at or above the four-company capex tally (e5014 'roughly $720 billion to $745 billion'), so nearly all capex is data-center spending. It keeps a 2% residual because 'No passage sizes non-data-center spending'. The answer also says why the upper side is more likely: the repeated guidance raises (e1254, e1549, e1649), and the four-company tallies of $720-760bn (e5014, e1856, e2458) all exceed the roughly $714bn total that 98% needs. The e1947 figure itself lies in rung 4. The answer names the rung 3 risk: Microsoft or the share could come in lower, and the 96% end of its bound gives $691bn. Under the definition, 'an explained related figure satisfies this requirement even though the other side of the line remains possible'. Rung 5 is excluded on the passages, because the ~$800bn figures cover five companies (e2759 'top 5 hyperscalers'; e5055 includes 'Oracle').\"}}, \"defects\": [{\"reader\": \"Reader 1\", \"type\": \"part above or added into its whole\", \"figures\": [\"F12\", \"F11\", \"F15\"], \"decision\": \"uphold\", \"why\": \"The answer itself treats F12 as the segment. F14 defines the share as 'the share of the four companies' 2026 capital expenditure that is data-center (AI infrastructure) spending', so it makes AI-infrastructure spending part of total capex. The reasons then call e1947's 'more than $730 billion' (F12) 'the supported floor' of the outcome. The family therefore holds a figure for the part above $730bn, while its forecast of the whole, total capex F11, is $720bn and its outcome F15 is $705.6bn. The system text says 'a part cannot exceed its whole', and the definition covers 'a segment is larger than the total it belongs to'. F14's basis concedes the point: the AI-infrastructure total 'is at or above the total-capex tally'. This inconsistency does not change the rung: the figure that exceeds the total is itself in rung 4.\"}]}\nModuleSpec(name='tiktoken', loader=<_frozen_importlib_external.SourceFileLoader object at 0x104ea0970>, origin='/Users/lchingen/.pyenv/versions/3.10.10/lib/python3.10/site-packages/tiktoken/__init__.py', submodule_search_locations=['/Users/lchingen/.pyenv/versions/3.10.10/lib/python3.10/site-packages/tiktoken'])\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_18","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'd=docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers; nl -ba $d/paid_gate.py | sed -n '\"'28,42p;119,153p'; nl -ba \"'$d/SMALL-DESIGN.md | sed -n '\"'360,378p'; nl -ba poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/reader-key.json | head -49; nl -ba poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/E/answers/claude.json | rg '85%|85\\\\.' | cut -c1-850; rg -n 'o200k|prompt tokens|tokenizer' \"'$d -g '\"'*.py' | head -20; python3 -B -c \\\"import os,tempfile,hashlib; from pathlib import Path; name=hashlib.sha1(b'https://openaipublic.blob.core.windows.net/encodings/o200k_base.tiktoken').hexdigest(); print([(str(p),p.exists()) for p in [Path(os.getenv('TIKTOKEN_CACHE_DIR',tempfile.gettempdir()+'/data-gym-cache'))/name,Path('/tmp/data-gym-cache')/name]])\\\"\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_18","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'd=docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers; nl -ba $d/paid_gate.py | sed -n '\"'28,42p;119,153p'; nl -ba \"'$d/SMALL-DESIGN.md | sed -n '\"'360,378p'; nl -ba poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/reader-key.json | head -49; nl -ba poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/E/answers/claude.json | rg '85%|85\\\\.' | cut -c1-850; rg -n 'o200k|prompt tokens|tokenizer' \"'$d -g '\"'*.py' | head -20; python3 -B -c \\\"import os,tempfile,hashlib; from pathlib import Path; name=hashlib.sha1(b'https://openaipublic.blob.core.windows.net/encodings/o200k_base.tiktoken').hexdigest(); print([(str(p),p.exists()) for p in [Path(os.getenv('TIKTOKEN_CACHE_DIR',tempfile.gettempdir()+'/data-gym-cache'))/name,Path('/tmp/data-gym-cache')/name]])\\\"\"","aggregated_output":"    28\timport calc_small as cs                                                 # noqa: E402\n    29\timport render_v13 as rv                                                 # noqa: E402\n    30\tfrom hivemind import config, model                                      # noqa: E402\n    31\t\n    32\tRUN = REPO / 'poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run'\n    33\tCONFIG = REPO / 'poc/config/poc-v118.json'                              # its re-judging model setting: gpt-6-luna, medium, 16000 output tokens\n    34\tSYSTEM = (HERE / 'judging-v13-draft.txt').read_text()\n    35\tSYSTEM_S = (HERE / 'judging-v13s.txt').read_text()                      # the small format (D737): one sentence changed\n    36\tCHECKERS = {None: cc, 'v13': cc, 'v13s': cs}\n    37\tAPPROVED_CEILING = 0.5                                                  # Alex, 2026-11-22 (D732): \"0.5 美元 (推荐)\"\n    38\tPINNED_PLAN_SHA256 = (HERE / 'paid-plan.sha256').read_text().strip() if (HERE / 'paid-plan.sha256').exists() else None\n    39\tAUTH = Path.home() / '.hivemind-keys/v13-paid-gate/authorization.json'\n    40\tDRAWS = (1, 2, 3)\n    41\tRETRY_MARK = '\\n\\nThe code refused the previous answer because: '\n    42\tPAIRS = [('old-1', ('v4', 9014), ('v8', 9022)), ('old-2', ('v14', 8729), ('v9', 8737)), ('old-3', ('v3', 9183), ('v35', 9177))]\n   119\t    out = {}\n   120\t    for chunk in re.split(r'\\n(?=- e\\d+ )', m.group(1) if m else ''):\n   121\t        if chunk.startswith('- e'):\n   122\t            out[chunk[2:].split(' ', 1)[0]] = chunk\n   123\t    return out\n   124\t\n   125\t\n   126\tdef item(unit, arm, draw, calls, family, shown, fmt='v13'):\n   127\t    archive = RUN / draw / 'prod/archive.sqlite'\n   128\t    if fmt == 'v13s':\n   129\t        user, evidence_ids, theme_ids = rv.render(archive, calls, family, shown=shown, fmt='v13s')\n   130\t        return {'unit': unit, 'arm': arm, 'draw_store': draw, 'members': sorted(shown), 'format': 'v13s', 'system': SYSTEM_S, 'user': user,\n   131\t                'schema': rv.v13s_schema(sorted(shown), evidence_ids, theme_ids), 'family': family, 'passages': passages(draw, evidence_ids),\n   132\t                'evidence_ids': evidence_ids, 'questions': questions(draw, sorted(shown)), 'shown': shown_lines(user)}\n   133\t    user, evidence_ids, theme_ids = rv.render(archive, calls, family, shown=shown)\n   134\t    return {'unit': unit, 'arm': arm, 'draw_store': draw, 'members': sorted(shown), 'system': SYSTEM, 'user': user,\n   135\t            'schema': rv.v13_schema(sorted(shown), evidence_ids, theme_ids), 'family': family, 'passages': passages(draw, evidence_ids),\n   136\t            'evidence_ids': evidence_ids, 'questions': questions(draw, sorted(shown)), 'shown': shown_lines(user)}\n   137\t\n   138\t\n   139\tdef build_plan(fmt='v13'):\n   140\t    items = []\n   141\t    small = fmt == 'v13s'\n   142\t    for draw, total, dc in PAIRS:\n   143\t        family = pair_family(draw, total, dc)\n   144\t        if small:\n   145\t            family = small_family(family, PAIR_SCOPES)\n   146\t        calls = [total, dc]\n   147\t        items.append(item(f'pair-{draw}', 'joint', draw, calls, family, [total[0], dc[0]], fmt))\n   148\t        for member in (total, dc):                            # the same union of evidence, one member's header and answer\n   149\t            items.append(item(f'pair-{draw}', f'separate-{member[0]}', draw, calls, only(family, member[0]), [member[0]], fmt))\n   150\t    for name, draw, view_id, call_id, what in SINGLETONS:\n   151\t        family = {'members': {view_id: {'what': W(*what), 'bands': bands(draw, view_id)}}, 'relations': [], 'shared': [], 'compare_periods': []}\n   152\t        if small:\n   153\t            family = small_family(family, notes={view_id: O_NOTE} if name == 'O' else None)\n   360\t  - for a forecast, the evidence for the band-deciding assumption;\n   361\t  - for an unresolved answer, the gap left after the strongest passages.\n   362\t  Adjudicators get the same definitions.\n   363\t- **Scope** (`blind4.py`; run folder `poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/`):\n   364\t  - cases S, E and P-v8;\n   365\t  - 6 unedited first replies (a fresh Claude subagent and a fresh read-only Codex session per case);\n   366\t  - 9 blinded packets (6 replies + 3 controls), each read by one Claude and one Codex reader: 18 first readings, plus adjudication\n   367\t    where they disagree.\n   368\t- **Controls**, each checked by the code before use:\n   369\t\n   370\t  | Control | Built from | Expected reading |\n   371\t  |---|---|---|\n   372\t  | K-unsupported | the saved third-run E Codex reply, with an unsized 15% cut | not supported |\n   373\t  | K-unresolved-unjustified | P-v8 Claude made unresolved with a vague reason | not supported |\n   374\t  | K-unresolved-justified | S made unresolved with the specific gap | supported |\n   375\t- **Pass:**\n   376\t  - every S and E reply has a settled \"yes\" on its forecast or on its reason for leaving the answer unresolved;\n   377\t  - both P-v8 replies have a settled \"yes\" on a forecast;\n   378\t  - all three controls read as expected;\n     1\t{\n     2\t \"U01\": {\n     3\t  \"case\": \"P-v8\",\n     4\t  \"writer\": \"K-unresolved-unjustified\",\n     5\t  \"answer\": \"controls/K-unresolved-unjustified.json\"\n     6\t },\n     7\t \"U02\": {\n     8\t  \"case\": \"S\",\n     9\t  \"writer\": \"claude\",\n    10\t  \"answer\": \"S/answers/claude.json\"\n    11\t },\n    12\t \"U03\": {\n    13\t  \"case\": \"E\",\n    14\t  \"writer\": \"K-unsupported\",\n    15\t  \"answer\": \"controls/K-unsupported.json\"\n    16\t },\n    17\t \"U04\": {\n    18\t  \"case\": \"S\",\n    19\t  \"writer\": \"K-unresolved-justified\",\n    20\t  \"answer\": \"controls/K-unresolved-justified.json\"\n    21\t },\n    22\t \"U05\": {\n    23\t  \"case\": \"E\",\n    24\t  \"writer\": \"codex\",\n    25\t  \"answer\": \"E/answers/codex.json\"\n    26\t },\n    27\t \"U06\": {\n    28\t  \"case\": \"E\",\n    29\t  \"writer\": \"claude\",\n    30\t  \"answer\": \"E/answers/claude.json\"\n    31\t },\n    32\t \"U07\": {\n    33\t  \"case\": \"P-v8\",\n    34\t  \"writer\": \"codex\",\n    35\t  \"answer\": \"P-v8/answers/codex.json\"\n    36\t },\n    37\t \"U08\": {\n    38\t  \"case\": \"S\",\n    39\t  \"writer\": \"codex\",\n    40\t  \"answer\": \"S/answers/codex.json\"\n    41\t },\n    42\t \"U09\": {\n    43\t  \"case\": \"P-v8\",\n    44\t  \"writer\": \"claude\",\n    45\t  \"answer\": \"P-v8/answers/claude.json\"\n    46\t }\n    47\t}   131\t      \"basis_text\": \"My estimate of FY2028 Vera CPU revenue growth on the FY2027 base. Management's 'more than double' (F2, at least 100% growth) is the benchmark, but it is a target for a fiscal year that has not started, for a product line still ramping, so it is discounted for time and execution rather than taken in full. Outside views put CPU market growth near 50% a year (AMD, F4; Bank of America's fivefold path from F5 to F6), so a doubling needs large share gains. Broad announced adoption (every major hyperscaler, neocloud, AI lab and OEM, e4689; Anthropic evaluating Vera, e5044; Arm servers' large revenue share, e4692) supports a pace well above the market, while AMD's own benchmark claims against Vera (e5399, e5400, e5401, e5402, e5404) show standalone CPU share will be contested. Weighting the company target most heavily,\n   162\t      \"reasons\": \"Reference: NVIDIA's own guidance. CFO Colette Kress reaffirmed about $20 billion of CPU revenue for FY2027 (F1, e3660) and said FY2028 would more than double it (F2, e4856); NVIDIA's wording ties both the base and the doubling to Vera CPU revenue (e4691, e4856). That gives more than $40 billion (F3), the bottom edge of rung 2 (Bullish, $40-50 billion). Forecast: this is a target for a full fiscal year that has not yet started, for a product line still ramping, so it is discounted for time and execution rather than taken in full; the whole of FY2028 is still to be sold. Outside benchmarks put CPU market growth near 50% a year (AMD's server CPU estimate, F4; Bank of America's path from $35 billion to $170 billion, F5 and F6), roughly half the pace NVIDIA needs, so a doubling requires large share gains. Adoption brea\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File \"<string>\", line 1, in <module>\n  File \"/Users/lchingen/.pyenv/versions/3.10.10/lib/python3.10/tempfile.py\", line 299, in gettempdir\n    return _os.fsdecode(_gettempdir())\n  File \"/Users/lchingen/.pyenv/versions/3.10.10/lib/python3.10/tempfile.py\", line 292, in _gettempdir\n    tempdir = _get_default_tempdir()\n  File \"/Users/lchingen/.pyenv/versions/3.10.10/lib/python3.10/tempfile.py\", line 223, in _get_default_tempdir\n    raise FileNotFoundError(_errno.ENOENT,\nFileNotFoundError: [Errno 2] No usable temporary directory found in ['/var/folders/lm/gw2hxzg52rd1brqm_bnlv6b00000gn/T/', '/tmp', '/var/tmp', '/usr/tmp', '/Users/lchingen/hivemind']\n","exit_code":1,"status":"failed"}}
